window.LoomManual = { chapters: [
{"id":"getting-started","file":"01-getting-started.md","title":"Getting started","html":"<h1 id=\"lm-getting-started\">Getting started</h1>\n<p>LoomWright is a free program for drawing wiring diagrams, written first for vehicle looms. You draw the circuit with real components, give each wire its colour, size, length and circuit, and LoomWright turns that into the lists you need at the bench: a wire list, cut lists, connector tables, a fuse schedule, labels and a printable build pack.</p>\n<p>It works offline. Your projects are ordinary files on your computer, saved with the extension <code>.loom</code>.</p>\n<blockquote class=\"lm-note\"><p>LoomWright is a planning and drawing tool. Its checks and hints are guidance only, and it does not replace a qualified electrician or engineer. Read the <a href=\"#lm-disclaimer\" data-chapter=\"disclaimer\" data-anchor=\"\">Disclaimer</a> before you build from your drawings.</p></blockquote>\n<p>This chapter covers installing LoomWright, opening it for the first time, and drawing a first small circuit.</p>\n<h2 id=\"lm-getting-started--which-version-to-use\" data-anchor=\"which-version-to-use\">Which version to use</h2>\n<p>LoomWright comes in three forms. They are the same program and open the same files.</p>\n<div class=\"lm-table\"><table><thead><tr><th>Version</th><th>Best for</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Desktop app (installer)</td><td>Everyday use on your own computer. Opens and saves files directly and keeps your components in one place.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Portable Windows copy</td><td>Running from a folder or USB stick without installing, or on a computer where you cannot install programs.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>In a web browser</td><td>Trying LoomWright out, or using it on a computer where you cannot run the desktop app.</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"lm-getting-started--installing-the-desktop-app\" data-anchor=\"installing-the-desktop-app\">Installing the desktop app</h2>\n<p>The installers are on the project&#39;s GitHub page, under <strong>Releases</strong>. Download the file for your computer:</p>\n<div class=\"lm-table\"><table><thead><tr><th>Computer</th><th>File to download</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Windows 10 or 11</td><td>The <code>.msi</code> file, or the <code>-setup.exe</code> file. Either installs LoomWright.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Mac with Apple Silicon (M1 or later)</td><td>The <code>.dmg</code> file. Open it and drag LoomWright to Applications.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Linux</td><td>The <code>.AppImage</code> file (runs without installing), or the <code>.deb</code> file for Debian and Ubuntu.</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>The builds are not code-signed yet, so your computer warns you the first time:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Windows</strong> shows &quot;Windows protected your PC&quot;. Choose <strong>More info</strong>, then <strong>Run anyway</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Mac</strong> says the app cannot be checked for malicious software. Right-click (or Control-click) LoomWright in Applications and choose <strong>Open</strong>, then <strong>Open</strong> again. You only need to do this once.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>On Windows and Linux, the installer tells the computer that <code>.loom</code> files belong to LoomWright, so double-clicking a project file opens it. On a Mac, open projects with <strong>File › Open</strong> (<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>O</kbd>).</p>\n<h2 id=\"lm-getting-started--the-portable-windows-copy\" data-anchor=\"the-portable-windows-copy\">The portable Windows copy</h2>\n<p>The <code>-portable.zip</code> file holds <code>LoomWright.exe</code> and an empty file called <code>portable</code>. Unzip the whole folder somewhere you can write to, such as a USB stick or your Documents folder, and run <code>LoomWright.exe</code>.</p>\n<p>While the <code>portable</code> file sits beside the program, LoomWright keeps its settings, the projects it remembers between sessions, its save points and your <strong>My components</strong> library in a folder called <code>LoomWright-data</code> next to the program, and leaves nothing on the computer itself. Delete the <code>portable</code> file and it behaves like an installed copy.</p>\n<p>The portable copy needs the Microsoft Edge WebView2 runtime. Windows 10 and 11 already have it.</p>\n<h2 id=\"lm-getting-started--running-in-a-web-browser\" data-anchor=\"running-in-a-web-browser\">Running in a web browser</h2>\n<p>LoomWright also runs in a current web browser such as Chrome, Edge, Firefox or Safari. Download or clone the LoomWright source and open <code>app/index.html</code> in the browser. Nothing is installed and nothing is sent anywhere.</p>\n<p>In a browser a few things work differently, because a web page is not allowed to read and write files on its own:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Save</strong> asks where to put the file each time (in Chrome and Edge), or downloads it to your Downloads folder (in Firefox and Safari).</li>\n<li><strong>My components</strong>, your saved palette and the projects LoomWright remembers are kept in the browser&#39;s own storage. Clearing your browsing data clears them, so export your components now and then (see <a href=\"#lm-component-builder--sharing-components-and-blocks\" data-chapter=\"component-builder\" data-anchor=\"sharing-components-and-blocks\">Component builder and My components</a>).</li>\n<li>LoomWright cannot notice when another program changes the open file, so the Claude add-on needs the desktop app.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>If you work on the LoomWright source, <code>npm run serve</code> serves the same files at <code>http://localhost:5173</code>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"lm-getting-started--the-first-time-you-open-loomwright\" data-anchor=\"the-first-time-you-open-loomwright\">The first time you open LoomWright</h2>\n<p>The first time it starts, LoomWright opens an example project: the auxiliary electrics of a Land Rover Defender 110 on three sheets. It has a split-charge system, a fuse box, a relay-switched light bar, a fridge feed and a work lamp through a Deutsch connector. Every feature in this manual can be tried on it, and you can reopen it at any time with <strong>File › Open example project</strong>.</p>\n<p>LoomWright remembers the projects you had open, and reopens them next time, including changes you had not saved. That is a safety net, not a substitute for saving: save your work to a <code>.loom</code> file.</p>\n<h2 id=\"lm-getting-started--your-first-circuit\" data-anchor=\"your-first-circuit\">Your first circuit</h2>\n<p>This short exercise draws a fused lamp: a battery, a fuse and a lamp, with an earth.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Choose <strong>File › New project</strong>. If the example is open and you have not changed it, the new project replaces it; otherwise it opens in a new tab.</li>\n<li>In the <strong>Parts</strong> pane on the left, type <code>battery</code> in the search box. Drag <strong>Battery</strong> onto the sheet. Do the same for <strong>Blade fuse</strong>, <strong>Lamp / bulb</strong> and <strong>Chassis earth</strong>. You can also click a part in the list to drop it in the middle of the view.</li>\n<li>Point at the end of the battery&#39;s <strong>+</strong> lead. The pointer becomes a cross, a blue circle marks the pin, and a tooltip names it. Click it, then click the fuse&#39;s left-hand pin. A wire appears between them, numbered <code>W1</code>.</li>\n<li>Click the fuse&#39;s other pin, then one end of the lamp. Wire the lamp&#39;s other end to the earth. Place a second earth and wire the battery&#39;s <strong>−</strong> pin to it.</li>\n<li>Click a wire to select it. Its details appear in the <strong>Properties</strong> pane on the right: set the colour, the size and a circuit name such as <code>Lamp</code>.</li>\n<li>Look at the <strong>Wire list</strong> at the bottom of the window. Every wire is there, with where it starts and ends.</li>\n<li>Save with <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>S</kbd>. Give the file a name; LoomWright adds <code>.loom</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The rest of this manual explains each step in more detail. Good places to go next:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"#lm-the-window\" data-chapter=\"the-window\" data-anchor=\"\">The window</a> names the parts of the screen.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#lm-drawing\" data-chapter=\"drawing\" data-anchor=\"\">Drawing</a> covers placing, wiring and arranging.</li>\n<li><a href=\"#lm-wires-and-colours\" data-chapter=\"wires-and-colours\" data-anchor=\"\">Wires and colours</a> covers wire colours, sizes and the colour codes.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"lm-getting-started--getting-help-inside-loomwright\" data-anchor=\"getting-help-inside-loomwright\">Getting help inside LoomWright</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>This manual is built in: open it from the <strong>Help</strong> menu, or press <kbd>F1</kbd>. Use the search box at the top to find a word anywhere in it.</li>\n<li>Press <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>K</kbd> to search every command, sheet, part and wire by name.</li>\n<li><strong>Help › Keyboard and mouse</strong> lists the main shortcuts. <a href=\"#lm-keyboard-and-mouse\" data-chapter=\"keyboard-and-mouse\" data-anchor=\"\">Keyboard and mouse</a> has the full list.</li>\n<li>Hold the pointer over a button to see what it does.</li>\n<li>The status bar at the bottom of the window shows a hint for what you are doing.</li>\n</ul>","text":"Getting started\nLoomWright is a free program for drawing wiring diagrams, written first for vehicle looms. You draw the circuit with real components, give each wire its colour, size, length and circuit, and LoomWright turns that into the lists you need at the bench: a wire list, cut lists, connector tables, a fuse schedule, labels and a printable build pack.\nIt works offline. Your projects are ordinary files on your computer, saved with the extension .loom.\nLoomWright is a planning and drawing tool. Its checks and hints are guidance only, and it does not replace a qualified electrician or engineer. Read the Disclaimer before you build from your drawings.\nThis chapter covers installing LoomWright, opening it for the first time, and drawing a first small circuit.\n\nWhich version to use\nLoomWright comes in three forms. They are the same program and open the same files.\nVersion Best for\nDesktop app (installer) Everyday use on your own computer. Opens and saves files directly and keeps your components in one place.\nPortable Windows copy Running from a folder or USB stick without installing, or on a computer where you cannot install programs.\nIn a web browser Trying LoomWright out, or using it on a computer where you cannot run the desktop app.\n\nInstalling the desktop app\nThe installers are on the project's GitHub page, under Releases. Download the file for your computer:\nComputer File to download\nWindows 10 or 11 The .msi file, or the -setup.exe file. Either installs LoomWright.\nMac with Apple Silicon (M1 or later) The .dmg file. Open it and drag LoomWright to Applications.\nLinux The .AppImage file (runs without installing), or the .deb file for Debian and Ubuntu.\nThe builds are not code-signed yet, so your computer warns you the first time:\nWindows shows \"Windows protected your PC\". Choose More info, then Run anyway.\nMac says the app cannot be checked for malicious software. Right-click (or Control-click) LoomWright in Applications and choose Open, then Open again. You only need to do this once.\nOn Windows and Linux, the installer tells the computer that .loom files belong to LoomWright, so double-clicking a project file opens it. On a Mac, open projects with File › Open (Ctrl+O).\n\nThe portable Windows copy\nThe -portable.zip file holds LoomWright.exe and an empty file called portable. Unzip the whole folder somewhere you can write to, such as a USB stick or your Documents folder, and run LoomWright.exe.\nWhile the portable file sits beside the program, LoomWright keeps its settings, the projects it remembers between sessions, its save points and your My components library in a folder called LoomWright-data next to the program, and leaves nothing on the computer itself. Delete the portable file and it behaves like an installed copy.\nThe portable copy needs the Microsoft Edge WebView2 runtime. Windows 10 and 11 already have it.\n\nRunning in a web browser\nLoomWright also runs in a current web browser such as Chrome, Edge, Firefox or Safari. Download or clone the LoomWright source and open app/index.html in the browser. Nothing is installed and nothing is sent anywhere.\nIn a browser a few things work differently, because a web page is not allowed to read and write files on its own:\nSave asks where to put the file each time (in Chrome and Edge), or downloads it to your Downloads folder (in Firefox and Safari).\nMy components, your saved palette and the projects LoomWright remembers are kept in the browser's own storage. Clearing your browsing data clears them, so export your components now and then (see Component builder and My components).\nLoomWright cannot notice when another program changes the open file, so the Claude add-on needs the desktop app.\nIf you work on the LoomWright source, npm run serve serves the same files at http://localhost:5173.\n\nThe first time you open LoomWright\nThe first time it starts, LoomWright opens an example project: the auxiliary electrics of a Land Rover Defender 110 on three sheets. It has a split-charge system, a fuse box, a relay-switched light bar, a fridge feed and a work lamp through a Deutsch connector. Every feature in this manual can be tried on it, and you can reopen it at any time with File › Open example project.\nLoomWright remembers the projects you had open, and reopens them next time, including changes you had not saved. That is a safety net, not a substitute for saving: save your work to a .loom file.\n\nYour first circuit\nThis short exercise draws a fused lamp: a battery, a fuse and a lamp, with an earth.\nChoose File › New project. If the example is open and you have not changed it, the new project replaces it; otherwise it opens in a new tab.\nIn the Parts pane on the left, type battery in the search box. Drag Battery onto the sheet. Do the same for Blade fuse, Lamp / bulb and Chassis earth. You can also click a part in the list to drop it in the middle of the view.\nPoint at the end of the battery's + lead. The pointer becomes a cross, a blue circle marks the pin, and a tooltip names it. Click it, then click the fuse's left-hand pin. A wire appears between them, numbered W1.\nClick the fuse's other pin, then one end of the lamp. Wire the lamp's other end to the earth. Place a second earth and wire the battery's − pin to it.\nClick a wire to select it. Its details appear in the Properties pane on the right: set the colour, the size and a circuit name such as Lamp.\nLook at the Wire list at the bottom of the window. Every wire is there, with where it starts and ends.\nSave with Ctrl+S. Give the file a name; LoomWright adds .loom.\nThe rest of this manual explains each step in more detail. Good places to go next:\nThe window names the parts of the screen.\nDrawing covers placing, wiring and arranging.\nWires and colours covers wire colours, sizes and the colour codes.\n\nGetting help inside LoomWright\nThis manual is built in: open it from the Help menu, or press F1. Use the search box at the top to find a word anywhere in it.\nPress Ctrl+K to search every command, sheet, part and wire by name.\nHelp › Keyboard and mouse lists the main shortcuts. Keyboard and mouse has the full list.\nHold the pointer over a button to see what it does.\nThe status bar at the bottom of the window shows a hint for what you are doing.","sections":[{"anchor":"","title":"Getting started","level":1,"start":0},{"anchor":"which-version-to-use","title":"Which version to use","level":2,"start":773},{"anchor":"installing-the-desktop-app","title":"Installing the desktop app","level":2,"start":1259},{"anchor":"the-portable-windows-copy","title":"The portable Windows copy","level":2,"start":2216},{"anchor":"running-in-a-web-browser","title":"Running in a web browser","level":2,"start":2873},{"anchor":"the-first-time-you-open-loomwright","title":"The first time you open LoomWright","level":2,"start":3808},{"anchor":"your-first-circuit","title":"Your first circuit","level":2,"start":4419},{"anchor":"getting-help-inside-loomwright","title":"Getting help inside LoomWright","level":2,"start":5801}]},
{"id":"the-window","file":"02-the-window.md","title":"The window","html":"<h1 id=\"lm-the-window\">The window</h1>\n<p>This chapter names the parts of the LoomWright window, so the rest of the manual can refer to them.</p>\n<figure class=\"lm-fig\"><img src=\"manual/img/window.png\" alt=\"The LoomWright window with the example project open on sheet 2\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>The LoomWright window: menus and search along the top, the tool rail and Parts pane on the left, the sheet in the middle with the Lists pane below it, and Properties on the right.</figcaption></figure>\n<h2 id=\"lm-the-window--title-bar-and-menus\" data-anchor=\"title-bar-and-menus\">Title bar and menus</h2>\n<p>Along the top of the window:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Menus</strong>: File, Edit, View, Arrange, Sheet, Tools, Reports, Revision and Help. Every command in LoomWright is in one of these menus. Menu items show their keyboard shortcut on the right.</li>\n<li><strong>Search</strong> (<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>K</kbd>): see <a href=\"#lm-the-window--search-everything\" data-chapter=\"the-window\" data-anchor=\"search-everything\">Search everything</a> below.</li>\n<li><strong>The project name</strong>, followed by the file name once the project has been saved. A dot (•) after the name means there are changes not yet saved to the file.</li>\n<li><strong>The moon button</strong> switches between the light and dark themes. The drawing sheet stays white in both, so wire colours look the same.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"lm-the-window--search-everything\" data-anchor=\"search-everything\">Search everything</h2>\n<p>Press <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>K</kbd>, or click <strong>Search</strong> in the title bar, and start typing. The list finds:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>commands from every menu, with their shortcuts,</li>\n<li>the sheets in the project,</li>\n<li>the other projects you have open,</li>\n<li>parts already placed, by reference (for example <code>K1</code>) or name,</li>\n<li>wires, by wire number,</li>\n<li>components in the library, to place a new one.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Use <kbd>↑</kbd> and <kbd>↓</kbd> to move through the list, <kbd>Enter</kbd> to choose, and <kbd>Esc</kbd> to close it. Choosing a placed part or a wire goes to its sheet and selects it.</p>\n<h2 id=\"lm-the-window--the-tool-rail\" data-anchor=\"the-tool-rail\">The tool rail</h2>\n<p>The narrow column of buttons on the far left gives one-click access to the commands you use most:</p>\n<div class=\"lm-table\"><table><thead><tr><th>Button</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>New, Open, Save</td><td>Start a new project, open a <code>.loom</code> file, save the project.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Parts, Project, History</td><td>Show or hide the left-hand pane on that tab.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Trace</td><td>Trace the circuit of the selected wire. See <a href=\"#lm-trace-and-live-test\" data-chapter=\"trace-and-live-test\" data-anchor=\"\">Circuit trace and live test</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Test</td><td>Start or stop the live circuit test.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Check</td><td>Show the design checks. See <a href=\"#lm-design-checks\" data-chapter=\"design-checks\" data-anchor=\"\">Design checks</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Builder</td><td>Open the component builder.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Colours</td><td>Open <strong>Wire colours and stock</strong>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Formboard</td><td>Open the harness formboard.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Vehicle</td><td>Open the component locations view.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Lists</td><td>Show or hide the Lists pane under the sheet.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Pack</td><td>Make a build pack to print or save as PDF.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>PDF</td><td>Print every sheet, or save them as a PDF.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Claude</td><td>How to use the optional Claude add-on.</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"lm-the-window--the-left-pane\" data-anchor=\"the-left-pane\">The left pane</h2>\n<p>The left pane has three tabs. Click the same tool rail button again to hide the pane and give the drawing more room.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Parts</strong> is the component library, with a search box at the top. Parts are grouped by category; click a heading to fold it away. <strong>My blocks</strong> and <strong>My components</strong> at the top hold the circuits and symbols you have saved yourself. See <a href=\"#lm-drawing--placing-parts\" data-chapter=\"drawing\" data-anchor=\"placing-parts\">Drawing</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Project</strong> holds the project details (title, vehicle, drawn by, revision, date), the list of sheets, the harnesses, the multicore cables and the totals for the whole project. See <a href=\"#lm-projects-and-sheets\" data-chapter=\"projects-and-sheets\" data-anchor=\"\">Projects and sheets</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>History</strong> holds issued revisions and the save points kept on this computer. See <a href=\"#lm-revisions-and-save-points\" data-chapter=\"revisions-and-save-points\" data-anchor=\"\">Revision history and save points</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"lm-the-window--project-tabs-and-sheet-tabs\" data-anchor=\"project-tabs-and-sheet-tabs\">Project tabs and sheet tabs</h2>\n<p>Above the drawing are two rows of tabs.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Project tabs</strong> (the top row) show each open project. Click a tab to switch to it. The <strong>×</strong> on a tab closes that project; if it has unsaved changes, LoomWright asks first. The <strong>+</strong> at the end starts a new project.</li>\n<li><strong>Sheet tabs</strong> (the second row) show the sheets of the current project, numbered in order. Click a tab to show that sheet, double-click it to rename it, and use the <strong>+</strong> to add a sheet. <kbd>PgDn</kbd> and <kbd>PgUp</kbd> step through the sheets.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>At the right-hand end of the sheet tabs are the zoom buttons. The figure between <strong>−</strong> and <strong>+</strong> is the zoom level; click it to fit the whole sheet in the window.</p>\n<h2 id=\"lm-the-window--the-drawing-area\" data-anchor=\"the-drawing-area\">The drawing area</h2>\n<p>The middle of the window is the sheet you are drawing on. The sheet is always white with black lettering, whichever theme you use, because that is how it prints.</p>\n<p>How to move around:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Scroll to move up and down; hold <kbd>Shift</kbd> while scrolling to move sideways. A trackpad scrolls in both directions.</li>\n<li>Hold <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> and scroll (or pinch on a trackpad) to zoom in and out around the pointer. Zoom runs from 10% to 500%.</li>\n<li>Hold <kbd>Space</kbd> and drag, or drag with the middle mouse button, to pan.</li>\n<li>Press <kbd>0</kbd> to fit the sheet to the window.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"lm-the-window--the-properties-pane\" data-anchor=\"the-properties-pane\">The Properties pane</h2>\n<p>The pane on the right shows the details of whatever is selected, and is where you change them:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Nothing selected</strong>: settings for the sheet and for the whole drawing (paper size, colour codes, wire labels, title block and so on). See <a href=\"#lm-projects-and-sheets--sheet-and-drawing-settings\" data-chapter=\"projects-and-sheets\" data-anchor=\"sheet-and-drawing-settings\">Projects and sheets</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>A part</strong>: its reference, its fields, its part data and a list of its pins with the wires on each.</li>\n<li><strong>A wire</strong>: its number, circuit, harness, colours, size, length, current, insulation and how each end is finished. See <a href=\"#lm-wires-and-colours\" data-chapter=\"wires-and-colours\" data-anchor=\"\">Wires and colours</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Several things</strong>: buttons to rotate, duplicate, align or delete them together, and fields to set the colour, size, circuit, harness or insulation of every selected wire at once.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>View › Properties</strong> hides or shows this pane.</p>\n<h2 id=\"lm-the-window--the-lists-pane\" data-anchor=\"the-lists-pane\">The Lists pane</h2>\n<p>Under the sheet is the Lists pane, with a tab for each list LoomWright keeps up to date as you draw: <strong>Wire list</strong>, <strong>Parts &amp; costs</strong>, <strong>Cable totals</strong>, <strong>Colour usage</strong>, <strong>Fuses &amp; loads</strong>, <strong>Connectors</strong>, <strong>Cut list</strong>, <strong>Circuits</strong> and <strong>Checks</strong>. The number on a tab is a count, for example how many wires there are.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>All sheets</strong> shows the whole project; untick it to list only the sheet you are looking at.</li>\n<li>Click a row to select that wire or part on the drawing, going to its sheet if need be.</li>\n<li>The arrow at the right-hand end folds the pane away. The <strong>Lists</strong> tool rail button brings it back.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><a href=\"#lm-lists-and-reports\" data-chapter=\"lists-and-reports\" data-anchor=\"\">Lists and reports</a> describes each list.</p>\n<h2 id=\"lm-the-window--the-status-bar\" data-anchor=\"the-status-bar\">The status bar</h2>\n<p>The bar along the bottom of the window shows, from left to right:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>the pointer position on the sheet, in drawing units, and the zone (such as <code>B4</code>) when the sheet has a frame,</li>\n<li>which sheet you are on, out of how many, and its paper size,</li>\n<li>how many parts and wires are on this sheet,</li>\n<li>the design checks: <strong>Checks passed</strong>, or how many need a look. Click it to open the <strong>Checks</strong> list,</li>\n<li>a hint for what you are doing now, such as how to finish a wire,</li>\n<li>where LoomWright is running (Desktop or Browser) and its version. During the live test this starts with <strong>LIVE TEST</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"lm-the-window--narrow-windows\" data-anchor=\"narrow-windows\">Narrow windows</h2>\n<p>LoomWright works in a window as narrow as 900 pixels. On a smaller screen the panes get narrower. In a browser window narrower than that, the panes stack above and below the drawing instead of beside it.</p>","text":"The window\nThis chapter names the parts of the LoomWright window, so the rest of the manual can refer to them.\nThe LoomWright window: menus and search along the top, the tool rail and Parts pane on the left, the sheet in the middle with the Lists pane below it, and Properties on the right.\n\nTitle bar and menus\nAlong the top of the window:\nMenus: File, Edit, View, Arrange, Sheet, Tools, Reports, Revision and Help. Every command in LoomWright is in one of these menus. Menu items show their keyboard shortcut on the right.\nSearch (Ctrl+K): see Search everything below.\nThe project name, followed by the file name once the project has been saved. A dot (•) after the name means there are changes not yet saved to the file.\nThe moon button switches between the light and dark themes. The drawing sheet stays white in both, so wire colours look the same.\n\nSearch everything\nPress Ctrl+K, or click Search in the title bar, and start typing. The list finds:\ncommands from every menu, with their shortcuts,\nthe sheets in the project,\nthe other projects you have open,\nparts already placed, by reference (for example K1) or name,\nwires, by wire number,\ncomponents in the library, to place a new one.\nUse ↑ and ↓ to move through the list, Enter to choose, and Esc to close it. Choosing a placed part or a wire goes to its sheet and selects it.\n\nThe tool rail\nThe narrow column of buttons on the far left gives one-click access to the commands you use most:\nButton What it does\nNew, Open, Save Start a new project, open a .loom file, save the project.\nParts, Project, History Show or hide the left-hand pane on that tab.\nTrace Trace the circuit of the selected wire. See Circuit trace and live test.\nTest Start or stop the live circuit test.\nCheck Show the design checks. See Design checks.\nBuilder Open the component builder.\nColours Open Wire colours and stock.\nFormboard Open the harness formboard.\nVehicle Open the component locations view.\nLists Show or hide the Lists pane under the sheet.\nPack Make a build pack to print or save as PDF.\nPDF Print every sheet, or save them as a PDF.\nClaude How to use the optional Claude add-on.\n\nThe left pane\nThe left pane has three tabs. Click the same tool rail button again to hide the pane and give the drawing more room.\nParts is the component library, with a search box at the top. Parts are grouped by category; click a heading to fold it away. My blocks and My components at the top hold the circuits and symbols you have saved yourself. See Drawing.\nProject holds the project details (title, vehicle, drawn by, revision, date), the list of sheets, the harnesses, the multicore cables and the totals for the whole project. See Projects and sheets.\nHistory holds issued revisions and the save points kept on this computer. See Revision history and save points.\n\nProject tabs and sheet tabs\nAbove the drawing are two rows of tabs.\nProject tabs (the top row) show each open project. Click a tab to switch to it. The × on a tab closes that project; if it has unsaved changes, LoomWright asks first. The + at the end starts a new project.\nSheet tabs (the second row) show the sheets of the current project, numbered in order. Click a tab to show that sheet, double-click it to rename it, and use the + to add a sheet. PgDn and PgUp step through the sheets.\nAt the right-hand end of the sheet tabs are the zoom buttons. The figure between − and + is the zoom level; click it to fit the whole sheet in the window.\n\nThe drawing area\nThe middle of the window is the sheet you are drawing on. The sheet is always white with black lettering, whichever theme you use, because that is how it prints.\nHow to move around:\nScroll to move up and down; hold Shift while scrolling to move sideways. A trackpad scrolls in both directions.\nHold Ctrl and scroll (or pinch on a trackpad) to zoom in and out around the pointer. Zoom runs from 10% to 500%.\nHold Space and drag, or drag with the middle mouse button, to pan.\nPress 0 to fit the sheet to the window.\n\nThe Properties pane\nThe pane on the right shows the details of whatever is selected, and is where you change them:\nNothing selected: settings for the sheet and for the whole drawing (paper size, colour codes, wire labels, title block and so on). See Projects and sheets.\nA part: its reference, its fields, its part data and a list of its pins with the wires on each.\nA wire: its number, circuit, harness, colours, size, length, current, insulation and how each end is finished. See Wires and colours.\nSeveral things: buttons to rotate, duplicate, align or delete them together, and fields to set the colour, size, circuit, harness or insulation of every selected wire at once.\nView › Properties hides or shows this pane.\n\nThe Lists pane\nUnder the sheet is the Lists pane, with a tab for each list LoomWright keeps up to date as you draw: Wire list, Parts & costs, Cable totals, Colour usage, Fuses & loads, Connectors, Cut list, Circuits and Checks. The number on a tab is a count, for example how many wires there are.\nAll sheets shows the whole project; untick it to list only the sheet you are looking at.\nClick a row to select that wire or part on the drawing, going to its sheet if need be.\nThe arrow at the right-hand end folds the pane away. The Lists tool rail button brings it back.\nLists and reports describes each list.\n\nThe status bar\nThe bar along the bottom of the window shows, from left to right:\nthe pointer position on the sheet, in drawing units, and the zone (such as B4) when the sheet has a frame,\nwhich sheet you are on, out of how many, and its paper size,\nhow many parts and wires are on this sheet,\nthe design checks: Checks passed, or how many need a look. Click it to open the Checks list,\na hint for what you are doing now, such as how to finish a wire,\nwhere LoomWright is running (Desktop or Browser) and its version. During the live test this starts with LIVE TEST.\n\nNarrow windows\nLoomWright works in a window as narrow as 900 pixels. On a smaller screen the panes get narrower. In a browser window narrower than that, the panes stack above and below the drawing instead of beside it.","sections":[{"anchor":"","title":"The window","level":1,"start":0},{"anchor":"title-bar-and-menus","title":"Title bar and menus","level":2,"start":292},{"anchor":"search-everything","title":"Search everything","level":2,"start":855},{"anchor":"the-tool-rail","title":"The tool rail","level":2,"start":1339},{"anchor":"the-left-pane","title":"The left pane","level":2,"start":2130},{"anchor":"project-tabs-and-sheet-tabs","title":"Project tabs and sheet tabs","level":2,"start":2804},{"anchor":"the-drawing-area","title":"The drawing area","level":2,"start":3451},{"anchor":"the-properties-pane","title":"The Properties pane","level":2,"start":3983},{"anchor":"the-lists-pane","title":"The Lists pane","level":2,"start":4705},{"anchor":"the-status-bar","title":"The status bar","level":2,"start":5315},{"anchor":"narrow-windows","title":"Narrow windows","level":2,"start":5882}]},
{"id":"drawing","file":"03-drawing.md","title":"Drawing","html":"<h1 id=\"lm-drawing\">Drawing</h1>\n<p>This chapter covers putting parts on a sheet, wiring them together, and arranging the drawing so it is easy to read.</p>\n<p>LoomWright draws on a grid of 10 drawing units. Parts and bend points snap to it, so wires line up. On a printed sheet, 4 drawing units are 1 mm.</p>\n<h2 id=\"lm-drawing--placing-parts\" data-anchor=\"placing-parts\">Placing parts</h2>\n<p>The <strong>Parts</strong> pane on the left holds the component library: batteries, fuses, fuse boxes, switches, relays, lamps, motors, gauges, connectors and electronic parts, grouped by category.</p>\n<p>To place a part:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Drag it</strong> from the list onto the sheet, or</li>\n<li><strong>Click it</strong> in the list to drop it in the middle of the view.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Type in <strong>Search parts</strong> to narrow the list. The search looks at the part&#39;s name, category and reference letter, so <code>relay</code>, <code>fuse</code> or <code>K</code> all work. You can also place a part from the <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>K</kbd> search.</p>\n<p>Each new part gets a <strong>reference</strong>: a letter for the kind of part and the next free number, such as <code>F1</code>, <code>F2</code> for fuses, <code>K1</code> for relays, <code>S1</code> for switches and <code>H1</code> for lamps. Change it in the Properties pane if you prefer your own scheme. <strong>Edit › Renumber wires and parts</strong> tidies the numbers later; see <a href=\"#lm-drawing--renumbering-wires-and-parts\" data-chapter=\"drawing\" data-anchor=\"renumbering-wires-and-parts\">Renumbering</a>.</p>\n<p>Some parts change shape with their fields: a fuse box draws as many ways as you give it, and connectors, busbars, terminal strips, modules and ICs take any number of pins.</p>\n<h2 id=\"lm-drawing--editing-a-part\" data-anchor=\"editing-a-part\">Editing a part</h2>\n<p>Click a part to select it. The Properties pane then shows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Reference</strong>, and the part&#39;s own fields, such as a fuse rating, a relay&#39;s contact rating, a lamp&#39;s wattage or a switch legend. Most fields are written under the symbol on the drawing.</li>\n<li><strong>Part data</strong>: part number, supplier, unit cost and where it is fitted. See <a href=\"#lm-part-data\" data-chapter=\"part-data\" data-anchor=\"\">Part data</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Rotate</strong>, <strong>Flip</strong>, <strong>Duplicate</strong> and <strong>Delete</strong> buttons.</li>\n<li><strong>Pins</strong>: every pin with its name, the wire on it and where that wire goes. A pin with nothing on it says <strong>free</strong>. The <strong>⤳</strong> button beside a pin traces the circuit from that pin.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Double-click a part on the sheet to jump straight to its first field.</p>\n<h2 id=\"lm-drawing--selecting\" data-anchor=\"selecting\">Selecting</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Click</strong> a part or wire to select it.</li>\n<li><strong>Shift+click</strong> adds a part or wire to the selection, or takes it out again.</li>\n<li><strong>Drag across empty space</strong> to draw a selection box. It selects the parts that are wholly inside the box and the wires that run between them. Hold <kbd>Shift</kbd> while dragging to add to what is already selected.</li>\n<li><kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>A</kbd> selects everything on the sheet.</li>\n<li><kbd>Esc</kbd> clears the selection.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"lm-drawing--moving-rotating-and-flipping\" data-anchor=\"moving-rotating-and-flipping\">Moving, rotating and flipping</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Drag</strong> a selected part, or several, to move them. Wires follow.</li>\n<li>The <strong>arrow keys</strong> move the selection 10 units; with <kbd>Shift</kbd>, 50 units.</li>\n<li><kbd>R</kbd> rotates the selection a quarter turn clockwise; <kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>R</kbd> turns it the other way.</li>\n<li><kbd>F</kbd> flips the selection left to right.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>If you press on a pin and drag, LoomWright moves the part instead of starting a wire.</p>\n<h2 id=\"lm-drawing--drawing-wires\" data-anchor=\"drawing-wires\">Drawing wires</h2>\n<p>To draw a wire, <strong>click a pin, then click another pin</strong>. Point at a pin first: the pointer turns into a cross and a blue circle marks the pin.</p>\n<figure class=\"lm-fig\"><img src=\"manual/img/drawing-a-wire.png\" alt=\"A wire being drawn from a fuse box way towards a switch\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>While you draw, the wire follows the pointer as a dashed line in the colour it will have.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>While you are drawing:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Click empty space</strong> to add a bend point on the way.</li>\n<li><strong>Click the starting pin again</strong>, or press <kbd>Esc</kbd>, to give up.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Things to know about new wires:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>A new wire takes the colour, tracer, size and harness of the last wire you changed, so a run of similar wires is quick to draw.</li>\n<li>Wires are numbered <code>W1</code>, <code>W2</code> and so on. The numbers are unique across the whole project, not only the sheet.</li>\n<li>A pin can carry several wires. Where two or more wires meet at a pin, LoomWright draws a junction dot (<strong>View › Junction dots</strong> turns them off).</li>\n<li>To join wires part-way along, place a <strong>Splice / junction</strong> part from <strong>Wiring &amp; connectors</strong> and wire to it.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"lm-drawing--shaping-wire-routes\" data-anchor=\"shaping-wire-routes\">Shaping wire routes</h2>\n<p>By default a wire is drawn with square corners: across, down, and across again. When you select such a wire, a small square handle sits on its middle run. <strong>Drag the handle</strong> sideways to move that run.</p>\n<p>For more control, add bend points:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Double-click a wire</strong> to add a bend point where you clicked.</li>\n<li><strong>Drag a bend point</strong> to move it. Bend points show as small squares when the wire is selected.</li>\n<li><strong>Double-click a bend point</strong> to remove it.</li>\n<li><strong>Clear bends</strong> in the wire&#39;s Properties puts it back to the automatic route.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Under <strong>Cable</strong> in the wire&#39;s Properties, <strong>Routing</strong> chooses <strong>Square corners</strong> or <strong>Straight lines</strong>. Straight lines run directly from point to point, which suits a block diagram or a quick sketch.</p>\n<h2 id=\"lm-drawing--wire-labels\" data-anchor=\"wire-labels\">Wire labels</h2>\n<p>Each wire carries a label. What it shows is set for the whole drawing under <strong>Wire labels</strong> (with nothing selected): <strong>Number, colour, size</strong>, <strong>Colour code only</strong>, or <strong>None</strong>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Drag a label</strong> to move it somewhere clearer. A thin leader line points back to its wire.</li>\n<li><strong>Double-click a moved label</strong> to put it back in its usual place.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"lm-drawing--copy-paste-and-duplicate\" data-anchor=\"copy-paste-and-duplicate\">Copy, paste and duplicate</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>C</kbd> copies the selected parts and the wires between them. <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>X</kbd> cuts them.</li>\n<li><kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>V</kbd> pastes them, a little below and to the right. You can paste onto another sheet, or into another open project.</li>\n<li><kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>D</kbd> duplicates the selection in one step.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Pasted parts get new references and pasted wires get new numbers, so nothing clashes. A wire is only copied if both of its ends are on copied parts.</p>\n<h2 id=\"lm-drawing--deleting-and-undoing\" data-anchor=\"deleting-and-undoing\">Deleting and undoing</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><kbd>Delete</kbd> (or <kbd>Backspace</kbd>) deletes the selection. Deleting a part also deletes the wires on it.</li>\n<li><kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Z</kbd> undoes, and <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>Z</kbd> (or <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Y</kbd>) redoes. LoomWright keeps the last 200 steps for each open project.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"lm-drawing--aligning-and-spacing-parts\" data-anchor=\"aligning-and-spacing-parts\">Aligning and spacing parts</h2>\n<p>While you drag parts, pink dashed <strong>guide lines</strong> appear when an edge or the centre of what you are moving lines up with another part on the sheet. Let go on a guide line and the parts are in line.</p>\n<p>To line up several parts at once, select them and use the <strong>Arrange</strong> menu, or the <strong>Arrange</strong> buttons in the Properties pane:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Align left</strong>, <strong>Align centres</strong>, <strong>Align right</strong> line parts up on a vertical line.</li>\n<li><strong>Align tops</strong>, <strong>Align middles</strong>, <strong>Align bottoms</strong> line them up on a horizontal line.</li>\n<li><strong>Distribute across</strong> and <strong>Distribute down</strong> space three or more parts evenly between the outermost two. In the Properties pane these are called <strong>Space evenly across</strong> and <strong>Space evenly down</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"lm-drawing--blocks-reusable-circuits\" data-anchor=\"blocks-reusable-circuits\">Blocks: reusable circuits</h2>\n<p>A block is a piece of circuit you use again and again, such as a relay-switched lamp with its fuse and switch.</p>\n<p>To make one, select the parts and the wires between them, then choose <strong>Edit › Save selection as block</strong> (or <strong>Save as block</strong> in the Properties pane). Give it a name. It appears in the Parts pane under <strong>My blocks</strong>.</p>\n<p>To use it, drag it onto a sheet or click it. Its parts arrive with fresh references and fresh wire numbers. To delete a block from the library, click the <strong>✕</strong> on it in <strong>My blocks</strong>; the message that follows offers <strong>Undo</strong>.</p>\n<p>Blocks are kept in your library on this computer, with <strong>My components</strong>, not in the project. To move them to another computer, see <a href=\"#lm-component-builder--sharing-components-and-blocks\" data-chapter=\"component-builder\" data-anchor=\"sharing-components-and-blocks\">Sharing components and blocks</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"lm-drawing--finding-and-replacing\" data-anchor=\"finding-and-replacing\">Finding and replacing</h2>\n<p><strong>Edit › Find</strong> (<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>F</kbd>) searches wire numbers, circuits, harnesses, wire notes, part references, part values and text, locations and part numbers. Tick or untick each kind to narrow the search, and choose <strong>All sheets</strong>, <strong>Match case</strong> or <strong>Whole words</strong> as needed. Click a result to go to it.</p>\n<p><strong>Edit › Find and replace</strong> (<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>H</kbd>) does the same, then changes every match with <strong>Replace all</strong>. It is useful for renaming a circuit or a harness throughout the project. <strong>Replace all</strong> can be undone with <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Z</kbd>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"lm-drawing--renumbering-wires-and-parts\" data-anchor=\"renumbering-wires-and-parts\">Renumbering wires and parts</h2>\n<p>After a lot of editing, wire numbers and references end up out of order. <strong>Edit › Renumber wires and parts</strong> numbers them again in drawing order: sheet by sheet, then top to bottom and left to right.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>For wires, choose a <strong>Prefix</strong> (such as <code>W</code>), the number to <strong>Start at</strong> and the <strong>Step</strong>. <strong>Start each sheet at the next hundred</strong> gives sheet 1 wires 101, 102…, sheet 2 wires 201, 202…, and so on.</li>\n<li><strong>Part references</strong> renumbers F1, F2, K1… as well.</li>\n<li><strong>All sheets</strong>, or only the sheet you are on.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A part drawn on several sheets keeps one reference, and connectors keep pointing at the connector they mate with. Renumbering can be undone.</p>\n<blockquote class=\"lm-note\"><p>Renumbering changes the numbers on any labels you have already printed. Do it before you print labels, not after.</p></blockquote>","text":"Drawing\nThis chapter covers putting parts on a sheet, wiring them together, and arranging the drawing so it is easy to read.\nLoomWright draws on a grid of 10 drawing units. Parts and bend points snap to it, so wires line up. On a printed sheet, 4 drawing units are 1 mm.\n\nPlacing parts\nThe Parts pane on the left holds the component library: batteries, fuses, fuse boxes, switches, relays, lamps, motors, gauges, connectors and electronic parts, grouped by category.\nTo place a part:\nDrag it from the list onto the sheet, or\nClick it in the list to drop it in the middle of the view.\nType in Search parts to narrow the list. The search looks at the part's name, category and reference letter, so relay, fuse or K all work. You can also place a part from the Ctrl+K search.\nEach new part gets a reference: a letter for the kind of part and the next free number, such as F1, F2 for fuses, K1 for relays, S1 for switches and H1 for lamps. Change it in the Properties pane if you prefer your own scheme. Edit › Renumber wires and parts tidies the numbers later; see Renumbering.\nSome parts change shape with their fields: a fuse box draws as many ways as you give it, and connectors, busbars, terminal strips, modules and ICs take any number of pins.\n\nEditing a part\nClick a part to select it. The Properties pane then shows:\nReference, and the part's own fields, such as a fuse rating, a relay's contact rating, a lamp's wattage or a switch legend. Most fields are written under the symbol on the drawing.\nPart data: part number, supplier, unit cost and where it is fitted. See Part data.\nRotate, Flip, Duplicate and Delete buttons.\nPins: every pin with its name, the wire on it and where that wire goes. A pin with nothing on it says free. The ⤳ button beside a pin traces the circuit from that pin.\nDouble-click a part on the sheet to jump straight to its first field.\n\nSelecting\nClick a part or wire to select it.\nShift+click adds a part or wire to the selection, or takes it out again.\nDrag across empty space to draw a selection box. It selects the parts that are wholly inside the box and the wires that run between them. Hold Shift while dragging to add to what is already selected.\nCtrl+A selects everything on the sheet.\nEsc clears the selection.\n\nMoving, rotating and flipping\nDrag a selected part, or several, to move them. Wires follow.\nThe arrow keys move the selection 10 units; with Shift, 50 units.\nR rotates the selection a quarter turn clockwise; Shift+R turns it the other way.\nF flips the selection left to right.\nIf you press on a pin and drag, LoomWright moves the part instead of starting a wire.\n\nDrawing wires\nTo draw a wire, click a pin, then click another pin. Point at a pin first: the pointer turns into a cross and a blue circle marks the pin.\nWhile you draw, the wire follows the pointer as a dashed line in the colour it will have.\nWhile you are drawing:\nClick empty space to add a bend point on the way.\nClick the starting pin again, or press Esc, to give up.\nThings to know about new wires:\nA new wire takes the colour, tracer, size and harness of the last wire you changed, so a run of similar wires is quick to draw.\nWires are numbered W1, W2 and so on. The numbers are unique across the whole project, not only the sheet.\nA pin can carry several wires. Where two or more wires meet at a pin, LoomWright draws a junction dot (View › Junction dots turns them off).\nTo join wires part-way along, place a Splice / junction part from Wiring & connectors and wire to it.\n\nShaping wire routes\nBy default a wire is drawn with square corners: across, down, and across again. When you select such a wire, a small square handle sits on its middle run. Drag the handle sideways to move that run.\nFor more control, add bend points:\nDouble-click a wire to add a bend point where you clicked.\nDrag a bend point to move it. Bend points show as small squares when the wire is selected.\nDouble-click a bend point to remove it.\nClear bends in the wire's Properties puts it back to the automatic route.\nUnder Cable in the wire's Properties, Routing chooses Square corners or Straight lines. Straight lines run directly from point to point, which suits a block diagram or a quick sketch.\n\nWire labels\nEach wire carries a label. What it shows is set for the whole drawing under Wire labels (with nothing selected): Number, colour, size, Colour code only, or None.\nDrag a label to move it somewhere clearer. A thin leader line points back to its wire.\nDouble-click a moved label to put it back in its usual place.\n\nCopy, paste and duplicate\nCtrl+C copies the selected parts and the wires between them. Ctrl+X cuts them.\nCtrl+V pastes them, a little below and to the right. You can paste onto another sheet, or into another open project.\nCtrl+D duplicates the selection in one step.\nPasted parts get new references and pasted wires get new numbers, so nothing clashes. A wire is only copied if both of its ends are on copied parts.\n\nDeleting and undoing\nDelete (or Backspace) deletes the selection. Deleting a part also deletes the wires on it.\nCtrl+Z undoes, and Ctrl+Shift+Z (or Ctrl+Y) redoes. LoomWright keeps the last 200 steps for each open project.\n\nAligning and spacing parts\nWhile you drag parts, pink dashed guide lines appear when an edge or the centre of what you are moving lines up with another part on the sheet. Let go on a guide line and the parts are in line.\nTo line up several parts at once, select them and use the Arrange menu, or the Arrange buttons in the Properties pane:\nAlign left, Align centres, Align right line parts up on a vertical line.\nAlign tops, Align middles, Align bottoms line them up on a horizontal line.\nDistribute across and Distribute down space three or more parts evenly between the outermost two. In the Properties pane these are called Space evenly across and Space evenly down.\n\nBlocks: reusable circuits\nA block is a piece of circuit you use again and again, such as a relay-switched lamp with its fuse and switch.\nTo make one, select the parts and the wires between them, then choose Edit › Save selection as block (or Save as block in the Properties pane). Give it a name. It appears in the Parts pane under My blocks.\nTo use it, drag it onto a sheet or click it. Its parts arrive with fresh references and fresh wire numbers. To delete a block from the library, click the ✕ on it in My blocks; the message that follows offers Undo.\nBlocks are kept in your library on this computer, with My components, not in the project. To move them to another computer, see Sharing components and blocks.\n\nFinding and replacing\nEdit › Find (Ctrl+F) searches wire numbers, circuits, harnesses, wire notes, part references, part values and text, locations and part numbers. Tick or untick each kind to narrow the search, and choose All sheets, Match case or Whole words as needed. Click a result to go to it.\nEdit › Find and replace (Ctrl+H) does the same, then changes every match with Replace all. It is useful for renaming a circuit or a harness throughout the project. Replace all can be undone with Ctrl+Z.\n\nRenumbering wires and parts\nAfter a lot of editing, wire numbers and references end up out of order. Edit › Renumber wires and parts numbers them again in drawing order: sheet by sheet, then top to bottom and left to right.\nFor wires, choose a Prefix (such as W), the number to Start at and the Step. Start each sheet at the next hundred gives sheet 1 wires 101, 102…, sheet 2 wires 201, 202…, and so on.\nPart references renumbers F1, F2, K1… as well.\nAll sheets, or only the sheet you are on.\nA part drawn on several sheets keeps one reference, and connectors keep pointing at the connector they mate with. Renumbering can be undone.\nRenumbering changes the numbers on any labels you have already printed. Do it before you print labels, not after.","sections":[{"anchor":"","title":"Drawing","level":1,"start":0},{"anchor":"placing-parts","title":"Placing parts","level":2,"start":272},{"anchor":"editing-a-part","title":"Editing a part","level":2,"start":1248},{"anchor":"selecting","title":"Selecting","level":2,"start":1869},{"anchor":"moving-rotating-and-flipping","title":"Moving, rotating and flipping","level":2,"start":2254},{"anchor":"drawing-wires","title":"Drawing wires","level":2,"start":2618},{"anchor":"shaping-wire-routes","title":"Shaping wire routes","level":2,"start":3500},{"anchor":"wire-labels","title":"Wire labels","level":2,"start":4202},{"anchor":"copy-paste-and-duplicate","title":"Copy, paste and duplicate","level":2,"start":4526},{"anchor":"deleting-and-undoing","title":"Deleting and undoing","level":2,"start":4943},{"anchor":"aligning-and-spacing-parts","title":"Aligning and spacing parts","level":2,"start":5167},{"anchor":"blocks-reusable-circuits","title":"Blocks: reusable circuits","level":2,"start":5838},{"anchor":"finding-and-replacing","title":"Finding and replacing","level":2,"start":6555},{"anchor":"renumbering-wires-and-parts","title":"Renumbering wires and parts","level":2,"start":7060}]},
{"id":"wires-and-colours","file":"04-wires-and-colours.md","title":"Wires and colours","html":"<h1 id=\"lm-wires-and-colours\">Wires and colours</h1>\n<p>Every wire in LoomWright carries the information you need to make it: a number, the circuit it belongs to, the harness it runs in, its colours, its size, its length and how each end is finished. This chapter covers all of it except the ends, which have their own chapter, <a href=\"#lm-harnesses-connectors-and-ends\" data-chapter=\"harnesses-connectors-and-ends\" data-anchor=\"\">Harnesses, connectors and wire ends</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"lm-wires-and-colours--the-wires-properties\" data-anchor=\"the-wires-properties\">The wire&#39;s properties</h2>\n<p>Click a wire to select it. The Properties pane shows the wire number and code at the top, where it runs from and to, and these sections:</p>\n<figure class=\"lm-fig\"><img src=\"manual/img/wire-properties.png\" alt=\"The Properties pane for a selected wire\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>A selected wire: identity, colour and cable details. The ends are further down the pane.</figcaption></figure>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Identity</strong>: wire number, circuit and harness.</li>\n<li><strong>Colour</strong>: main colour, tracer and second tracer.</li>\n<li><strong>Cable</strong>: size, insulation, multicore cable, length, current, routing and notes.</li>\n<li><strong>End A</strong> and <strong>End B</strong>: terminal, fitting, strip length, seal and heat shrink for each end.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>At the bottom are <strong>Trace circuit</strong>, <strong>Swap ends</strong> (which turns the wire round, so End A becomes End B), <strong>Clear bends</strong> and <strong>Delete</strong>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"lm-wires-and-colours--wire-number-circuit-and-harness\" data-anchor=\"wire-number-circuit-and-harness\">Wire number, circuit and harness</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Wire number</strong> is the number printed on the drawing, the wire list and the labels. LoomWright gives each new wire the next free number. Numbers should be unique across the project; <a href=\"#lm-drawing--renumbering-wires-and-parts\" data-chapter=\"drawing\" data-anchor=\"renumbering-wires-and-parts\">Renumbering</a> tidies them.</li>\n<li><strong>Circuit</strong> is a name for what the wire does, such as <code>Spot lamps</code> or <code>Fridge</code>. Give every wire in one circuit the same name. Circuit names appear in the wire list, the cut list, the fuse schedule and the colour usage list.</li>\n<li><strong>Harness</strong> is the loom the wire runs in, such as <code>Engine bay</code>, <code>Dash</code> or <code>Rear body</code>. Pick one from the list, or type a new name to add a harness. Harnesses decide which cut list a wire goes on and which formboard it is laid out on. See <a href=\"#lm-harnesses-connectors-and-ends--harnesses\" data-chapter=\"harnesses-connectors-and-ends\" data-anchor=\"harnesses\">Harnesses</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"lm-wires-and-colours--colours-and-tracers\" data-anchor=\"colours-and-tracers\">Colours and tracers</h2>\n<p>A wire has a <strong>main colour</strong> and up to two <strong>tracers</strong> (the thin stripe along the insulation). The wire is drawn in its colours on the sheet, and its colour code is written on its label, for example <code>NR</code> for brown with a red tracer.</p>\n<p>If the colour you pick is already used by other wires, a note under the colour says what for, such as &quot;R is also used for Charging in Rear body&quot;. If the same colour is already used for a different circuit in the same harness, the note is shown as a warning, because two identical wires in one loom are easy to mix up.</p>\n<h2 id=\"lm-wires-and-colours--colour-codes\" data-anchor=\"colour-codes\">Colour codes</h2>\n<p>LoomWright writes colour codes in one of four ways. Choose under <strong>Colour codes</strong> in the drawing settings (click an empty part of the sheet), or in <strong>Tools › Wire colours and stock</strong>.</p>\n<div class=\"lm-table\"><table><thead><tr><th>Colour</th><th>Land Rover / British</th><th>DIN 72551 / IEC 60757</th><th>SAE / US</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Black</td><td>B</td><td>BK</td><td>BLK</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Brown</td><td>N</td><td>BN</td><td>BRN</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Red</td><td>R</td><td>RD</td><td>RED</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Orange</td><td>O</td><td>OG</td><td>ORN</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Yellow</td><td>Y</td><td>YE</td><td>YEL</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Green</td><td>G</td><td>GN</td><td>DK GRN</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Light green</td><td>LG</td><td>LGN</td><td>LT GRN</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Blue</td><td>U</td><td>BU</td><td>DK BLU</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Light blue</td><td>LU</td><td>LBU</td><td>LT BLU</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Purple / violet</td><td>P</td><td>VT</td><td>PPL</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Grey / slate</td><td>S</td><td>GY</td><td>GRY</td></tr>\n<tr><td>White</td><td>W</td><td>WH</td><td>WHT</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Pink</td><td>K</td><td>PK</td><td>PNK</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Tan</td><td></td><td></td><td>TAN</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Turquoise</td><td></td><td>TQ</td><td></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Natural / clear</td><td></td><td>NT</td><td>NAT</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Land Rover / British</strong> writes the main colour then the tracer with nothing between: <code>NR</code> is brown with a red tracer.</li>\n<li><strong>DIN</strong> and <strong>SAE</strong> put a slash between them: <code>BN/RD</code>, <code>BRN/RED</code>.</li>\n<li>Where a standard has no code for a colour, LoomWright uses the DIN code, or the colour&#39;s name.</li>\n<li><strong>Custom only</strong> offers only your own colours in the wire pickers, with your own codes. See <a href=\"#lm-wires-and-colours--your-own-colours\" data-chapter=\"wires-and-colours\" data-anchor=\"your-own-colours\">Your own colours</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Changing the standard relabels every wire in the project. The colours themselves do not change.</p>\n<h2 id=\"lm-wires-and-colours--your-own-colours\" data-anchor=\"your-own-colours\">Your own colours</h2>\n<p>If your cable does not match a standard colour, or you want your own codes, add your own colours. Open <strong>Tools › Wire colours and stock</strong> (or the <strong>Colours</strong> button on the tool rail) and use <strong>Add colour</strong> under <strong>My colours</strong>. Give each colour a name, a code and a shade for the drawing.</p>\n<figure class=\"lm-fig\"><img src=\"manual/img/wire-colours.png\" alt=\"The Wire colours dialog\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Tools, Wire colours and stock: the colour code standard, the colours you stock, your own colours and the standard colours.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>Your own colours are saved in the project, and also in your palette on this computer, so new projects start with them. In an older project, <strong>Add my saved palette</strong> brings them in. A colour that wires still use cannot be removed; change those wires first.</p>\n<h2 id=\"lm-wires-and-colours--colours-i-stock\" data-anchor=\"colours-i-stock\">Colours I stock</h2>\n<p>Most people building one loom buy a handful of colours, not sixteen. LoomWright can keep you to the colours you have:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Open <strong>Tools › Wire colours and stock</strong>.</li>\n<li>Tick <strong>Stock</strong> beside each colour you have on the shelf. <strong>Stock = colours in use</strong> ticks the colours the project already uses.</li>\n<li>Tick <strong>Only offer colours I stock in wire pickers</strong>. The same setting is in the drawing settings as <strong>Only offer colours I stock</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Now the colour pickers list only those colours. With few colours, the same colour ends up doing several jobs. That is normal on a home-built loom, and the <strong>Colour usage</strong> list keeps track of it.</p>\n<h2 id=\"lm-wires-and-colours--colour-usage\" data-anchor=\"colour-usage\">Colour usage</h2>\n<p>The <strong>Colour usage</strong> tab in the Lists pane lists every colour code in the project and every job it does: which circuit, in which harness, on which sheets, and the wire numbers.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>reused</strong> marks a colour used for more than one circuit.</li>\n<li><strong>same harness</strong> marks a colour used for different circuits inside one harness. These are the ones worth a second look: tell them apart by wire number, a sleeve marker or a label.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Reusing a colour is fine when the circuits run in different harnesses, or when every wire is numbered. The design checks also note a colour used for different circuits in one harness, as information rather than a fault.</p>\n<h2 id=\"lm-wires-and-colours--wire-size\" data-anchor=\"wire-size\">Wire size</h2>\n<p><strong>Size</strong> is the conductor&#39;s cross-section in mm², with the nearest AWG size beside it. The sizes on offer, with the current LoomWright uses as a rough guide for each, are:</p>\n<div class=\"lm-table\"><table><thead><tr><th>mm²</th><th>≈ AWG</th><th>Guide current</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>0.35</td><td>22</td><td>5 A</td></tr>\n<tr><td>0.5</td><td>20</td><td>7.5 A</td></tr>\n<tr><td>0.75</td><td>18</td><td>10 A</td></tr>\n<tr><td>1.0</td><td>17</td><td>15 A</td></tr>\n<tr><td>1.5</td><td>16</td><td>20 A</td></tr>\n<tr><td>2.0</td><td>14</td><td>25 A</td></tr>\n<tr><td>2.5</td><td>13</td><td>25 A</td></tr>\n<tr><td>4.0</td><td>12</td><td>30 A</td></tr>\n<tr><td>6.0</td><td>10</td><td>40 A</td></tr>\n<tr><td>10</td><td>8</td><td>60 A</td></tr>\n<tr><td>16</td><td>6</td><td>80 A</td></tr>\n<tr><td>25</td><td>4</td><td>120 A</td></tr>\n<tr><td>35</td><td>2</td><td>150 A</td></tr>\n<tr><td>50</td><td>1/0</td><td>200 A</td></tr>\n<tr><td>70</td><td>2/0</td><td>250 A</td></tr>\n<tr><td>95</td><td>3/0</td><td>300 A</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>The guide figures are for a single PVC automotive cable in free air. A wire bundled tightly in a loom, running hot in an engine bay, or a long run, can need a size larger. Use them to catch mistakes, then check your cable maker&#39;s ratings, and always fuse to protect the wire.</p>\n<p>On the drawing, heavier wires are drawn thicker.</p>\n<h2 id=\"lm-wires-and-colours--length-current-and-voltage-drop\" data-anchor=\"length-current-and-voltage-drop\">Length, current and voltage drop</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Length (m)</strong> is the length of the wire as fitted, in metres. Lengths add up into the cable totals, the cut lists and the parts cost. If you lay the harness out on a formboard, LoomWright can fill the lengths in for you: see <a href=\"#lm-harness-formboard--wire-lengths\" data-chapter=\"harness-formboard\" data-anchor=\"wire-lengths\">Harness formboard</a>. <strong>File › Import wire lengths</strong> brings them in from a list.</li>\n<li><strong>Current (A)</strong> is the current the wire carries when its load is on.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>With both filled in, LoomWright works out the <strong>voltage drop</strong> along the wire and shows it under the current, for example &quot;Voltage drop 0.02 V (0.2% of 12 V) one way at 25 A&quot;. It uses the resistance of copper at 20 °C for the one conductor, so it does not include the earth return or the connections. A drop of more than 3% of the system voltage is shown as a warning and appears in the design checks. Set the system voltage (12, 24 or 48 V) in the drawing settings.</p>\n<h2 id=\"lm-wires-and-colours--insulation\" data-anchor=\"insulation\">Insulation</h2>\n<p><strong>Insulation</strong> records the kind of cable, with its temperature rating:</p>\n<div class=\"lm-table\"><table><thead><tr><th>Insulation</th><th>Temperature rating</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Thin-wall FLRY-B</td><td>105 °C</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Standard PVC (FLY)</td><td>70–105 °C</td></tr>\n<tr><td>TXL (thin XLPE)</td><td>125 °C</td></tr>\n<tr><td>GXL (XLPE)</td><td>125 °C</td></tr>\n<tr><td>SXL (heavy XLPE)</td><td>125 °C</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Tefzel / ETFE (Spec 55)</td><td>150 °C</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Silicone</td><td>180 °C</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Battery / welding cable</td><td>90 °C</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>It appears in the cut list and the cable totals, so you buy the right cable. LoomWright does not change the guide currents for different insulation.</p>\n<h2 id=\"lm-wires-and-colours--multicore-cable-routing-and-notes\" data-anchor=\"multicore-cable-routing-and-notes\">Multicore cable, routing and notes</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>In cable</strong> makes the wire one core of a multicore or screened cable. See <a href=\"#lm-harnesses-connectors-and-ends--multicore-and-screened-cables\" data-chapter=\"harnesses-connectors-and-ends\" data-anchor=\"multicore-and-screened-cables\">Multicore and screened cables</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Routing</strong> chooses <strong>Square corners</strong> or <strong>Straight lines</strong> for how the wire is drawn. See <a href=\"#lm-drawing--shaping-wire-routes\" data-chapter=\"drawing\" data-anchor=\"shaping-wire-routes\">Shaping wire routes</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Notes</strong> is free text for anything else. It appears in the wire list.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"lm-wires-and-colours--changing-many-wires-at-once\" data-anchor=\"changing-many-wires-at-once\">Changing many wires at once</h2>\n<p>Select several wires (drag a box round them, or <kbd>Shift</kbd>+click each). Under <strong>Set on all selected wires</strong> in the Properties pane you can set the <strong>Main colour</strong>, <strong>Size</strong>, <strong>Circuit</strong>, <strong>Harness</strong> and <strong>Insulation</strong> of all of them in one go. Leave a field on <strong>Keep as is</strong> to leave it alone.</p>\n<p>To change one value throughout the project, such as renaming a circuit, use <strong>Edit › Find and replace</strong>; see <a href=\"#lm-drawing--finding-and-replacing\" data-chapter=\"drawing\" data-anchor=\"finding-and-replacing\">Finding and replacing</a>.</p>","text":"Wires and colours\nEvery wire in LoomWright carries the information you need to make it: a number, the circuit it belongs to, the harness it runs in, its colours, its size, its length and how each end is finished. This chapter covers all of it except the ends, which have their own chapter, Harnesses, connectors and wire ends.\n\nThe wire's properties\nClick a wire to select it. The Properties pane shows the wire number and code at the top, where it runs from and to, and these sections:\nA selected wire: identity, colour and cable details. The ends are further down the pane.\nIdentity: wire number, circuit and harness.\nColour: main colour, tracer and second tracer.\nCable: size, insulation, multicore cable, length, current, routing and notes.\nEnd A and End B: terminal, fitting, strip length, seal and heat shrink for each end.\nAt the bottom are Trace circuit, Swap ends (which turns the wire round, so End A becomes End B), Clear bends and Delete.\n\nWire number, circuit and harness\nWire number is the number printed on the drawing, the wire list and the labels. LoomWright gives each new wire the next free number. Numbers should be unique across the project; Renumbering tidies them.\nCircuit is a name for what the wire does, such as Spot lamps or Fridge. Give every wire in one circuit the same name. Circuit names appear in the wire list, the cut list, the fuse schedule and the colour usage list.\nHarness is the loom the wire runs in, such as Engine bay, Dash or Rear body. Pick one from the list, or type a new name to add a harness. Harnesses decide which cut list a wire goes on and which formboard it is laid out on. See Harnesses.\n\nColours and tracers\nA wire has a main colour and up to two tracers (the thin stripe along the insulation). The wire is drawn in its colours on the sheet, and its colour code is written on its label, for example NR for brown with a red tracer.\nIf the colour you pick is already used by other wires, a note under the colour says what for, such as \"R is also used for Charging in Rear body\". If the same colour is already used for a different circuit in the same harness, the note is shown as a warning, because two identical wires in one loom are easy to mix up.\n\nColour codes\nLoomWright writes colour codes in one of four ways. Choose under Colour codes in the drawing settings (click an empty part of the sheet), or in Tools › Wire colours and stock.\nColour Land Rover / British DIN 72551 / IEC 60757 SAE / US\nBlack B BK BLK\nBrown N BN BRN\nRed R RD RED\nOrange O OG ORN\nYellow Y YE YEL\nGreen G GN DK GRN\nLight green LG LGN LT GRN\nBlue U BU DK BLU\nLight blue LU LBU LT BLU\nPurple / violet P VT PPL\nGrey / slate S GY GRY\nWhite W WH WHT\nPink K PK PNK\nTan TAN\nTurquoise TQ\nNatural / clear NT NAT\nLand Rover / British writes the main colour then the tracer with nothing between: NR is brown with a red tracer.\nDIN and SAE put a slash between them: BN/RD, BRN/RED.\nWhere a standard has no code for a colour, LoomWright uses the DIN code, or the colour's name.\nCustom only offers only your own colours in the wire pickers, with your own codes. See Your own colours.\nChanging the standard relabels every wire in the project. The colours themselves do not change.\n\nYour own colours\nIf your cable does not match a standard colour, or you want your own codes, add your own colours. Open Tools › Wire colours and stock (or the Colours button on the tool rail) and use Add colour under My colours. Give each colour a name, a code and a shade for the drawing.\nTools, Wire colours and stock: the colour code standard, the colours you stock, your own colours and the standard colours.\nYour own colours are saved in the project, and also in your palette on this computer, so new projects start with them. In an older project, Add my saved palette brings them in. A colour that wires still use cannot be removed; change those wires first.\n\nColours I stock\nMost people building one loom buy a handful of colours, not sixteen. LoomWright can keep you to the colours you have:\nOpen Tools › Wire colours and stock.\nTick Stock beside each colour you have on the shelf. Stock = colours in use ticks the colours the project already uses.\nTick Only offer colours I stock in wire pickers. The same setting is in the drawing settings as Only offer colours I stock.\nNow the colour pickers list only those colours. With few colours, the same colour ends up doing several jobs. That is normal on a home-built loom, and the Colour usage list keeps track of it.\n\nColour usage\nThe Colour usage tab in the Lists pane lists every colour code in the project and every job it does: which circuit, in which harness, on which sheets, and the wire numbers.\nreused marks a colour used for more than one circuit.\nsame harness marks a colour used for different circuits inside one harness. These are the ones worth a second look: tell them apart by wire number, a sleeve marker or a label.\nReusing a colour is fine when the circuits run in different harnesses, or when every wire is numbered. The design checks also note a colour used for different circuits in one harness, as information rather than a fault.\n\nWire size\nSize is the conductor's cross-section in mm², with the nearest AWG size beside it. The sizes on offer, with the current LoomWright uses as a rough guide for each, are:\nmm² ≈ AWG Guide current\n0.35 22 5 A\n0.5 20 7.5 A\n0.75 18 10 A\n1.0 17 15 A\n1.5 16 20 A\n2.0 14 25 A\n2.5 13 25 A\n4.0 12 30 A\n6.0 10 40 A\n10 8 60 A\n16 6 80 A\n25 4 120 A\n35 2 150 A\n50 1/0 200 A\n70 2/0 250 A\n95 3/0 300 A\nThe guide figures are for a single PVC automotive cable in free air. A wire bundled tightly in a loom, running hot in an engine bay, or a long run, can need a size larger. Use them to catch mistakes, then check your cable maker's ratings, and always fuse to protect the wire.\nOn the drawing, heavier wires are drawn thicker.\n\nLength, current and voltage drop\nLength (m) is the length of the wire as fitted, in metres. Lengths add up into the cable totals, the cut lists and the parts cost. If you lay the harness out on a formboard, LoomWright can fill the lengths in for you: see Harness formboard. File › Import wire lengths brings them in from a list.\nCurrent (A) is the current the wire carries when its load is on.\nWith both filled in, LoomWright works out the voltage drop along the wire and shows it under the current, for example \"Voltage drop 0.02 V (0.2% of 12 V) one way at 25 A\". It uses the resistance of copper at 20 °C for the one conductor, so it does not include the earth return or the connections. A drop of more than 3% of the system voltage is shown as a warning and appears in the design checks. Set the system voltage (12, 24 or 48 V) in the drawing settings.\n\nInsulation\nInsulation records the kind of cable, with its temperature rating:\nInsulation Temperature rating\nThin-wall FLRY-B 105 °C\nStandard PVC (FLY) 70–105 °C\nTXL (thin XLPE) 125 °C\nGXL (XLPE) 125 °C\nSXL (heavy XLPE) 125 °C\nTefzel / ETFE (Spec 55) 150 °C\nSilicone 180 °C\nBattery / welding cable 90 °C\nIt appears in the cut list and the cable totals, so you buy the right cable. LoomWright does not change the guide currents for different insulation.\n\nMulticore cable, routing and notes\nIn cable makes the wire one core of a multicore or screened cable. See Multicore and screened cables.\nRouting chooses Square corners or Straight lines for how the wire is drawn. See Shaping wire routes.\nNotes is free text for anything else. It appears in the wire list.\n\nChanging many wires at once\nSelect several wires (drag a box round them, or Shift+click each). Under Set on all selected wires in the Properties pane you can set the Main colour, Size, Circuit, Harness and Insulation of all of them in one go. Leave a field on Keep as is to leave it alone.\nTo change one value throughout the project, such as renaming a circuit, use Edit › Find and replace; see Finding and replacing.","sections":[{"anchor":"","title":"Wires and colours","level":1,"start":0},{"anchor":"the-wires-properties","title":"The wire's properties","level":2,"start":328},{"anchor":"wire-number-circuit-and-harness","title":"Wire number, circuit and harness","level":2,"start":952},{"anchor":"colours-and-tracers","title":"Colours and tracers","level":2,"start":1644},{"anchor":"colour-codes","title":"Colour codes","level":2,"start":2206},{"anchor":"your-own-colours","title":"Your own colours","level":2,"start":3199},{"anchor":"colours-i-stock","title":"Colours I stock","level":2,"start":3865},{"anchor":"colour-usage","title":"Colour usage","level":2,"start":4473},{"anchor":"wire-size","title":"Wire size","level":2,"start":5110},{"anchor":"length-current-and-voltage-drop","title":"Length, current and voltage drop","level":2,"start":5829},{"anchor":"insulation","title":"Insulation","level":2,"start":6687},{"anchor":"multicore-cable-routing-and-notes","title":"Multicore cable, routing and notes","level":2,"start":7140},{"anchor":"changing-many-wires-at-once","title":"Changing many wires at once","level":2,"start":7446}]},
{"id":"projects-and-sheets","file":"05-projects-and-sheets.md","title":"Projects and sheets","html":"<h1 id=\"lm-projects-and-sheets\">Projects and sheets</h1>\n<p>A <strong>project</strong> is one vehicle or one piece of equipment. It is saved as one <code>.loom</code> file and can hold as many <strong>sheets</strong> as you need: charging, lighting, dash, trailer and so on. Wire numbers, part references, harnesses, colours, cables and revisions belong to the whole project, so the lists and checks cover every sheet.</p>\n<h2 id=\"lm-projects-and-sheets--working-with-several-projects\" data-anchor=\"working-with-several-projects\">Working with several projects</h2>\n<p>Each open project has a tab above the sheet tabs. You can have several open at once and copy parts and wires between them.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>File › New project</strong> (or the <strong>+</strong> at the end of the project tabs) starts an empty project. New projects start with the colours from your saved palette.</li>\n<li><strong>File › Open</strong> (<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>O</kbd>) opens a <code>.loom</code> file in a new tab. If the current tab is an untouched new project, the file replaces it.</li>\n<li><strong>File › Close project</strong>, or the <strong>×</strong> on its tab, closes a project. If it has unsaved changes, LoomWright asks before closing.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"lm-projects-and-sheets--project-details\" data-anchor=\"project-details\">Project details</h2>\n<p>Open the <strong>Project</strong> pane (the <strong>Project</strong> button on the tool rail). At the top are the project&#39;s details, which appear in the title block of every sheet:</p>\n<div class=\"lm-table\"><table><thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Shown in the title block as</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Title</td><td>PROJECT</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Vehicle</td><td>VEHICLE / CLIENT</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Drawn by</td><td>DRAWN BY</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Revision</td><td>REV</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Date</td><td>DATE</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>The title is also the name on the project tab. When you issue a revision, LoomWright sets the revision letter and date for you; see <a href=\"#lm-revisions-and-save-points\" data-chapter=\"revisions-and-save-points\" data-anchor=\"\">Revision history and save points</a>.</p>\n<p>The <strong>Totals</strong> section at the bottom of the pane counts sheets, parts and wires, and the total length of cable, over the whole project.</p>\n<h2 id=\"lm-projects-and-sheets--sheets\" data-anchor=\"sheets\">Sheets</h2>\n<p>The sheet tabs above the drawing show every sheet in order. The <strong>Sheet</strong> menu and the Project pane hold the same commands:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>New sheet</strong> (or <strong>+</strong> at the end of the sheet tabs) adds an empty sheet at the end and lets you type its name.</li>\n<li><strong>Duplicate sheet</strong> copies the current sheet, with every part and wire, next to it. The copied wires get new numbers, so wire numbers stay unique.</li>\n<li><strong>Rename sheet</strong>: or double-click its tab, or edit the name in the Project pane.</li>\n<li><strong>Move sheet left</strong> and <strong>Move sheet right</strong> change the order. The Project pane has up and down arrows for the same thing.</li>\n<li><strong>Delete sheet</strong> removes the sheet and everything on it. A project always keeps at least one sheet. Deleting can be undone.</li>\n<li><strong>Previous sheet</strong> (<kbd>PgUp</kbd>) and <strong>Next sheet</strong> (<kbd>PgDn</kbd>) step through them.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"lm-projects-and-sheets--sheet-and-drawing-settings\" data-anchor=\"sheet-and-drawing-settings\">Sheet and drawing settings</h2>\n<p>Click an empty part of the sheet so nothing is selected. The Properties pane then shows the settings.</p>\n<p>Under <strong>Sheet</strong>, for this sheet only:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Name</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Paper size</strong>: <strong>Project default</strong>, <strong>Fit to drawing</strong>, or A4, A3, A2 or A1.</li>\n<li><strong>Orientation</strong>: <strong>Project default</strong>, <strong>Landscape</strong> or <strong>Portrait</strong>.</li>\n<li>How many parts and wires the sheet has, and the length of cable on it.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Under <strong>Drawing</strong>, for every sheet in the project:</p>\n<div class=\"lm-table\"><table><thead><tr><th>Setting</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Paper size, all sheets</td><td>The paper size for sheets left on <strong>Project default</strong>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Orientation</td><td>The orientation for sheets left on <strong>Project default</strong>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>System voltage</td><td>12, 24 or 48 V. Used for voltage drop and for working out current from wattage.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Colour codes</td><td>Land Rover / British, DIN, SAE, or Custom only. See <a href=\"#lm-wires-and-colours--colour-codes\" data-chapter=\"wires-and-colours\" data-anchor=\"colour-codes\">Colour codes</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Wire labels</td><td>What each wire&#39;s label shows: <strong>Number, colour, size</strong>, <strong>Colour code only</strong>, or <strong>None</strong>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Title block and revision table</td><td>Draws the title block, with the revision table above it.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Colour key</td><td>Draws a key of every wire colour used on the sheet.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Cross-references</td><td>Writes where an off-sheet link continues, such as <code>→ 2/B4</code>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Junction dots</td><td>Draws a dot where two or more wires meet at a pin.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Black and white</td><td>Draws wires in dark grey for printing on a mono printer. See <a href=\"#lm-projects-and-sheets--black-and-white-drawings\" data-chapter=\"projects-and-sheets\" data-anchor=\"black-and-white-drawings\">Black and white drawings</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Only offer colours I stock</td><td>Limits the wire colour pickers to the colours you stock.</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Under <strong>Build</strong>, also for the whole project:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Cut allowance per wire (m)</strong>: extra length added to every wire in the cut list for stripping and terminating. It starts at 0.15 m.</li>\n<li><strong>Currency symbol</strong>: used for prices in the parts list and cable totals. It starts at £.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Wire colours…</strong> opens the colours dialog, and <strong>Project details</strong> opens the Project pane.</p>\n<h2 id=\"lm-projects-and-sheets--paper-sizes-frame-and-zones\" data-anchor=\"paper-sizes-frame-and-zones\">Paper sizes, frame and zones</h2>\n<p>With <strong>Fit to drawing</strong>, the sheet has no fixed size: it grows to fit whatever you draw. When you print or export it, LoomWright draws a border round the drawing and adds the colour key and title block beside it.</p>\n<p>With a fixed paper size (A4 to A1), the editor shows the sheet as it will print: a frame with a zone grid round the edge, the colour key in the top right-hand corner, and the title block and revision table in the bottom right-hand corner. Keep the drawing inside the frame.</p>\n<div class=\"lm-table\"><table><thead><tr><th>Size</th><th>Zones across × down (landscape)</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>A4</td><td>6 × 4</td></tr>\n<tr><td>A3</td><td>8 × 6</td></tr>\n<tr><td>A2</td><td>12 × 8</td></tr>\n<tr><td>A1</td><td>16 × 12</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Zones are named by a row letter and a column number, such as <code>B4</code>, as on an engineering drawing. The status bar shows the zone under the pointer, and cross-references use zones to say exactly where a link continues.</p>\n<h2 id=\"lm-projects-and-sheets--joining-sheets\" data-anchor=\"joining-sheets\">Joining sheets</h2>\n<p>A circuit often runs across several sheets: the battery and main fuses on sheet 1, the lighting on sheet 2. LoomWright has three ways to join them, and all three count as real connections for tracing, testing, checks and lists.</p>\n<h3 id=\"lm-projects-and-sheets--off-sheet-references\" data-anchor=\"off-sheet-references\">Off-sheet references</h3>\n<p>Place an <strong>Off-sheet reference</strong> (in <strong>Wiring &amp; connectors</strong>) where a wire leaves the sheet, and another on the sheet where it arrives. Give both the same <strong>Link name (same on both sheets)</strong>, such as <code>AUX FEED</code>. The <strong>Text</strong> field is what is written in the box, such as <code>TO FUSE BOX FB1</code>.</p>\n<p>With <strong>Cross-references</strong> on, LoomWright writes under each reference where the other end is: <code>→ 2/B4</code> means sheet 2, zone B4 (or only the sheet number on a sheet without zones). Click that text on the drawing to go there. The Properties pane lists the other ends under <strong>Continues on</strong>; click one to go to it.</p>\n<h3 id=\"lm-projects-and-sheets--net-labels\" data-anchor=\"net-labels\">Net labels</h3>\n<p>A <strong>Net label</strong> joins every wire whose label has the same <strong>Net name</strong>, on any sheet, without drawing a wire between them. Use it for a supply such as <code>IGN 15 (+12V)</code> that many parts share. Net names and off-sheet link names are matched without regard to capitals, and a net label and an off-sheet reference with the same name are joined too.</p>\n<h3 id=\"lm-projects-and-sheets--the-same-part-on-several-sheets\" data-anchor=\"the-same-part-on-several-sheets\">The same part on several sheets</h3>\n<p>If a part with the same reference and the same type is placed on two sheets, LoomWright treats it as one part drawn twice. For example, fuse box <code>FB1</code> can appear on the lighting sheet with ways 1 and 3 wired, and on the accessories sheet with way 5 wired. The Properties pane lists the other sheets under <strong>Also drawn on</strong>. Relays work the same way, so a relay&#39;s coil can be on one sheet and its contacts on another.</p>\n<p>The design checks warn if one reference is used for two different kinds of part on different sheets, and flag it as an error if a reference is used twice on one sheet.</p>\n<h2 id=\"lm-projects-and-sheets--title-block-and-revision-table\" data-anchor=\"title-block-and-revision-table\">Title block and revision table</h2>\n<p>With <strong>Title block and revision table</strong> ticked, each sheet carries a title block with:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>SHEET TITLE (the sheet name),</li>\n<li>PROJECT, VEHICLE / CLIENT and SIZE,</li>\n<li>DRAWN BY, DATE, REV, SHEET (for example <code>2 of 5</code>) and DRAWN WITH LoomWright.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Above it, once you have issued revisions, a revision table lists the latest six: revision, date, description and who issued it.</p>\n<h2 id=\"lm-projects-and-sheets--black-and-white-drawings\" data-anchor=\"black-and-white-drawings\">Black and white drawings</h2>\n<p>Colour wires are hard to tell apart on a mono printout. With <strong>Black and white</strong> on (in the drawing settings, or <strong>View › Black and white drawings</strong>), wires are drawn in dark grey, tracers are not drawn, and the colour key lists the codes without colour swatches.</p>\n<p>In black and white the only way to read a wire&#39;s colour is its code on the label, so keep <strong>Wire labels</strong> on <strong>Number, colour, size</strong> or <strong>Colour code only</strong>.</p>\n<p>The build pack has its own black-and-white option, so you can keep the editor in colour and still print the pack in black and white.</p>","text":"Projects and sheets\nA project is one vehicle or one piece of equipment. It is saved as one .loom file and can hold as many sheets as you need: charging, lighting, dash, trailer and so on. Wire numbers, part references, harnesses, colours, cables and revisions belong to the whole project, so the lists and checks cover every sheet.\n\nWorking with several projects\nEach open project has a tab above the sheet tabs. You can have several open at once and copy parts and wires between them.\nFile › New project (or the + at the end of the project tabs) starts an empty project. New projects start with the colours from your saved palette.\nFile › Open (Ctrl+O) opens a .loom file in a new tab. If the current tab is an untouched new project, the file replaces it.\nFile › Close project, or the × on its tab, closes a project. If it has unsaved changes, LoomWright asks before closing.\n\nProject details\nOpen the Project pane (the Project button on the tool rail). At the top are the project's details, which appear in the title block of every sheet:\nField Shown in the title block as\nTitle PROJECT\nVehicle VEHICLE / CLIENT\nDrawn by DRAWN BY\nRevision REV\nDate DATE\nThe title is also the name on the project tab. When you issue a revision, LoomWright sets the revision letter and date for you; see Revision history and save points.\nThe Totals section at the bottom of the pane counts sheets, parts and wires, and the total length of cable, over the whole project.\n\nSheets\nThe sheet tabs above the drawing show every sheet in order. The Sheet menu and the Project pane hold the same commands:\nNew sheet (or + at the end of the sheet tabs) adds an empty sheet at the end and lets you type its name.\nDuplicate sheet copies the current sheet, with every part and wire, next to it. The copied wires get new numbers, so wire numbers stay unique.\nRename sheet: or double-click its tab, or edit the name in the Project pane.\nMove sheet left and Move sheet right change the order. The Project pane has up and down arrows for the same thing.\nDelete sheet removes the sheet and everything on it. A project always keeps at least one sheet. Deleting can be undone.\nPrevious sheet (PgUp) and Next sheet (PgDn) step through them.\n\nSheet and drawing settings\nClick an empty part of the sheet so nothing is selected. The Properties pane then shows the settings.\nUnder Sheet, for this sheet only:\nName.\nPaper size: Project default, Fit to drawing, or A4, A3, A2 or A1.\nOrientation: Project default, Landscape or Portrait.\nHow many parts and wires the sheet has, and the length of cable on it.\nUnder Drawing, for every sheet in the project:\nSetting What it does\nPaper size, all sheets The paper size for sheets left on Project default.\nOrientation The orientation for sheets left on Project default.\nSystem voltage 12, 24 or 48 V. Used for voltage drop and for working out current from wattage.\nColour codes Land Rover / British, DIN, SAE, or Custom only. See Colour codes.\nWire labels What each wire's label shows: Number, colour, size, Colour code only, or None.\nTitle block and revision table Draws the title block, with the revision table above it.\nColour key Draws a key of every wire colour used on the sheet.\nCross-references Writes where an off-sheet link continues, such as → 2/B4.\nJunction dots Draws a dot where two or more wires meet at a pin.\nBlack and white Draws wires in dark grey for printing on a mono printer. See Black and white drawings.\nOnly offer colours I stock Limits the wire colour pickers to the colours you stock.\nUnder Build, also for the whole project:\nCut allowance per wire (m): extra length added to every wire in the cut list for stripping and terminating. It starts at 0.15 m.\nCurrency symbol: used for prices in the parts list and cable totals. It starts at £.\nWire colours… opens the colours dialog, and Project details opens the Project pane.\n\nPaper sizes, frame and zones\nWith Fit to drawing, the sheet has no fixed size: it grows to fit whatever you draw. When you print or export it, LoomWright draws a border round the drawing and adds the colour key and title block beside it.\nWith a fixed paper size (A4 to A1), the editor shows the sheet as it will print: a frame with a zone grid round the edge, the colour key in the top right-hand corner, and the title block and revision table in the bottom right-hand corner. Keep the drawing inside the frame.\nSize Zones across × down (landscape)\nA4 6 × 4\nA3 8 × 6\nA2 12 × 8\nA1 16 × 12\nZones are named by a row letter and a column number, such as B4, as on an engineering drawing. The status bar shows the zone under the pointer, and cross-references use zones to say exactly where a link continues.\n\nJoining sheets\nA circuit often runs across several sheets: the battery and main fuses on sheet 1, the lighting on sheet 2. LoomWright has three ways to join them, and all three count as real connections for tracing, testing, checks and lists.\n\nOff-sheet references\nPlace an Off-sheet reference (in Wiring & connectors) where a wire leaves the sheet, and another on the sheet where it arrives. Give both the same Link name (same on both sheets), such as AUX FEED. The Text field is what is written in the box, such as TO FUSE BOX FB1.\nWith Cross-references on, LoomWright writes under each reference where the other end is: → 2/B4 means sheet 2, zone B4 (or only the sheet number on a sheet without zones). Click that text on the drawing to go there. The Properties pane lists the other ends under Continues on; click one to go to it.\n\nNet labels\nA Net label joins every wire whose label has the same Net name, on any sheet, without drawing a wire between them. Use it for a supply such as IGN 15 (+12V) that many parts share. Net names and off-sheet link names are matched without regard to capitals, and a net label and an off-sheet reference with the same name are joined too.\n\nThe same part on several sheets\nIf a part with the same reference and the same type is placed on two sheets, LoomWright treats it as one part drawn twice. For example, fuse box FB1 can appear on the lighting sheet with ways 1 and 3 wired, and on the accessories sheet with way 5 wired. The Properties pane lists the other sheets under Also drawn on. Relays work the same way, so a relay's coil can be on one sheet and its contacts on another.\nThe design checks warn if one reference is used for two different kinds of part on different sheets, and flag it as an error if a reference is used twice on one sheet.\n\nTitle block and revision table\nWith Title block and revision table ticked, each sheet carries a title block with:\nSHEET TITLE (the sheet name),\nPROJECT, VEHICLE / CLIENT and SIZE,\nDRAWN BY, DATE, REV, SHEET (for example 2 of 5) and DRAWN WITH LoomWright.\nAbove it, once you have issued revisions, a revision table lists the latest six: revision, date, description and who issued it.\n\nBlack and white drawings\nColour wires are hard to tell apart on a mono printout. With Black and white on (in the drawing settings, or View › Black and white drawings), wires are drawn in dark grey, tracers are not drawn, and the colour key lists the codes without colour swatches.\nIn black and white the only way to read a wire's colour is its code on the label, so keep Wire labels on Number, colour, size or Colour code only.\nThe build pack has its own black-and-white option, so you can keep the editor in colour and still print the pack in black and white.","sections":[{"anchor":"","title":"Projects and sheets","level":1,"start":0},{"anchor":"working-with-several-projects","title":"Working with several projects","level":2,"start":333},{"anchor":"project-details","title":"Project details","level":2,"start":878},{"anchor":"sheets","title":"Sheets","level":2,"start":1454},{"anchor":"sheet-and-drawing-settings","title":"Sheet and drawing settings","level":2,"start":2205},{"anchor":"paper-sizes-frame-and-zones","title":"Paper sizes, frame and zones","level":2,"start":3853},{"anchor":"joining-sheets","title":"Joining sheets","level":2,"start":4656},{"anchor":"off-sheet-references","title":"Off-sheet references","level":3,"start":4900},{"anchor":"net-labels","title":"Net labels","level":3,"start":5491},{"anchor":"the-same-part-on-several-sheets","title":"The same part on several sheets","level":3,"start":5836},{"anchor":"title-block-and-revision-table","title":"Title block and revision table","level":2,"start":6448},{"anchor":"black-and-white-drawings","title":"Black and white drawings","level":2,"start":6832}]},
{"id":"files-and-saving","file":"06-files-and-saving.md","title":"Files and saving","html":"<h1 id=\"lm-files-and-saving\">Files and saving</h1>\n<p>This chapter covers how LoomWright saves your work, what it keeps on your computer besides your project files, and where.</p>\n<h2 id=\"lm-files-and-saving--project-files\" data-anchor=\"project-files\">Project files</h2>\n<p>Each project is saved as one file with the extension <code>.loom</code>. It holds every sheet, part and wire, the project settings, your own colours, any custom components the project uses, the harness formboard layouts, the vehicle picture and the issued revisions.</p>\n<p>A <code>.loom</code> file is plain text (JSON), so:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>it is small, and safe to email or keep in cloud storage,</li>\n<li>it works well with version control such as Git, which can show what changed between two versions,</li>\n<li>other programs can read it. The format is described in the LoomWright source, in <span class=\"lm-path\" title=\"In the LoomWright source: docs/file-format.md\">the .loom file format <code>docs/file-format.md</code></span>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"lm-files-and-saving--saving\" data-anchor=\"saving\">Saving</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>File › Save</strong> (<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>S</kbd>) saves the project. The first time, it asks where, and suggests a file name made from the project title.</li>\n<li><strong>File › Save as</strong> (<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>S</kbd>) saves a copy under a new name; from then on, the project is saved to the new file.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A dot (•) after the project name in the title bar, and on its tab, means there are changes not yet saved to the file.</p>\n<p>In the <strong>desktop app</strong>, Save writes straight to the file. The file is written in full and then swapped in, so a program reading it at that moment never sees half a file.</p>\n<p>In a <strong>web browser</strong>, a page is not allowed to write to a file it opened. In Chrome and Edge, Save asks where to save each time. In Firefox and Safari, it downloads the file to your Downloads folder; move it somewhere sensible and open it from there next time.</p>\n<h2 id=\"lm-files-and-saving--opening-files\" data-anchor=\"opening-files\">Opening files</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>File › Open</strong> (<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>O</kbd>) opens a <code>.loom</code> file in a new tab.</li>\n<li>On Windows and Linux, double-clicking a <code>.loom</code> file opens it in LoomWright.</li>\n<li><strong>File › Open example project</strong> opens the Defender example.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Files saved by earlier versions of LoomWright open normally and are brought up to date when you next save them.</p>\n<h2 id=\"lm-files-and-saving--when-the-file-changes-on-disk\" data-anchor=\"when-the-file-changes-on-disk\">When the file changes on disk</h2>\n<p>The desktop app checks every second or two whether the open file has been changed by another program, such as the Claude add-on, a Git pull, or a sync service.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>If you have no unsaved changes, LoomWright reloads the file and says <strong>Updated from file. Ctrl+Z undoes the change.</strong> Undo takes you back to how it was before the reload.</li>\n<li>If you do have unsaved changes, it says <strong>This file changed on disk</strong> and offers <strong>Reload</strong>. Reloading replaces your unsaved changes with the file; ignoring it keeps your version, and your next save overwrites the file.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A browser cannot watch files, so this only happens in the desktop app.</p>\n<h2 id=\"lm-files-and-saving--what-loomwright-remembers-between-sessions\" data-anchor=\"what-loomwright-remembers-between-sessions\">What LoomWright remembers between sessions</h2>\n<p>Besides your project files, LoomWright keeps a few things of its own on this computer:</p>\n<div class=\"lm-table\"><table><thead><tr><th>What</th><th>Where it is kept</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>The projects you had open, including unsaved changes</td><td>The app&#39;s own storage. They reopen next time you start LoomWright.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Save points (copies of each project at points in time)</td><td>The app&#39;s own storage. See <a href=\"#lm-revisions-and-save-points--save-points\" data-chapter=\"revisions-and-save-points\" data-anchor=\"save-points\">Save points</a>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>My components, My blocks and your saved palette of colours</td><td>A file called <code>library.json</code> in the desktop app&#39;s data folder, or the browser&#39;s storage when running in a browser.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Language, theme and similar settings</td><td>The app&#39;s own storage.</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>The projects LoomWright remembers are a safety net in case the computer or the program stops unexpectedly. They are not a substitute for saving: save your work to a <code>.loom</code> file, and keep a copy somewhere else.</p>\n<h2 id=\"lm-files-and-saving--where-the-desktop-app-keeps-its-data\" data-anchor=\"where-the-desktop-app-keeps-its-data\">Where the desktop app keeps its data</h2>\n<div class=\"lm-table\"><table><thead><tr><th>System</th><th>Folder</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Windows</td><td><code>%APPDATA%\\org.loomwright.app</code></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Mac</td><td><code>~/Library/Application Support/org.loomwright.app</code></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Linux</td><td><code>~/.local/share/org.loomwright.app</code></td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Help › About LoomWright</strong> shows the exact path of your library file on this computer.</p>\n<p>In the portable Windows copy, everything is kept in the <code>LoomWright-data</code> folder next to <code>LoomWright.exe</code> instead: the library, the app&#39;s own storage and the save points. Copy the whole folder to move LoomWright, with your components and remembered projects, to another computer. See <a href=\"#lm-getting-started--the-portable-windows-copy\" data-chapter=\"getting-started\" data-anchor=\"the-portable-windows-copy\">The portable Windows copy</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"lm-files-and-saving--in-a-web-browser\" data-anchor=\"in-a-web-browser\">In a web browser</h2>\n<p>When LoomWright runs in a browser, everything it remembers lives in that browser&#39;s storage for the page&#39;s address. That has some consequences:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>A different browser, or the same browser in a private window, starts empty.</li>\n<li>Clearing your browsing data or site data clears your remembered projects, save points and <strong>My components</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Export your components and blocks now and then with <strong>Tools › Export my components and blocks</strong>, and keep your projects as <code>.loom</code> files.</p>\n<h2 id=\"lm-files-and-saving--sharing-a-project-with-someone\" data-anchor=\"sharing-a-project-with-someone\">Sharing a project with someone</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Send the <code>.loom</code> file. Custom components used in the project travel inside it, so the other person sees the same symbols. Their colour palette and blocks are their own.</li>\n<li>For someone without LoomWright, use <strong>File › Save read-only web page</strong>. It writes a single web page with the drawings and the wire list, which anyone can open in a browser. See <a href=\"#lm-import-and-export--a-read-only-web-page\" data-chapter=\"import-and-export\" data-anchor=\"a-read-only-web-page\">Import and export</a>.</li>\n<li>For the workshop, print a build pack. See <a href=\"#lm-build-pack-and-labels\" data-chapter=\"build-pack-and-labels\" data-anchor=\"\">Build pack and wire labels</a>.</li>\n</ul>","text":"Files and saving\nThis chapter covers how LoomWright saves your work, what it keeps on your computer besides your project files, and where.\n\nProject files\nEach project is saved as one file with the extension .loom. It holds every sheet, part and wire, the project settings, your own colours, any custom components the project uses, the harness formboard layouts, the vehicle picture and the issued revisions.\nA .loom file is plain text (JSON), so:\nit is small, and safe to email or keep in cloud storage,\nit works well with version control such as Git, which can show what changed between two versions,\nother programs can read it. The format is described in the LoomWright source, in the .loom file format docs/file-format.md.\n\nSaving\nFile › Save (Ctrl+S) saves the project. The first time, it asks where, and suggests a file name made from the project title.\nFile › Save as (Ctrl+Shift+S) saves a copy under a new name; from then on, the project is saved to the new file.\nA dot (•) after the project name in the title bar, and on its tab, means there are changes not yet saved to the file.\nIn the desktop app, Save writes straight to the file. The file is written in full and then swapped in, so a program reading it at that moment never sees half a file.\nIn a web browser, a page is not allowed to write to a file it opened. In Chrome and Edge, Save asks where to save each time. In Firefox and Safari, it downloads the file to your Downloads folder; move it somewhere sensible and open it from there next time.\n\nOpening files\nFile › Open (Ctrl+O) opens a .loom file in a new tab.\nOn Windows and Linux, double-clicking a .loom file opens it in LoomWright.\nFile › Open example project opens the Defender example.\nFiles saved by earlier versions of LoomWright open normally and are brought up to date when you next save them.\n\nWhen the file changes on disk\nThe desktop app checks every second or two whether the open file has been changed by another program, such as the Claude add-on, a Git pull, or a sync service.\nIf you have no unsaved changes, LoomWright reloads the file and says Updated from file. Ctrl+Z undoes the change. Undo takes you back to how it was before the reload.\nIf you do have unsaved changes, it says This file changed on disk and offers Reload. Reloading replaces your unsaved changes with the file; ignoring it keeps your version, and your next save overwrites the file.\nA browser cannot watch files, so this only happens in the desktop app.\n\nWhat LoomWright remembers between sessions\nBesides your project files, LoomWright keeps a few things of its own on this computer:\nWhat Where it is kept\nThe projects you had open, including unsaved changes The app's own storage. They reopen next time you start LoomWright.\nSave points (copies of each project at points in time) The app's own storage. See Save points.\nMy components, My blocks and your saved palette of colours A file called library.json in the desktop app's data folder, or the browser's storage when running in a browser.\nLanguage, theme and similar settings The app's own storage.\nThe projects LoomWright remembers are a safety net in case the computer or the program stops unexpectedly. They are not a substitute for saving: save your work to a .loom file, and keep a copy somewhere else.\n\nWhere the desktop app keeps its data\nSystem Folder\nWindows %APPDATA%\\org.loomwright.app\nMac ~/Library/Application Support/org.loomwright.app\nLinux ~/.local/share/org.loomwright.app\nHelp › About LoomWright shows the exact path of your library file on this computer.\nIn the portable Windows copy, everything is kept in the LoomWright-data folder next to LoomWright.exe instead: the library, the app's own storage and the save points. Copy the whole folder to move LoomWright, with your components and remembered projects, to another computer. See The portable Windows copy.\n\nIn a web browser\nWhen LoomWright runs in a browser, everything it remembers lives in that browser's storage for the page's address. That has some consequences:\nA different browser, or the same browser in a private window, starts empty.\nClearing your browsing data or site data clears your remembered projects, save points and My components.\nExport your components and blocks now and then with Tools › Export my components and blocks, and keep your projects as .loom files.\n\nSharing a project with someone\nSend the .loom file. Custom components used in the project travel inside it, so the other person sees the same symbols. Their colour palette and blocks are their own.\nFor someone without LoomWright, use File › Save read-only web page. It writes a single web page with the drawings and the wire list, which anyone can open in a browser. See Import and export.\nFor the workshop, print a build pack. See Build pack and wire labels.","sections":[{"anchor":"","title":"Files and saving","level":1,"start":0},{"anchor":"project-files","title":"Project files","level":2,"start":140},{"anchor":"saving","title":"Saving","level":2,"start":727},{"anchor":"opening-files","title":"Opening files","level":2,"start":1514},{"anchor":"when-the-file-changes-on-disk","title":"When the file changes on disk","level":2,"start":1826},{"anchor":"what-loomwright-remembers-between-sessions","title":"What LoomWright remembers between sessions","level":2,"start":2467},{"anchor":"where-the-desktop-app-keeps-its-data","title":"Where the desktop app keeps its data","level":2,"start":3276},{"anchor":"in-a-web-browser","title":"In a web browser","level":2,"start":3849},{"anchor":"sharing-a-project-with-someone","title":"Sharing a project with someone","level":2,"start":4323}]},
{"id":"harnesses-connectors-and-ends","file":"07-harnesses-connectors-and-ends.md","title":"Harnesses, connectors and wire ends","html":"<h1 id=\"lm-harnesses-connectors-and-ends\">Harnesses, connectors and wire ends</h1>\n<p>A drawing shows what connects to what. To build the loom you also need to know which bundle each wire runs in, what connector it goes into, and how each end is finished. This chapter covers harnesses, connectors, terminals and multicore cables.</p>\n<h2 id=\"lm-harnesses-connectors-and-ends--harnesses\" data-anchor=\"harnesses\">Harnesses</h2>\n<p>A <strong>harness</strong> is one bundle of wires that is made up on the bench and fitted as a unit, such as the engine bay harness, the dash harness or the rear body harness.</p>\n<p>Give each wire a harness in its Properties, under <strong>Identity</strong>. Pick one from the list or type a new name. LoomWright then:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>makes a separate cut list for each harness,</li>\n<li>lays each harness out on its own formboard (see <a href=\"#lm-harness-formboard\" data-chapter=\"harness-formboard\" data-anchor=\"\">Harness formboard</a>),</li>\n<li>shows in the <strong>Colour usage</strong> list which colours are used for different jobs inside one harness,</li>\n<li>can export one harness at a time for WireViz.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The <strong>Harnesses</strong> section of the Project pane lists every harness with the number of wires in it:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Add harness</strong> adds one. Type its name in the box.</li>\n<li>Edit a name in the list to rename the harness. Every wire in it, and its formboard layout, follow the new name.</li>\n<li>The formboard button beside a harness opens its formboard.</li>\n<li>The remove button deletes the harness from the list. Its wires are left with no harness; they are not deleted.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"lm-harnesses-connectors-and-ends--connectors\" data-anchor=\"connectors\">Connectors</h2>\n<p>The library has three kinds of connector, all under <strong>Wiring &amp; connectors</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Connector (series)</strong> is a real connector from a named range, with the right number of ways, the housing part number and the terminals that go in it. Use this one for anything you will buy.</li>\n<li><strong>Inline connector</strong> draws both halves of a generic connector with numbered cavities, for a quick drawing.</li>\n<li><strong>Connector (one side)</strong> draws one half of a generic connector.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Bulkhead / grommet</strong> is also in that category, for where a harness passes through a panel.</p>\n<h3 id=\"lm-harnesses-connectors-and-ends--choosing-the-series-and-size\" data-anchor=\"choosing-the-series-and-size\">Choosing the series and size</h3>\n<p>Select a <strong>Connector (series)</strong> and set its fields:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Series</strong>: the connector range. See the table below.</li>\n<li><strong>Ways</strong>: how many cavities. LoomWright rounds it to the nearest size the range is made in, such as 2, 3, 4, 6, 8 or 12 for Deutsch DT.</li>\n<li><strong>Side</strong>: <strong>Plug (sockets)</strong> or <strong>Receptacle (pins)</strong>. This decides which terminals the cavities take.</li>\n<li><strong>Housing part no.</strong>: filled in for the ranges where LoomWright knows the number, such as <code>DT06-4S</code>. Type your own to override it.</li>\n<li><strong>Mates with (reference)</strong>: the reference of the other half, such as <code>X2</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Under the fields, a note sums up the series: the current per contact, the wire sizes it takes, whether it is sealed, the housing and wedge lock part numbers and the terminal it uses.</p>\n<div class=\"lm-table\"><table><thead><tr><th>Series</th><th>Ways</th><th>Rating and wire sizes</th><th>Sealed</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Deutsch DT</td><td>2, 3, 4, 6, 8, 12</td><td>13 A per contact, 0.5–2.0 mm²</td><td>Yes</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Deutsch DTM</td><td>2, 3, 4, 6, 8, 12</td><td>7.5 A per contact, 0.35–1.5 mm²</td><td>Yes</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Deutsch DTP</td><td>2, 4</td><td>25 A per contact, 2.0–6.0 mm²</td><td>Yes</td></tr>\n<tr><td>TE Superseal 1.5</td><td>1 to 6</td><td>14 A, 0.5–1.5 mm²</td><td>Yes</td></tr>\n<tr><td>TE Econoseal J Mark II</td><td>1, 2, 3, 4, 6, 8, 10, 12</td><td>20 A, 0.5–2.0 mm²</td><td>Yes</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Aptiv Metri-Pack 150</td><td>1 to 6</td><td>14 A, 0.35–0.8 mm²</td><td>Yes</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Aptiv Metri-Pack 280</td><td>1, 2, 3, 4, 6</td><td>30 A, 0.8–3.0 mm²</td><td>Yes</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Bullet 4.7 mm (classic Land Rover)</td><td>1 to 4</td><td>15 A, 0.5–2.0 mm²</td><td>No</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Spade 6.3 mm (Lucar)</td><td>1 to 4</td><td>20 A</td><td>No</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Anderson SB50</td><td>2</td><td>50 A</td><td>No</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Anderson SB120</td><td>2</td><td>120 A</td><td>No</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Anderson SB175</td><td>2</td><td>175 A</td><td>No</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Anderson Powerpole</td><td>1 to 4</td><td>30 A or 45 A</td><td>No</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Generic connector</td><td>Any</td><td>–</td><td>–</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<blockquote class=\"lm-note\"><p>The part numbers, ratings and wire ranges LoomWright shows are a guide to get you started. Check every part number against the maker&#39;s current catalogue before you order, and use the maker&#39;s ratings, not LoomWright&#39;s.</p></blockquote>\n<h3 id=\"lm-harnesses-connectors-and-ends--mating-pairs\" data-anchor=\"mating-pairs\">Mating pairs</h3>\n<p>Draw both halves of a connection, for example plug <code>X1</code> on the dash side and receptacle <code>X2</code> on the rear body side, and set <strong>Mates with</strong> on one to the reference of the other. LoomWright then joins cavity 1 of one to cavity 1 of the other, cavity 2 to cavity 2, and so on. Circuits trace straight through the pair, and the live test and checks treat it as connected.</p>\n<p>The two halves can be on different sheets.</p>\n<h3 id=\"lm-harnesses-connectors-and-ends--connector-tables\" data-anchor=\"connector-tables\">Connector tables</h3>\n<p>The <strong>Connectors</strong> tab in the Lists pane, and the build pack, list every connector cavity by cavity: the wire in it, its colour code and size, its circuit, where the wire goes, the terminal and the seal.</p>\n<p>If you have not chosen a terminal for a wire end in a series connector, the table shows the terminal the series uses (pins in a receptacle, sockets in a plug), and a wire seal for a sealed series. Empty cavities in a sealed connector need cavity plugs; the build pack counts them.</p>\n<h2 id=\"lm-harnesses-connectors-and-ends--wire-ends\" data-anchor=\"wire-ends\">Wire ends</h2>\n<p>Each wire has two ends. <strong>End A</strong> is where you started drawing it, <strong>End B</strong> where you finished; the heading of each section names the pin, such as <strong>End A: BAT1:+</strong>. <strong>Swap ends</strong> at the bottom of the wire&#39;s Properties exchanges them.</p>\n<p>For each end you can set:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Terminal</strong>: what is crimped or soldered on. The list has bare and tinned ends, bootlace ferrules, soldered joints and butt splices; ring terminals from M4 to M10 in the red, blue and yellow insulated sizes; tube lugs from 10 to 70 mm²; male and female spade terminals (2.8, 4.8 and 6.3 mm); 4.7 mm bullets; and the contacts for the Deutsch, Superseal, Metri-Pack, Econoseal and Anderson ranges.</li>\n<li><strong>Fitting</strong>: <strong>Crimp</strong>, <strong>Crimp and solder</strong>, <strong>Solder</strong>, <strong>Screw terminal</strong> or <strong>Ferrule</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Strip (mm)</strong>: how much insulation to strip.</li>\n<li><strong>Seal</strong>: <strong>Wire seal</strong>, <strong>Cavity plug</strong> or <strong>Gel / grease</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Heat shrink</strong>: free text, such as <code>6 mm adhesive, 25 mm</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Terminals marked <strong>✕ size</strong> in the list are not made for this wire&#39;s size. If you choose one anyway, the design checks point it out. For example, a red ring terminal takes 0.5–1.5 mm², so it is marked on a 2.5 mm² wire.</p>\n<p>The ends appear in the cut list (one column for each end), in the connector tables and in the build pack, so whoever makes the loom knows what goes on each end.</p>\n<h2 id=\"lm-harnesses-connectors-and-ends--multicore-and-screened-cables\" data-anchor=\"multicore-and-screened-cables\">Multicore and screened cables</h2>\n<p>Some wires run together inside one sheath: a 4-core trailer cable, a twin-and-earth feed, or a screened twisted pair for a sensor. LoomWright calls that a <strong>cable</strong>.</p>\n<p>To set one up:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Open <strong>Tools › Cables (multicore, screened)</strong>, or <strong>Cables…</strong> in the Project pane.</li>\n<li><strong>Add cable</strong>, then give it a name (such as <code>C1</code> or <code>Trailer</code>), a <strong>Type</strong> (<strong>Single cores</strong>, <strong>Multicore (sheathed)</strong>, <strong>Twisted pair</strong>, <strong>Screened / shielded</strong> or <strong>Twisted and screened</strong>), the number of <strong>Cores</strong> and a <strong>Part number</strong> if you have one.</li>\n<li>On each wire that is a core of it, choose the cable under <strong>Cable › In cable</strong> in the wire&#39;s Properties.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>A quicker way: select the wires first, then open <strong>Cables</strong> and use <strong>New cable from the selected wires</strong>.</p>\n<p>The dialog shows how many cores each cable has in use and which wires they are. The cable&#39;s name is added to the labels of its wires in square brackets, such as <code>W31 B 0.75mm² [C1]</code>. The cut list, the build pack and the WireViz export show the cable too.</p>","text":"Harnesses, connectors and wire ends\nA drawing shows what connects to what. To build the loom you also need to know which bundle each wire runs in, what connector it goes into, and how each end is finished. This chapter covers harnesses, connectors, terminals and multicore cables.\n\nHarnesses\nA harness is one bundle of wires that is made up on the bench and fitted as a unit, such as the engine bay harness, the dash harness or the rear body harness.\nGive each wire a harness in its Properties, under Identity. Pick one from the list or type a new name. LoomWright then:\nmakes a separate cut list for each harness,\nlays each harness out on its own formboard (see Harness formboard),\nshows in the Colour usage list which colours are used for different jobs inside one harness,\ncan export one harness at a time for WireViz.\nThe Harnesses section of the Project pane lists every harness with the number of wires in it:\nAdd harness adds one. Type its name in the box.\nEdit a name in the list to rename the harness. Every wire in it, and its formboard layout, follow the new name.\nThe formboard button beside a harness opens its formboard.\nThe remove button deletes the harness from the list. Its wires are left with no harness; they are not deleted.\n\nConnectors\nThe library has three kinds of connector, all under Wiring & connectors:\nConnector (series) is a real connector from a named range, with the right number of ways, the housing part number and the terminals that go in it. Use this one for anything you will buy.\nInline connector draws both halves of a generic connector with numbered cavities, for a quick drawing.\nConnector (one side) draws one half of a generic connector.\nBulkhead / grommet is also in that category, for where a harness passes through a panel.\n\nChoosing the series and size\nSelect a Connector (series) and set its fields:\nSeries: the connector range. See the table below.\nWays: how many cavities. LoomWright rounds it to the nearest size the range is made in, such as 2, 3, 4, 6, 8 or 12 for Deutsch DT.\nSide: Plug (sockets) or Receptacle (pins). This decides which terminals the cavities take.\nHousing part no.: filled in for the ranges where LoomWright knows the number, such as DT06-4S. Type your own to override it.\nMates with (reference): the reference of the other half, such as X2.\nUnder the fields, a note sums up the series: the current per contact, the wire sizes it takes, whether it is sealed, the housing and wedge lock part numbers and the terminal it uses.\nSeries Ways Rating and wire sizes Sealed\nDeutsch DT 2, 3, 4, 6, 8, 12 13 A per contact, 0.5–2.0 mm² Yes\nDeutsch DTM 2, 3, 4, 6, 8, 12 7.5 A per contact, 0.35–1.5 mm² Yes\nDeutsch DTP 2, 4 25 A per contact, 2.0–6.0 mm² Yes\nTE Superseal 1.5 1 to 6 14 A, 0.5–1.5 mm² Yes\nTE Econoseal J Mark II 1, 2, 3, 4, 6, 8, 10, 12 20 A, 0.5–2.0 mm² Yes\nAptiv Metri-Pack 150 1 to 6 14 A, 0.35–0.8 mm² Yes\nAptiv Metri-Pack 280 1, 2, 3, 4, 6 30 A, 0.8–3.0 mm² Yes\nBullet 4.7 mm (classic Land Rover) 1 to 4 15 A, 0.5–2.0 mm² No\nSpade 6.3 mm (Lucar) 1 to 4 20 A No\nAnderson SB50 2 50 A No\nAnderson SB120 2 120 A No\nAnderson SB175 2 175 A No\nAnderson Powerpole 1 to 4 30 A or 45 A No\nGeneric connector Any – –\nThe part numbers, ratings and wire ranges LoomWright shows are a guide to get you started. Check every part number against the maker's current catalogue before you order, and use the maker's ratings, not LoomWright's.\n\nMating pairs\nDraw both halves of a connection, for example plug X1 on the dash side and receptacle X2 on the rear body side, and set Mates with on one to the reference of the other. LoomWright then joins cavity 1 of one to cavity 1 of the other, cavity 2 to cavity 2, and so on. Circuits trace straight through the pair, and the live test and checks treat it as connected.\nThe two halves can be on different sheets.\n\nConnector tables\nThe Connectors tab in the Lists pane, and the build pack, list every connector cavity by cavity: the wire in it, its colour code and size, its circuit, where the wire goes, the terminal and the seal.\nIf you have not chosen a terminal for a wire end in a series connector, the table shows the terminal the series uses (pins in a receptacle, sockets in a plug), and a wire seal for a sealed series. Empty cavities in a sealed connector need cavity plugs; the build pack counts them.\n\nWire ends\nEach wire has two ends. End A is where you started drawing it, End B where you finished; the heading of each section names the pin, such as End A: BAT1:+. Swap ends at the bottom of the wire's Properties exchanges them.\nFor each end you can set:\nTerminal: what is crimped or soldered on. The list has bare and tinned ends, bootlace ferrules, soldered joints and butt splices; ring terminals from M4 to M10 in the red, blue and yellow insulated sizes; tube lugs from 10 to 70 mm²; male and female spade terminals (2.8, 4.8 and 6.3 mm); 4.7 mm bullets; and the contacts for the Deutsch, Superseal, Metri-Pack, Econoseal and Anderson ranges.\nFitting: Crimp, Crimp and solder, Solder, Screw terminal or Ferrule.\nStrip (mm): how much insulation to strip.\nSeal: Wire seal, Cavity plug or Gel / grease.\nHeat shrink: free text, such as 6 mm adhesive, 25 mm.\nTerminals marked ✕ size in the list are not made for this wire's size. If you choose one anyway, the design checks point it out. For example, a red ring terminal takes 0.5–1.5 mm², so it is marked on a 2.5 mm² wire.\nThe ends appear in the cut list (one column for each end), in the connector tables and in the build pack, so whoever makes the loom knows what goes on each end.\n\nMulticore and screened cables\nSome wires run together inside one sheath: a 4-core trailer cable, a twin-and-earth feed, or a screened twisted pair for a sensor. LoomWright calls that a cable.\nTo set one up:\nOpen Tools › Cables (multicore, screened), or Cables… in the Project pane.\nAdd cable, then give it a name (such as C1 or Trailer), a Type (Single cores, Multicore (sheathed), Twisted pair, Screened / shielded or Twisted and screened), the number of Cores and a Part number if you have one.\nOn each wire that is a core of it, choose the cable under Cable › In cable in the wire's Properties.\nA quicker way: select the wires first, then open Cables and use New cable from the selected wires.\nThe dialog shows how many cores each cable has in use and which wires they are. The cable's name is added to the labels of its wires in square brackets, such as W31 B 0.75mm² [C1]. The cut list, the build pack and the WireViz export show the cable too.","sections":[{"anchor":"","title":"Harnesses, connectors and wire ends","level":1,"start":0},{"anchor":"harnesses","title":"Harnesses","level":2,"start":282},{"anchor":"connectors","title":"Connectors","level":2,"start":1247},{"anchor":"choosing-the-series-and-size","title":"Choosing the series and size","level":3,"start":1771},{"anchor":"mating-pairs","title":"Mating pairs","level":3,"start":3405},{"anchor":"connector-tables","title":"Connector tables","level":3,"start":3822},{"anchor":"wire-ends","title":"Wire ends","level":2,"start":4321},{"anchor":"multicore-and-screened-cables","title":"Multicore and screened cables","level":2,"start":5559}]},
{"id":"part-data","file":"08-part-data.md","title":"Part data","html":"<h1 id=\"lm-part-data\">Part data</h1>\n<p>Besides its electrical fields, every part can carry the information you need to buy it and find it again on the vehicle: a part number, a supplier, a price and where it is fitted. LoomWright gathers these into the parts list, the build pack and the vehicle locations view.</p>\n<h2 id=\"lm-part-data--entering-part-data\" data-anchor=\"entering-part-data\">Entering part data</h2>\n<p>Select a part. The <strong>Part data</strong> section of the Properties pane has four fields:</p>\n<div class=\"lm-table\"><table><thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>What to put in it</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Part number</td><td>The maker&#39;s or supplier&#39;s part number, such as <code>DT06-2S</code> or a relay&#39;s catalogue number.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Supplier</td><td>Where you buy it.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Unit cost</td><td>The price of one, in the currency set under <strong>Build</strong> in the drawing settings.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Location</td><td>Where it is fitted, such as <code>Engine bay, left</code> or <code>Under seat box</code>.</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Earth symbols, splices, off-sheet references, net labels, text notes and group boxes have no part data, because they are not things you buy. (An earth stud, which you do fit, has.)</p>\n<p>As you type a supplier or a location, LoomWright suggests the ones you have already used in the project.</p>\n<p>For a <strong>Connector (series)</strong>, the housing part number LoomWright suggests is used as the part number unless you type your own.</p>\n<blockquote class=\"lm-note\"><p>Part numbers that LoomWright fills in for you, such as connector housings and terminals, are a starting point. Check them against the maker&#39;s catalogue before ordering.</p></blockquote>\n<h2 id=\"lm-part-data--the-parts-list-and-costs\" data-anchor=\"the-parts-list-and-costs\">The parts list and costs</h2>\n<p>The <strong>Parts &amp; costs</strong> tab in the Lists pane lists everything you need to buy:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Parts are grouped when they are the same component with the same value, part number and supplier: three 10 A blade fuses from one supplier make one line with a quantity of 3.</li>\n<li>A part drawn on several sheets with the same reference, such as a fuse box, is counted once.</li>\n<li>Each line shows the quantity, component, value, part number, supplier, unit price, line total, the references (such as <code>F1, F2</code>) and the locations.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Under the table are the totals for parts, for cable and for both, and how many lines still have no price. <strong>Reports › Export parts and costs (CSV)</strong> saves the list for a spreadsheet.</p>\n<h2 id=\"lm-part-data--cable-prices\" data-anchor=\"cable-prices\">Cable prices</h2>\n<p>Cable is priced by the metre, by size. Open the <strong>Cable totals</strong> tab in the Lists pane. Under the table, <strong>Price per metre</strong> has a box for each size used in the project; type the price you pay.</p>\n<p>The cable totals then show, for each size, colour and insulation:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>how many wires and how many metres the drawing uses,</li>\n<li><strong>Buy (+15%)</strong>: the metres to buy, with 15% added for waste and mistakes,</li>\n<li>the cost of that length.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Missing lengths</strong> counts wires of that kind with no length. Fill those in, or the totals come out short. The cable cost is included in the total on the <strong>Parts &amp; costs</strong> tab.</p>\n<h2 id=\"lm-part-data--the-currency-symbol\" data-anchor=\"the-currency-symbol\">The currency symbol</h2>\n<p>Prices are plain numbers; LoomWright does not convert currencies. The <strong>Currency symbol</strong> under <strong>Build</strong> in the drawing settings (click an empty part of the sheet) is the symbol written in front of them. It starts as £.</p>\n<h2 id=\"lm-part-data--locations\" data-anchor=\"locations\">Locations</h2>\n<p>The <strong>Location</strong> field says where a part lives on the vehicle. It appears in the parts list and the build pack, and you can type it in the vehicle view as well, where you can also mark each part on a picture of the vehicle. See <a href=\"#lm-vehicle-locations\" data-chapter=\"vehicle-locations\" data-anchor=\"\">Vehicle locations view</a>.</p>\n<p>Using the same wording for the same place, such as always <code>Under seat box</code>, keeps the parts list tidy.</p>\n<p>Location is also one of the things <strong>Edit › Find</strong> can search.</p>","text":"Part data\nBesides its electrical fields, every part can carry the information you need to buy it and find it again on the vehicle: a part number, a supplier, a price and where it is fitted. LoomWright gathers these into the parts list, the build pack and the vehicle locations view.\n\nEntering part data\nSelect a part. The Part data section of the Properties pane has four fields:\nField What to put in it\nPart number The maker's or supplier's part number, such as DT06-2S or a relay's catalogue number.\nSupplier Where you buy it.\nUnit cost The price of one, in the currency set under Build in the drawing settings.\nLocation Where it is fitted, such as Engine bay, left or Under seat box.\nEarth symbols, splices, off-sheet references, net labels, text notes and group boxes have no part data, because they are not things you buy. (An earth stud, which you do fit, has.)\nAs you type a supplier or a location, LoomWright suggests the ones you have already used in the project.\nFor a Connector (series), the housing part number LoomWright suggests is used as the part number unless you type your own.\nPart numbers that LoomWright fills in for you, such as connector housings and terminals, are a starting point. Check them against the maker's catalogue before ordering.\n\nThe parts list and costs\nThe Parts & costs tab in the Lists pane lists everything you need to buy:\nParts are grouped when they are the same component with the same value, part number and supplier: three 10 A blade fuses from one supplier make one line with a quantity of 3.\nA part drawn on several sheets with the same reference, such as a fuse box, is counted once.\nEach line shows the quantity, component, value, part number, supplier, unit price, line total, the references (such as F1, F2) and the locations.\nUnder the table are the totals for parts, for cable and for both, and how many lines still have no price. Reports › Export parts and costs (CSV) saves the list for a spreadsheet.\n\nCable prices\nCable is priced by the metre, by size. Open the Cable totals tab in the Lists pane. Under the table, Price per metre has a box for each size used in the project; type the price you pay.\nThe cable totals then show, for each size, colour and insulation:\nhow many wires and how many metres the drawing uses,\nBuy (+15%): the metres to buy, with 15% added for waste and mistakes,\nthe cost of that length.\nMissing lengths counts wires of that kind with no length. Fill those in, or the totals come out short. The cable cost is included in the total on the Parts & costs tab.\n\nThe currency symbol\nPrices are plain numbers; LoomWright does not convert currencies. The Currency symbol under Build in the drawing settings (click an empty part of the sheet) is the symbol written in front of them. It starts as £.\n\nLocations\nThe Location field says where a part lives on the vehicle. It appears in the parts list and the build pack, and you can type it in the vehicle view as well, where you can also mark each part on a picture of the vehicle. See Vehicle locations view.\nUsing the same wording for the same place, such as always Under seat box, keeps the parts list tidy.\nLocation is also one of the things Edit › Find can search.","sections":[{"anchor":"","title":"Part data","level":1,"start":0},{"anchor":"entering-part-data","title":"Entering part data","level":2,"start":284},{"anchor":"the-parts-list-and-costs","title":"The parts list and costs","level":2,"start":1266},{"anchor":"cable-prices","title":"Cable prices","level":2,"start":1959},{"anchor":"the-currency-symbol","title":"The currency symbol","level":2,"start":2542},{"anchor":"locations","title":"Locations","level":2,"start":2776}]},
{"id":"component-builder","file":"09-component-builder.md","title":"Component builder and My components","html":"<h1 id=\"lm-component-builder\">Component builder and My components</h1>\n<p>The library covers the common parts of a vehicle loom, but sooner or later you need something it does not have: a particular switch panel, a controller with its own pinout, a sensor. The component builder lets you draw your own symbol, give it pins and fields, and save it to <strong>My components</strong> so you can use it in any project.</p>\n<p>The builder&#39;s own screen is in English only, whatever language the rest of LoomWright is set to.</p>\n<h2 id=\"lm-component-builder--opening-the-builder\" data-anchor=\"opening-the-builder\">Opening the builder</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Tools › Component builder</strong>, or the <strong>Builder</strong> button on the tool rail, starts a new component.</li>\n<li><strong>New</strong> under <strong>My components</strong> in the Parts pane does the same.</li>\n<li>To change a component you made earlier, click <strong>Edit</strong> on it under <strong>My components</strong>, or select one on the sheet and click <strong>Edit component design</strong> in its Properties.</li>\n</ul>\n<figure class=\"lm-fig\"><img src=\"manual/img/component-builder.png\" alt=\"The component builder\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>The component builder: drawing tools on the left, the symbol in the middle, and the component&#39;s details, pins and shapes on the right.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>The window has three parts: the tools on the left, the symbol being drawn in the middle, and the component&#39;s details on the right. The dashed box in the middle is the component&#39;s outline, with its size written above it.</p>\n<h2 id=\"lm-component-builder--starting-from-a-library-symbol\" data-anchor=\"starting-from-a-library-symbol\">Starting from a library symbol</h2>\n<p>Often the quickest start is a library part that is nearly right. Choose it under <strong>Start from a library symbol</strong>. The builder copies its shapes, pins and fields, and you change what you need. The library part itself is not changed.</p>\n<p><strong>Clear drawing</strong> removes every shape and pin, to start from nothing.</p>\n<h2 id=\"lm-component-builder--drawing-the-symbol\" data-anchor=\"drawing-the-symbol\">Drawing the symbol</h2>\n<p>Pick a tool, then draw in the middle:</p>\n<div class=\"lm-table\"><table><thead><tr><th>Tool</th><th>Key</th><th>How to use it</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Select and move</td><td><kbd>V</kbd></td><td>Click a shape or pin to select it; drag it to move it.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Line</td><td><kbd>L</kbd></td><td>Drag from one end to the other.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Rectangle</td><td><kbd>B</kbd></td><td>Drag from one corner to the opposite corner.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Circle</td><td><kbd>C</kbd></td><td>Drag from the centre outwards.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Text</td><td><kbd>T</kbd></td><td>Click where the text goes, then type it in the panel on the right.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Pin (connection point)</td><td><kbd>P</kbd></td><td>Click on the edge of the symbol where a wire should attach.</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Shapes snap to a 5-unit grid. Pins snap to the 10-unit grid of the drawing, so wires line up with them, and stay inside the outline.</li>\n<li><kbd>Esc</kbd> goes back to <strong>Select and move</strong>.</li>\n<li><kbd>Delete</kbd> removes the selected shape or pin.</li>\n<li><kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Z</kbd>, or <strong>Undo</strong>, undoes the last change in the builder.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Select a shape to change it exactly on the right: its position and size, line width, whether it is dashed, its fill (none, white or black), and for text its words, size and alignment. <strong>Duplicate</strong> copies it and <strong>Delete shape</strong> removes it.</p>\n<h2 id=\"lm-component-builder--pins\" data-anchor=\"pins\">Pins</h2>\n<p>Every pin needs an id that is different from the others on the component. The builder numbers them 1, 2, 3… as you place them. Select a pin to change its <strong>Pin number / id</strong>, such as <code>30</code>, <code>87</code> or <code>GND</code>, and its <strong>Description</strong>, such as <code>Switched feed</code>. Both appear in the Properties pane when the component is used, and the id is what the wire list shows, as in <code>K5:87</code>.</p>\n<p>Choose ids with care: LoomWright treats pins with ids such as <code>-</code>, <code>31</code>, <code>E</code>, <code>GND</code> or <code>85</code> as earth pins when it checks circuits for an earth return.</p>\n<p>The <strong>Pins</strong> list on the right shows them all; click one to select it.</p>\n<h2 id=\"lm-component-builder--the-components-details\" data-anchor=\"the-components-details\">The component&#39;s details</h2>\n<p>At the top of the right-hand panel:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Name</strong>: as it appears in the Parts pane and the parts list.</li>\n<li><strong>Reference prefix</strong>: the letter for its references, such as <code>K</code> for a relay or <code>S</code> for a switch. New parts get the next free number after it.</li>\n<li><strong>Text under symbol</strong>: written under the symbol on the drawing. Put a field name in braces to show the field&#39;s value, such as <code>{rating}</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Width</strong> and <strong>Height</strong>: the size of the outline, in steps of 20.</li>\n<li><strong>Fields, one per line as key = default</strong>: the values each use of the component can have, such as <code>rating = 15A</code> or <code>legend = WORK LAMP</code>. Each field appears in the Properties pane when the part is placed.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Text shapes can show fields too: a text of <code>{legend}</code> shows each part&#39;s own legend.</p>\n<p>The <strong>Preview</strong> shows the component as it will look on a sheet.</p>\n<h2 id=\"lm-component-builder--saving\" data-anchor=\"saving\">Saving</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Save to my components</strong> saves the component and closes the builder.</li>\n<li><strong>Save and place</strong> saves it and drops one onto the current sheet.</li>\n<li><strong>Cancel</strong> closes the builder without saving.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A component needs at least one shape, and its pins need different ids, before it can be saved.</p>\n<p>Saved components appear in the Parts pane under <strong>My components</strong>, and can be used in any project on this computer. When you place one, LoomWright also stores a copy of it in the project file, so the project still opens correctly on a computer that does not have it. If you change a component&#39;s design, projects that use it pick up the new design when they are open.</p>\n<h2 id=\"lm-component-builder--deleting-a-component\" data-anchor=\"deleting-a-component\">Deleting a component</h2>\n<p>Open the component in the builder and click <strong>Delete from library</strong>, then click again to confirm. It is removed from <strong>My components</strong>. Projects that already use it keep their own copy, so their drawings do not change.</p>\n<h2 id=\"lm-component-builder--sharing-components-and-blocks\" data-anchor=\"sharing-components-and-blocks\">Sharing components and blocks</h2>\n<p><strong>Tools › Export my components and blocks</strong> saves all your components, your blocks and your saved palette of colours in one file, <code>loomwright-library.json</code>. Use it to:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>back up your library,</li>\n<li>move it to another computer,</li>\n<li>share it with someone else.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Tools › Import components and blocks</strong> reads such a file and adds its contents to your library. A component or block with the same id as one you have already is replaced by the one in the file.</p>\n<h2 id=\"lm-component-builder--components-from-a-picture-with-claude\" data-anchor=\"components-from-a-picture-with-claude\">Components from a picture, with Claude</h2>\n<p>With the optional Claude add-on, Claude can make a component for you from a photo or a datasheet picture of a part&#39;s pinout. It appears under <strong>My components</strong> the next time you switch back to LoomWright. Check the pins against the part before you rely on it. See <a href=\"#lm-claude-add-on\" data-chapter=\"claude-add-on\" data-anchor=\"\">The Claude add-on</a>.</p>","text":"Component builder and My components\nThe library covers the common parts of a vehicle loom, but sooner or later you need something it does not have: a particular switch panel, a controller with its own pinout, a sensor. The component builder lets you draw your own symbol, give it pins and fields, and save it to My components so you can use it in any project.\nThe builder's own screen is in English only, whatever language the rest of LoomWright is set to.\n\nOpening the builder\nTools › Component builder, or the Builder button on the tool rail, starts a new component.\nNew under My components in the Parts pane does the same.\nTo change a component you made earlier, click Edit on it under My components, or select one on the sheet and click Edit component design in its Properties.\nThe component builder: drawing tools on the left, the symbol in the middle, and the component's details, pins and shapes on the right.\nThe window has three parts: the tools on the left, the symbol being drawn in the middle, and the component's details on the right. The dashed box in the middle is the component's outline, with its size written above it.\n\nStarting from a library symbol\nOften the quickest start is a library part that is nearly right. Choose it under Start from a library symbol. The builder copies its shapes, pins and fields, and you change what you need. The library part itself is not changed.\nClear drawing removes every shape and pin, to start from nothing.\n\nDrawing the symbol\nPick a tool, then draw in the middle:\nTool Key How to use it\nSelect and move V Click a shape or pin to select it; drag it to move it.\nLine L Drag from one end to the other.\nRectangle B Drag from one corner to the opposite corner.\nCircle C Drag from the centre outwards.\nText T Click where the text goes, then type it in the panel on the right.\nPin (connection point) P Click on the edge of the symbol where a wire should attach.\nShapes snap to a 5-unit grid. Pins snap to the 10-unit grid of the drawing, so wires line up with them, and stay inside the outline.\nEsc goes back to Select and move.\nDelete removes the selected shape or pin.\nCtrl+Z, or Undo, undoes the last change in the builder.\nSelect a shape to change it exactly on the right: its position and size, line width, whether it is dashed, its fill (none, white or black), and for text its words, size and alignment. Duplicate copies it and Delete shape removes it.\n\nPins\nEvery pin needs an id that is different from the others on the component. The builder numbers them 1, 2, 3… as you place them. Select a pin to change its Pin number / id, such as 30, 87 or GND, and its Description, such as Switched feed. Both appear in the Properties pane when the component is used, and the id is what the wire list shows, as in K5:87.\nChoose ids with care: LoomWright treats pins with ids such as -, 31, E, GND or 85 as earth pins when it checks circuits for an earth return.\nThe Pins list on the right shows them all; click one to select it.\n\nThe component's details\nAt the top of the right-hand panel:\nName: as it appears in the Parts pane and the parts list.\nReference prefix: the letter for its references, such as K for a relay or S for a switch. New parts get the next free number after it.\nText under symbol: written under the symbol on the drawing. Put a field name in braces to show the field's value, such as {rating}.\nWidth and Height: the size of the outline, in steps of 20.\nFields, one per line as key = default: the values each use of the component can have, such as rating = 15A or legend = WORK LAMP. Each field appears in the Properties pane when the part is placed.\nText shapes can show fields too: a text of {legend} shows each part's own legend.\nThe Preview shows the component as it will look on a sheet.\n\nSaving\nSave to my components saves the component and closes the builder.\nSave and place saves it and drops one onto the current sheet.\nCancel closes the builder without saving.\nA component needs at least one shape, and its pins need different ids, before it can be saved.\nSaved components appear in the Parts pane under My components, and can be used in any project on this computer. When you place one, LoomWright also stores a copy of it in the project file, so the project still opens correctly on a computer that does not have it. If you change a component's design, projects that use it pick up the new design when they are open.\n\nDeleting a component\nOpen the component in the builder and click Delete from library, then click again to confirm. It is removed from My components. Projects that already use it keep their own copy, so their drawings do not change.\n\nSharing components and blocks\nTools › Export my components and blocks saves all your components, your blocks and your saved palette of colours in one file, loomwright-library.json. Use it to:\nback up your library,\nmove it to another computer,\nshare it with someone else.\nTools › Import components and blocks reads such a file and adds its contents to your library. A component or block with the same id as one you have already is replaced by the one in the file.\n\nComponents from a picture, with Claude\nWith the optional Claude add-on, Claude can make a component for you from a photo or a datasheet picture of a part's pinout. It appears under My components the next time you switch back to LoomWright. Check the pins against the part before you rely on it. See The Claude add-on.","sections":[{"anchor":"","title":"Component builder and My components","level":1,"start":0},{"anchor":"opening-the-builder","title":"Opening the builder","level":2,"start":458},{"anchor":"starting-from-a-library-symbol","title":"Starting from a library symbol","level":2,"start":1138},{"anchor":"drawing-the-symbol","title":"Drawing the symbol","level":2,"start":1464},{"anchor":"pins","title":"Pins","level":2,"start":2411},{"anchor":"the-components-details","title":"The component's details","level":2,"start":2979},{"anchor":"saving","title":"Saving","level":2,"start":3763},{"anchor":"deleting-a-component","title":"Deleting a component","level":2,"start":4399},{"anchor":"sharing-components-and-blocks","title":"Sharing components and blocks","level":2,"start":4632},{"anchor":"components-from-a-picture-with-claude","title":"Components from a picture, with Claude","level":2,"start":5096}]},
{"id":"design-checks","file":"10-design-checks.md","title":"Design checks","html":"<h1 id=\"lm-design-checks\">Design checks</h1>\n<p>As you draw, LoomWright checks the project for common mistakes: a fuse too big for the wire it protects, a wire carrying more than its size suits, a load with no earth, a supply wired straight to earth. The checks run all the time and cover every sheet.</p>\n<blockquote class=\"lm-note\"><p>The checks are rules of thumb. They catch slips, but they cannot prove a design is safe. Wire sizes and fuse ratings in particular depend on the cable, how it is bundled and routed, how hot it gets and how long the run is. Always check your cable maker&#39;s and fuse maker&#39;s data, and fuse every circuit to protect its wire. See the <a href=\"#lm-disclaimer\" data-chapter=\"disclaimer\" data-anchor=\"\">Disclaimer</a>.</p></blockquote>\n<h2 id=\"lm-design-checks--where-to-see-them\" data-anchor=\"where-to-see-them\">Where to see them</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>The <strong>status bar</strong> says <strong>Checks passed</strong>, or how many checks need a look. Click it to open the list.</li>\n<li>The <strong>Checks</strong> tab in the Lists pane lists every finding, with its level.</li>\n<li><strong>Tools › Run design checks</strong>, or the <strong>Check</strong> button on the tool rail, opens the list and says how many there are.</li>\n<li>With nothing selected, the Properties pane says how many findings are on the sheet you are looking at.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Click a finding in the list to go to the wire or part it is about.</p>\n<h2 id=\"lm-design-checks--levels\" data-anchor=\"levels\">Levels</h2>\n<div class=\"lm-table\"><table><thead><tr><th>Level</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>error</td><td>Something is wrong and will not work as drawn, or is dangerous.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>check</td><td>Probably a mistake, or a figure outside the usual guide. Look at it and decide.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>info</td><td>Worth knowing, but often deliberate. Not counted in the status bar.</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"lm-design-checks--what-is-checked\" data-anchor=\"what-is-checked\">What is checked</h2>\n<h3 id=\"lm-design-checks--errors\" data-anchor=\"errors\">Errors</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A wire connected to a pin that no longer exists</strong>, for example after a connector was made smaller or a custom component lost a pin.</li>\n<li><strong>A wire that starts and ends on the same pin.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>A component type missing from the library</strong>, usually a custom component from another computer that the project file did not carry.</li>\n<li><strong>A reference used twice on one sheet.</strong> Give each part its own reference. (The same reference on different sheets means the same part drawn twice; see <a href=\"#lm-projects-and-sheets--the-same-part-on-several-sheets\" data-chapter=\"projects-and-sheets\" data-anchor=\"the-same-part-on-several-sheets\">The same part on several sheets</a>.)</li>\n<li><strong>A supply connected straight to earth</strong>: a battery&#39;s positive, or another supply, with nothing between it and earth. That is a short circuit.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"lm-design-checks--checks\" data-anchor=\"checks\">Checks</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Voltage drop over 3%</strong> of the system voltage on a wire, from its length, size and current. Consider a heavier size.</li>\n<li><strong>Current above the guide figure</strong> for the wire&#39;s size. See <a href=\"#lm-wires-and-colours--wire-size\" data-chapter=\"wires-and-colours\" data-anchor=\"wire-size\">Wire size</a> for the guide figures.</li>\n<li><strong>A fuse bigger than the guide figure for a wire it feeds.</strong> A fuse protects the wire after it; if the fuse is larger than the wire can carry, the wire can overheat before the fuse blows.</li>\n<li><strong>A fuse or relay with more load than its rating</strong>, from the fuse schedule. See <a href=\"#lm-fuses-and-loads\" data-chapter=\"fuses-and-loads\" data-anchor=\"\">Fuses and loads</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>A load fed from a supply with no fuse in between.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>A load with no earth return.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>A wire size outside what its terminal takes</strong>, for example 2.5 mm² wire in a terminal for 0.5–1.5 mm².</li>\n<li><strong>The same reference used for different kinds of part</strong> on different sheets, such as <code>F1</code> as a fuse on one sheet and a relay on another.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"lm-design-checks--information\" data-anchor=\"information\">Information</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A part with no wires connected.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>A pin left unconnected</strong> on a part that normally has every pin used. Parts that often have spare pins, such as fuse boxes, connectors, busbars, terminal strips, modules, ICs, rotary and 8-pin rocker switches and DC-DC chargers, are not reported pin by pin.</li>\n<li><strong>A circuit using more than one colour.</strong> That is fine across a connector, where the colour may change; otherwise it is usual to keep one colour per circuit.</li>\n<li><strong>A colour used for different circuits in one harness.</strong> Tell the wires apart by wire number or a sleeve.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"lm-design-checks--things-the-checks-cannot-know\" data-anchor=\"things-the-checks-cannot-know\">Things the checks cannot know</h2>\n<p>The checks only know what is on the drawing. They do not know:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>the real current of a load unless you give it a current or wattage,</li>\n<li>the length of a wire unless you fill it in,</li>\n<li>how tightly a loom is bundled, how hot it runs, or the cable&#39;s real rating,</li>\n<li>whether a part is fitted the right way round, or a terminal crimped well.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A project with no findings is a good sign, not a guarantee. Test the finished loom with a meter, a fused supply and care before you connect it to a battery.</p>","text":"Design checks\nAs you draw, LoomWright checks the project for common mistakes: a fuse too big for the wire it protects, a wire carrying more than its size suits, a load with no earth, a supply wired straight to earth. The checks run all the time and cover every sheet.\nThe checks are rules of thumb. They catch slips, but they cannot prove a design is safe. Wire sizes and fuse ratings in particular depend on the cable, how it is bundled and routed, how hot it gets and how long the run is. Always check your cable maker's and fuse maker's data, and fuse every circuit to protect its wire. See the Disclaimer.\n\nWhere to see them\nThe status bar says Checks passed, or how many checks need a look. Click it to open the list.\nThe Checks tab in the Lists pane lists every finding, with its level.\nTools › Run design checks, or the Check button on the tool rail, opens the list and says how many there are.\nWith nothing selected, the Properties pane says how many findings are on the sheet you are looking at.\nClick a finding in the list to go to the wire or part it is about.\n\nLevels\nLevel Meaning\nerror Something is wrong and will not work as drawn, or is dangerous.\ncheck Probably a mistake, or a figure outside the usual guide. Look at it and decide.\ninfo Worth knowing, but often deliberate. Not counted in the status bar.\n\nWhat is checked\n\nErrors\nA wire connected to a pin that no longer exists, for example after a connector was made smaller or a custom component lost a pin.\nA wire that starts and ends on the same pin.\nA component type missing from the library, usually a custom component from another computer that the project file did not carry.\nA reference used twice on one sheet. Give each part its own reference. (The same reference on different sheets means the same part drawn twice; see The same part on several sheets.)\nA supply connected straight to earth: a battery's positive, or another supply, with nothing between it and earth. That is a short circuit.\n\nChecks\nVoltage drop over 3% of the system voltage on a wire, from its length, size and current. Consider a heavier size.\nCurrent above the guide figure for the wire's size. See Wire size for the guide figures.\nA fuse bigger than the guide figure for a wire it feeds. A fuse protects the wire after it; if the fuse is larger than the wire can carry, the wire can overheat before the fuse blows.\nA fuse or relay with more load than its rating, from the fuse schedule. See Fuses and loads.\nA load fed from a supply with no fuse in between.\nA load with no earth return.\nA wire size outside what its terminal takes, for example 2.5 mm² wire in a terminal for 0.5–1.5 mm².\nThe same reference used for different kinds of part on different sheets, such as F1 as a fuse on one sheet and a relay on another.\n\nInformation\nA part with no wires connected.\nA pin left unconnected on a part that normally has every pin used. Parts that often have spare pins, such as fuse boxes, connectors, busbars, terminal strips, modules, ICs, rotary and 8-pin rocker switches and DC-DC chargers, are not reported pin by pin.\nA circuit using more than one colour. That is fine across a connector, where the colour may change; otherwise it is usual to keep one colour per circuit.\nA colour used for different circuits in one harness. Tell the wires apart by wire number or a sleeve.\n\nThings the checks cannot know\nThe checks only know what is on the drawing. They do not know:\nthe real current of a load unless you give it a current or wattage,\nthe length of a wire unless you fill it in,\nhow tightly a loom is bundled, how hot it runs, or the cable's real rating,\nwhether a part is fitted the right way round, or a terminal crimped well.\nA project with no findings is a good sign, not a guarantee. Test the finished loom with a meter, a fused supply and care before you connect it to a battery.","sections":[{"anchor":"","title":"Design checks","level":1,"start":0},{"anchor":"where-to-see-them","title":"Where to see them","level":2,"start":611},{"anchor":"levels","title":"Levels","level":2,"start":1073},{"anchor":"what-is-checked","title":"What is checked","level":2,"start":1324},{"anchor":"errors","title":"Errors","level":3,"start":1341},{"anchor":"checks","title":"Checks","level":3,"start":1974},{"anchor":"information","title":"Information","level":3,"start":2773},{"anchor":"things-the-checks-cannot-know","title":"Things the checks cannot know","level":2,"start":3329}]},
{"id":"trace-and-live-test","file":"11-trace-and-live-test.md","title":"Circuit trace and live test","html":"<h1 id=\"lm-trace-and-live-test\">Circuit trace and live test</h1>\n<p>Two tools help you follow a circuit through a drawing: the <strong>trace</strong>, which lights up everything connected to one wire, and the <strong>live test</strong>, which lets you operate switches and fuses to see what would run.</p>\n<h2 id=\"lm-trace-and-live-test--tracing-a-circuit\" data-anchor=\"tracing-a-circuit\">Tracing a circuit</h2>\n<p>A trace highlights one circuit: every wire joined to the one you pick, on every sheet.</p>\n<p>To start a trace, do any of these:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>hold <kbd>Alt</kbd> and click a wire or a pin,</li>\n<li>select a wire and press <kbd>T</kbd>, or click <strong>Trace</strong> on the tool rail,</li>\n<li>click <strong>Trace circuit</strong> in a wire&#39;s Properties,</li>\n<li>click the <strong>⤳</strong> beside a pin in a part&#39;s Properties,</li>\n<li>click a row in the <strong>Circuits</strong> list.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>While a trace is on:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>the traced wires are highlighted, and the sheet tabs mark the other sheets the circuit reaches,</li>\n<li>the Properties pane shows the circuit&#39;s name, how many wires and connections it has and on how many sheets, and lists its wires sheet by sheet. Click a wire in the list to go to it,</li>\n<li>the status bar says <strong>Tracing a circuit across all sheets. Esc to clear.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Press <kbd>Esc</kbd> to clear the trace.</p>\n<h3 id=\"lm-trace-and-live-test--what-counts-as-one-circuit\" data-anchor=\"what-counts-as-one-circuit\">What counts as one circuit</h3>\n<p>A circuit, for the trace, is everything joined by wire with nothing in between that can break it. The trace follows wires through:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>splices, busbars, terminal strips and bulkhead grommets,</li>\n<li>mated connector pairs, cavity to cavity,</li>\n<li>off-sheet references and net labels with the same name, to other sheets,</li>\n<li>the same part drawn on several sheets.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>It stops at anything that can switch or break the circuit, or that uses the power: switches, fuses, relays and loads. So tracing the feed to a lamp shows the wires from the switch to the lamp, not the battery side of the fuse. Trace the other side separately.</p>\n<p>All earth points belong to one circuit, called <strong>EARTH</strong>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"lm-trace-and-live-test--the-circuits-name\" data-anchor=\"the-circuits-name\">The circuit&#39;s name</h3>\n<p>LoomWright names a traced circuit from, in order of preference: the name of a net label or off-sheet link on it; the circuit names you gave its wires; a supply it starts from (such as <code>BAT1 +</code>). Otherwise it is numbered.</p>\n<h2 id=\"lm-trace-and-live-test--the-circuits-list\" data-anchor=\"the-circuits-list\">The Circuits list</h2>\n<p>The <strong>Circuits</strong> tab in the Lists pane lists every circuit in the project: its name, how many wires and connections it has, which sheets it is on, and the pins it goes to. Click a row to trace it.</p>\n<p>This list is a good way to check a drawing: a circuit you expected to be one piece that shows up as two usually means a missing wire or a mistyped link name.</p>\n<h2 id=\"lm-trace-and-live-test--the-live-test\" data-anchor=\"the-live-test\">The live test</h2>\n<p>The live test treats every battery as live and lets you operate the switches and fuses, to see which wires would carry power and which loads would run.</p>\n<p>Start it with <strong>Tools › Live circuit test</strong>, or the <strong>Test</strong> button on the tool rail. The sheet gets a green border, the status bar shows <strong>LIVE TEST</strong>, and you cannot edit the drawing until you stop.</p>\n<figure class=\"lm-fig\"><img src=\"manual/img/live-test.png\" alt=\"The live test on the example project\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>The live test: switch S1 is on, so relay K1 has pulled in and the light bar H1 is lit. Powered wires glow.</figcaption></figure>\n<h3 id=\"lm-trace-and-live-test--operating-parts\" data-anchor=\"operating-parts\">Operating parts</h3>\n<p>Click a part on the sheet to move it to its next position, or choose its position under <strong>Controls</strong> in the Properties pane. The parts you can operate, and their positions, are:</p>\n<div class=\"lm-table\"><table><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Positions</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Blade fuse, Maxi / MIDI / ANL fuse, fusible link, circuit breaker</td><td>OK, Blown</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Switch, push button (normally open), pressure and temperature switches</td><td>Open, Closed</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Push button (normally closed)</td><td>Closed, Pressed (open)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Changeover switch</td><td>Position 1, Position 2</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Double-pole changeover</td><td>Position A, Position B</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Illuminated rocker switch</td><td>Off, On</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Rocker switch, 8 pin (Contura style)</td><td>Down, Off, Up</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Rotary switch</td><td>Off, then each position</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Ignition switch</td><td>0 Off, I Accessory, II Ignition, III Start</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Battery isolator</td><td>Off, On (starts On)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Door / pin switch</td><td>Door shut, Door open</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Split charge relay (VSR)</td><td>Open, Combined</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Flasher unit</td><td>Off, Flashing (on) (starts on)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Winch control box</td><td>Stop, Wind in, Wind out</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Alternator</td><td>Engine off, Engine running</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Solar panel</td><td>Dark, Sun</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Relays are not operated by hand.</strong> A relay pulls in by itself when its coil has a feed on one side and an earth on the other, as a real one would. To test a relay circuit, operate the switch that feeds its coil.</p>\n<p>Fuse boxes always pass power to every way; to test a blown fuse, use separate fuse parts.</p>\n<h3 id=\"lm-trace-and-live-test--what-you-see\" data-anchor=\"what-you-see\">What you see</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Powered wires glow</strong>, and loads that have both a live feed and an earth are highlighted as working.</li>\n<li><strong>Working now</strong>, in the Properties pane, counts the loads on, the relays pulled in and the live wires, and names the parts that are working.</li>\n<li>If a position connects a supply straight to earth, a <strong>Short circuit</strong> warning names the supply.</li>\n<li>Switches show their position under them on the sheet.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Reset all</strong> puts every part back to its starting position. <strong>Stop test</strong>, or <kbd>Esc</kbd>, ends the test.</p>\n<blockquote class=\"lm-note\"><p>The live test follows connections only. It does not model voltage, current, voltage drop or timing, so it cannot tell you that a wire is too thin, a fuse too small, or a relay too slow. It answers one question: with the switches set like this, is there a path from a supply, through this load, to earth?</p></blockquote>\n<h2 id=\"lm-trace-and-live-test--using-them-together\" data-anchor=\"using-them-together\">Using them together</h2>\n<p>A good way to check a new circuit:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Trace each wire you have added, to make sure it joins what you meant and nothing else.</li>\n<li>Run the live test, operate the switch for the circuit, and check the right load comes on and nothing else does.</li>\n<li>Blow its fuse, and check the load goes off.</li>\n<li>Open the <strong>Checks</strong> list and deal with anything new.</li>\n</ol>","text":"Circuit trace and live test\nTwo tools help you follow a circuit through a drawing: the trace, which lights up everything connected to one wire, and the live test, which lets you operate switches and fuses to see what would run.\n\nTracing a circuit\nA trace highlights one circuit: every wire joined to the one you pick, on every sheet.\nTo start a trace, do any of these:\nhold Alt and click a wire or a pin,\nselect a wire and press T, or click Trace on the tool rail,\nclick Trace circuit in a wire's Properties,\nclick the ⤳ beside a pin in a part's Properties,\nclick a row in the Circuits list.\nWhile a trace is on:\nthe traced wires are highlighted, and the sheet tabs mark the other sheets the circuit reaches,\nthe Properties pane shows the circuit's name, how many wires and connections it has and on how many sheets, and lists its wires sheet by sheet. Click a wire in the list to go to it,\nthe status bar says Tracing a circuit across all sheets. Esc to clear.\nPress Esc to clear the trace.\n\nWhat counts as one circuit\nA circuit, for the trace, is everything joined by wire with nothing in between that can break it. The trace follows wires through:\nsplices, busbars, terminal strips and bulkhead grommets,\nmated connector pairs, cavity to cavity,\noff-sheet references and net labels with the same name, to other sheets,\nthe same part drawn on several sheets.\nIt stops at anything that can switch or break the circuit, or that uses the power: switches, fuses, relays and loads. So tracing the feed to a lamp shows the wires from the switch to the lamp, not the battery side of the fuse. Trace the other side separately.\nAll earth points belong to one circuit, called EARTH.\n\nThe circuit's name\nLoomWright names a traced circuit from, in order of preference: the name of a net label or off-sheet link on it; the circuit names you gave its wires; a supply it starts from (such as BAT1 +). Otherwise it is numbered.\n\nThe Circuits list\nThe Circuits tab in the Lists pane lists every circuit in the project: its name, how many wires and connections it has, which sheets it is on, and the pins it goes to. Click a row to trace it.\nThis list is a good way to check a drawing: a circuit you expected to be one piece that shows up as two usually means a missing wire or a mistyped link name.\n\nThe live test\nThe live test treats every battery as live and lets you operate the switches and fuses, to see which wires would carry power and which loads would run.\nStart it with Tools › Live circuit test, or the Test button on the tool rail. The sheet gets a green border, the status bar shows LIVE TEST, and you cannot edit the drawing until you stop.\nThe live test: switch S1 is on, so relay K1 has pulled in and the light bar H1 is lit. Powered wires glow.\n\nOperating parts\nClick a part on the sheet to move it to its next position, or choose its position under Controls in the Properties pane. The parts you can operate, and their positions, are:\nPart Positions\nBlade fuse, Maxi / MIDI / ANL fuse, fusible link, circuit breaker OK, Blown\nSwitch, push button (normally open), pressure and temperature switches Open, Closed\nPush button (normally closed) Closed, Pressed (open)\nChangeover switch Position 1, Position 2\nDouble-pole changeover Position A, Position B\nIlluminated rocker switch Off, On\nRocker switch, 8 pin (Contura style) Down, Off, Up\nRotary switch Off, then each position\nIgnition switch 0 Off, I Accessory, II Ignition, III Start\nBattery isolator Off, On (starts On)\nDoor / pin switch Door shut, Door open\nSplit charge relay (VSR) Open, Combined\nFlasher unit Off, Flashing (on) (starts on)\nWinch control box Stop, Wind in, Wind out\nAlternator Engine off, Engine running\nSolar panel Dark, Sun\nRelays are not operated by hand. A relay pulls in by itself when its coil has a feed on one side and an earth on the other, as a real one would. To test a relay circuit, operate the switch that feeds its coil.\nFuse boxes always pass power to every way; to test a blown fuse, use separate fuse parts.\n\nWhat you see\nPowered wires glow, and loads that have both a live feed and an earth are highlighted as working.\nWorking now, in the Properties pane, counts the loads on, the relays pulled in and the live wires, and names the parts that are working.\nIf a position connects a supply straight to earth, a Short circuit warning names the supply.\nSwitches show their position under them on the sheet.\nReset all puts every part back to its starting position. Stop test, or Esc, ends the test.\nThe live test follows connections only. It does not model voltage, current, voltage drop or timing, so it cannot tell you that a wire is too thin, a fuse too small, or a relay too slow. It answers one question: with the switches set like this, is there a path from a supply, through this load, to earth?\n\nUsing them together\nA good way to check a new circuit:\nTrace each wire you have added, to make sure it joins what you meant and nothing else.\nRun the live test, operate the switch for the circuit, and check the right load comes on and nothing else does.\nBlow its fuse, and check the load goes off.\nOpen the Checks list and deal with anything new.","sections":[{"anchor":"","title":"Circuit trace and live test","level":1,"start":0},{"anchor":"tracing-a-circuit","title":"Tracing a circuit","level":2,"start":229},{"anchor":"what-counts-as-one-circuit","title":"What counts as one circuit","level":3,"start":993},{"anchor":"the-circuits-name","title":"The circuit's name","level":3,"start":1676},{"anchor":"the-circuits-list","title":"The Circuits list","level":2,"start":1915},{"anchor":"the-live-test","title":"The live test","level":2,"start":2285},{"anchor":"operating-parts","title":"Operating parts","level":3,"start":2748},{"anchor":"what-you-see","title":"What you see","level":3,"start":3998},{"anchor":"using-them-together","title":"Using them together","level":2,"start":4789}]},
{"id":"fuses-and-loads","file":"12-fuses-and-loads.md","title":"Fuses and loads","html":"<h1 id=\"lm-fuses-and-loads\">Fuses and loads</h1>\n<p>The <strong>Fuses &amp; loads</strong> tab in the Lists pane is a fuse and relay schedule with a load budget. For every fuse, fuse box way, relay and supply in the project it adds up what is downstream and compares it with the rating.</p>\n<blockquote class=\"lm-note\"><p>These figures are estimates for planning, made from the numbers you type in. They are a worst case with everything switched on, and they ignore inrush current (motors and lamps draw far more for a moment when they start), cable heating and voltage drop. Choose fuses to protect the wire, using the cable and fuse makers&#39; data.</p></blockquote>\n<h2 id=\"lm-fuses-and-loads--how-loomwright-works-out-a-load\" data-anchor=\"how-loomwright-works-out-a-load\">How LoomWright works out a load</h2>\n<p>A part&#39;s current comes from its fields:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>a <strong>current</strong> field with amps in it, such as the Generic load&#39;s <strong>Current</strong> of <code>5A</code>,</li>\n<li>or a <strong>power</strong> field in watts, divided by the system voltage. A lamp or light bar <strong>Rating</strong> of <code>55W</code> at 12 V is 4.6 A, and <code>2 × 60W</code> is read as 120 W, so 10 A.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Some parts are counted in a fixed way:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>a relay coil counts as 0.2 A,</li>\n<li>a DC-DC charger draws its rated output current plus 10% from its input.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Parts without a current or power field, such as motors, pumps, fans and the winch, count as 0 A. To include them, use a <strong>Generic load</strong> with its <strong>Current</strong> filled in, or give your own component a field called <code>amps</code> (such as <code>amps = 8A</code>) or <code>watts</code>.</p>\n<p>Set the system voltage (12, 24 or 48 V) in the drawing settings; it is used for every conversion from watts.</p>\n<h2 id=\"lm-fuses-and-loads--fuses-and-breakers\" data-anchor=\"fuses-and-breakers\">Fuses and breakers</h2>\n<p>The first table lists every fuse, circuit breaker, fusible link and fuse box way:</p>\n<div class=\"lm-table\"><table><thead><tr><th>Column</th><th>What it shows</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Fuse</td><td>The fuse&#39;s reference, or the fuse box and way, such as <code>FB1 way 3</code>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Rating A</td><td>The fuse rating, from its field. Fuse box ways take theirs, in order, from the box&#39;s <strong>Ratings (comma list)</strong>, such as <code>20,15,5,10,10,5</code>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Load A</td><td>The total current of every load it feeds, with every switch on, or the largest <strong>Current</strong> you gave a wire leaving it, whichever is higher.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Use</td><td>The load as a share of the rating, with a bar.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Smallest wire</td><td>The smallest wire size on the fuse&#39;s output. The fuse should protect this wire.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Feeds</td><td>The loads downstream, each with its current.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Circuits</td><td>The circuit names of the wires it feeds.</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Each row is marked when the load is over the rating, or over 80% of it. A fuse with no rating is marked too. Fuses running near their rating blow on a warm day or when a motor starts; leave some margin.</p>\n<p>The design checks also flag a fuse bigger than the guide figure for a wire it feeds. See <a href=\"#lm-design-checks--checks\" data-chapter=\"design-checks\" data-anchor=\"checks\">Design checks</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"lm-fuses-and-loads--relays\" data-anchor=\"relays\">Relays</h2>\n<p>The second table lists each relay&#39;s contact rating (from its <strong>Rating</strong> field; 30 A if none is given), the current its contacts switch, and what they switch. A relay whose contacts carry more than their rating is flagged in the design checks.</p>\n<h2 id=\"lm-fuses-and-loads--supplies\" data-anchor=\"supplies\">Supplies</h2>\n<p>The third table lists batteries, alternators, solar panels and DC-DC chargers, with the load on each:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Batteries</strong>: their capacity, the load they feed, and <strong>Hours at full load</strong>. That figure assumes half the battery&#39;s capacity is usable, which suits a lead-acid battery you do not want to flatten; a lithium battery can give more.</li>\n<li><strong>Alternators</strong>: the load against their rated output.</li>\n<li><strong>Solar panels</strong>: their rating, worked out from the panel&#39;s power and the system voltage, against the load.</li>\n<li><strong>DC-DC chargers</strong>: the load on their output against their rating.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"lm-fuses-and-loads--exporting-the-schedule\" data-anchor=\"exporting-the-schedule\">Exporting the schedule</h2>\n<p><strong>Reports › Export fuses and loads (CSV)</strong> saves the three tables for a spreadsheet. The build pack includes them under <strong>Fuses, relays and load budget</strong>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"lm-fuses-and-loads--making-the-figures-useful\" data-anchor=\"making-the-figures-useful\">Making the figures useful</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Give every fuse a rating, and every fuse box its list of ratings.</li>\n<li>Give every lamp and light its wattage, and every other load its current, from the maker&#39;s data.</li>\n<li>Give every wire its size, so the <strong>Smallest wire</strong> column and the fuse checks mean something.</li>\n<li>Remember the totals assume everything is on together. A fridge, a winch and the work lights are rarely all on at once; you can judge which loads really run together.</li>\n</ul>","text":"Fuses and loads\nThe Fuses & loads tab in the Lists pane is a fuse and relay schedule with a load budget. For every fuse, fuse box way, relay and supply in the project it adds up what is downstream and compares it with the rating.\nThese figures are estimates for planning, made from the numbers you type in. They are a worst case with everything switched on, and they ignore inrush current (motors and lamps draw far more for a moment when they start), cable heating and voltage drop. Choose fuses to protect the wire, using the cable and fuse makers' data.\n\nHow LoomWright works out a load\nA part's current comes from its fields:\na current field with amps in it, such as the Generic load's Current of 5A,\nor a power field in watts, divided by the system voltage. A lamp or light bar Rating of 55W at 12 V is 4.6 A, and 2 × 60W is read as 120 W, so 10 A.\nSome parts are counted in a fixed way:\na relay coil counts as 0.2 A,\na DC-DC charger draws its rated output current plus 10% from its input.\nParts without a current or power field, such as motors, pumps, fans and the winch, count as 0 A. To include them, use a Generic load with its Current filled in, or give your own component a field called amps (such as amps = 8A) or watts.\nSet the system voltage (12, 24 or 48 V) in the drawing settings; it is used for every conversion from watts.\n\nFuses and breakers\nThe first table lists every fuse, circuit breaker, fusible link and fuse box way:\nColumn What it shows\nFuse The fuse's reference, or the fuse box and way, such as FB1 way 3.\nRating A The fuse rating, from its field. Fuse box ways take theirs, in order, from the box's Ratings (comma list), such as 20,15,5,10,10,5.\nLoad A The total current of every load it feeds, with every switch on, or the largest Current you gave a wire leaving it, whichever is higher.\nUse The load as a share of the rating, with a bar.\nSmallest wire The smallest wire size on the fuse's output. The fuse should protect this wire.\nFeeds The loads downstream, each with its current.\nCircuits The circuit names of the wires it feeds.\nEach row is marked when the load is over the rating, or over 80% of it. A fuse with no rating is marked too. Fuses running near their rating blow on a warm day or when a motor starts; leave some margin.\nThe design checks also flag a fuse bigger than the guide figure for a wire it feeds. See Design checks.\n\nRelays\nThe second table lists each relay's contact rating (from its Rating field; 30 A if none is given), the current its contacts switch, and what they switch. A relay whose contacts carry more than their rating is flagged in the design checks.\n\nSupplies\nThe third table lists batteries, alternators, solar panels and DC-DC chargers, with the load on each:\nBatteries: their capacity, the load they feed, and Hours at full load. That figure assumes half the battery's capacity is usable, which suits a lead-acid battery you do not want to flatten; a lithium battery can give more.\nAlternators: the load against their rated output.\nSolar panels: their rating, worked out from the panel's power and the system voltage, against the load.\nDC-DC chargers: the load on their output against their rating.\n\nExporting the schedule\nReports › Export fuses and loads (CSV) saves the three tables for a spreadsheet. The build pack includes them under Fuses, relays and load budget.\n\nMaking the figures useful\nGive every fuse a rating, and every fuse box its list of ratings.\nGive every lamp and light its wattage, and every other load its current, from the maker's data.\nGive every wire its size, so the Smallest wire column and the fuse checks mean something.\nRemember the totals assume everything is on together. A fridge, a winch and the work lights are rarely all on at once; you can judge which loads really run together.","sections":[{"anchor":"","title":"Fuses and loads","level":1,"start":0},{"anchor":"how-loomwright-works-out-a-load","title":"How LoomWright works out a load","level":2,"start":558},{"anchor":"fuses-and-breakers","title":"Fuses and breakers","level":2,"start":1343},{"anchor":"relays","title":"Relays","level":2,"start":2374},{"anchor":"supplies","title":"Supplies","level":2,"start":2621},{"anchor":"exporting-the-schedule","title":"Exporting the schedule","level":2,"start":3173},{"anchor":"making-the-figures-useful","title":"Making the figures useful","level":2,"start":3344}]},
{"id":"lists-and-reports","file":"13-lists-and-reports.md","title":"Lists and reports","html":"<h1 id=\"lm-lists-and-reports\">Lists and reports</h1>\n<p>LoomWright keeps a set of lists up to date as you draw. They are in the Lists pane under the sheet, in the <strong>Reports</strong> menu, and in the build pack. This chapter describes each one.</p>\n<h2 id=\"lm-lists-and-reports--using-the-lists-pane\" data-anchor=\"using-the-lists-pane\">Using the Lists pane</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Click a tab to show a list. <strong>Reports</strong> in the menu bar opens the same lists.</li>\n<li><strong>All sheets</strong> lists the whole project. Untick it to list only the sheet you are looking at.</li>\n<li>Click a row to select that wire or part on the drawing. If it is on another sheet, LoomWright goes there.</li>\n<li>The arrow at the right-hand end folds the pane away; the <strong>Lists</strong> button on the tool rail brings it back.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"lm-lists-and-reports--wire-list\" data-anchor=\"wire-list\">Wire list</h2>\n<p>Every wire, one row each, in drawing order:</p>\n<div class=\"lm-table\"><table><thead><tr><th>Column</th><th>What it shows</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Sheet</td><td>The sheet number.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Wire</td><td>The wire number.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>From, To</td><td>The two ends, as reference and pin, such as <code>F1:2</code> and <code>U1:IN</code>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Colour, Code</td><td>The colour in words, with a swatch, and its colour code.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Size, AWG</td><td>The size in mm² and the nearest AWG.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Length m</td><td>The length you entered.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Current A</td><td>The current you entered.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Drop V</td><td>The voltage drop, when length and current are both filled in.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Circuit, Harness, Cable</td><td>As set on the wire.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Notes</td><td>The wire&#39;s notes.</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"lm-lists-and-reports--parts--costs\" data-anchor=\"parts--costs\">Parts &amp; costs</h2>\n<p>Everything to buy, grouped, with quantities, part numbers, suppliers, prices, references and locations, and totals for parts and cable. See <a href=\"#lm-part-data--the-parts-list-and-costs\" data-chapter=\"part-data\" data-anchor=\"the-parts-list-and-costs\">Part data</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"lm-lists-and-reports--cable-totals\" data-anchor=\"cable-totals\">Cable totals</h2>\n<p>The cable to buy, by size, colour and insulation: how many wires, the total metres, the metres to buy with 15% added, the price per metre and the cost. Type your price per metre for each size under the table. See <a href=\"#lm-part-data--cable-prices\" data-chapter=\"part-data\" data-anchor=\"cable-prices\">Cable prices</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"lm-lists-and-reports--colour-usage\" data-anchor=\"colour-usage\">Colour usage</h2>\n<p>Every colour code and every job it does, by circuit and harness. See <a href=\"#lm-wires-and-colours--colour-usage\" data-chapter=\"wires-and-colours\" data-anchor=\"colour-usage\">Colour usage</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"lm-lists-and-reports--fuses--loads\" data-anchor=\"fuses--loads\">Fuses &amp; loads</h2>\n<p>The fuse and relay schedule and the load budget. See <a href=\"#lm-fuses-and-loads\" data-chapter=\"fuses-and-loads\" data-anchor=\"\">Fuses and loads</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"lm-lists-and-reports--connectors\" data-anchor=\"connectors\">Connectors</h2>\n<p>Every connector, cavity by cavity: the wire, its code and size, its circuit, where it goes, the terminal and the seal. Each connector&#39;s heading gives its series, housing, side, mating half, wedge lock and sheet. See <a href=\"#lm-harnesses-connectors-and-ends--connector-tables\" data-chapter=\"harnesses-connectors-and-ends\" data-anchor=\"connector-tables\">Connector tables</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"lm-lists-and-reports--cut-list\" data-anchor=\"cut-list\">Cut list</h2>\n<p>The list to cut wires from, one section per harness, each headed with how many wires and how many metres to cut. Within a harness, wires are listed heaviest first.</p>\n<div class=\"lm-table\"><table><thead><tr><th>Column</th><th>What it shows</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Wire, Code, Size, Insulation</td><td>Which wire, and what to cut it from.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Length m</td><td>The length you entered.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Cut m</td><td>The length plus the <strong>cut allowance</strong> for stripping and terminating. The allowance is set under <strong>Build</strong> in the drawing settings; it starts at 0.15 m.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>From, End A</td><td>Where the first end goes, and its terminal, strip length, heat shrink and seal.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>To, End B</td><td>The same for the other end.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Cable</td><td>The multicore cable it is a core of, if any.</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Harnesses are listed in alphabetical order. Wires with no harness come last, under <strong>No harness</strong>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"lm-lists-and-reports--circuits\" data-anchor=\"circuits\">Circuits</h2>\n<p>Every circuit, with its wires, connections, sheets and the pins it joins. Click a row to trace it. See <a href=\"#lm-trace-and-live-test--the-circuits-list\" data-chapter=\"trace-and-live-test\" data-anchor=\"the-circuits-list\">The Circuits list</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"lm-lists-and-reports--checks\" data-anchor=\"checks\">Checks</h2>\n<p>The design checks. See <a href=\"#lm-design-checks\" data-chapter=\"design-checks\" data-anchor=\"\">Design checks</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"lm-lists-and-reports--exporting-lists-as-csv\" data-anchor=\"exporting-lists-as-csv\">Exporting lists as CSV</h2>\n<p>Each list can be saved as a CSV file for a spreadsheet, from the <strong>Reports</strong> menu:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Export wire list (CSV)</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Export parts and costs (CSV)</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Export cable totals (CSV)</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Export cut list (CSV)</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Export connector tables (CSV)</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Export fuses and loads (CSV)</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Export wire labels (CSV)</strong>, for a label printer; see <a href=\"#lm-build-pack-and-labels--wire-labels\" data-chapter=\"build-pack-and-labels\" data-anchor=\"wire-labels\">Wire labels</a></li>\n<li><strong>Export colour usage (CSV)</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>The CSV files always cover the whole project.</p>\n<h2 id=\"lm-lists-and-reports--printing-lists\" data-anchor=\"printing-lists\">Printing lists</h2>\n<p>To print the lists, use the build pack, which puts any of them together with the drawings in one document. See <a href=\"#lm-build-pack-and-labels\" data-chapter=\"build-pack-and-labels\" data-anchor=\"\">Build pack and wire labels</a>. <strong>File › Print or save PDF (all sheets)</strong> prints every sheet followed by the wire list.</p>","text":"Lists and reports\nLoomWright keeps a set of lists up to date as you draw. They are in the Lists pane under the sheet, in the Reports menu, and in the build pack. This chapter describes each one.\n\nUsing the Lists pane\nClick a tab to show a list. Reports in the menu bar opens the same lists.\nAll sheets lists the whole project. Untick it to list only the sheet you are looking at.\nClick a row to select that wire or part on the drawing. If it is on another sheet, LoomWright goes there.\nThe arrow at the right-hand end folds the pane away; the Lists button on the tool rail brings it back.\n\nWire list\nEvery wire, one row each, in drawing order:\nColumn What it shows\nSheet The sheet number.\nWire The wire number.\nFrom, To The two ends, as reference and pin, such as F1:2 and U1:IN.\nColour, Code The colour in words, with a swatch, and its colour code.\nSize, AWG The size in mm² and the nearest AWG.\nLength m The length you entered.\nCurrent A The current you entered.\nDrop V The voltage drop, when length and current are both filled in.\nCircuit, Harness, Cable As set on the wire.\nNotes The wire's notes.\n\nParts & costs\nEverything to buy, grouped, with quantities, part numbers, suppliers, prices, references and locations, and totals for parts and cable. See Part data.\n\nCable totals\nThe cable to buy, by size, colour and insulation: how many wires, the total metres, the metres to buy with 15% added, the price per metre and the cost. Type your price per metre for each size under the table. See Cable prices.\n\nColour usage\nEvery colour code and every job it does, by circuit and harness. See Colour usage.\n\nFuses & loads\nThe fuse and relay schedule and the load budget. See Fuses and loads.\n\nConnectors\nEvery connector, cavity by cavity: the wire, its code and size, its circuit, where it goes, the terminal and the seal. Each connector's heading gives its series, housing, side, mating half, wedge lock and sheet. See Connector tables.\n\nCut list\nThe list to cut wires from, one section per harness, each headed with how many wires and how many metres to cut. Within a harness, wires are listed heaviest first.\nColumn What it shows\nWire, Code, Size, Insulation Which wire, and what to cut it from.\nLength m The length you entered.\nCut m The length plus the cut allowance for stripping and terminating. The allowance is set under Build in the drawing settings; it starts at 0.15 m.\nFrom, End A Where the first end goes, and its terminal, strip length, heat shrink and seal.\nTo, End B The same for the other end.\nCable The multicore cable it is a core of, if any.\nHarnesses are listed in alphabetical order. Wires with no harness come last, under No harness.\n\nCircuits\nEvery circuit, with its wires, connections, sheets and the pins it joins. Click a row to trace it. See The Circuits list.\n\nChecks\nThe design checks. See Design checks.\n\nExporting lists as CSV\nEach list can be saved as a CSV file for a spreadsheet, from the Reports menu:\nExport wire list (CSV)\nExport parts and costs (CSV)\nExport cable totals (CSV)\nExport cut list (CSV)\nExport connector tables (CSV)\nExport fuses and loads (CSV)\nExport wire labels (CSV), for a label printer; see Wire labels\nExport colour usage (CSV)\nThe CSV files always cover the whole project.\n\nPrinting lists\nTo print the lists, use the build pack, which puts any of them together with the drawings in one document. See Build pack and wire labels. File › Print or save PDF (all sheets) prints every sheet followed by the wire list.","sections":[{"anchor":"","title":"Lists and reports","level":1,"start":0},{"anchor":"using-the-lists-pane","title":"Using the Lists pane","level":2,"start":196},{"anchor":"wire-list","title":"Wire list","level":2,"start":590},{"anchor":"parts--costs","title":"Parts & costs","level":2,"start":1103},{"anchor":"cable-totals","title":"Cable totals","level":2,"start":1269},{"anchor":"colour-usage","title":"Colour usage","level":2,"start":1510},{"anchor":"fuses--loads","title":"Fuses & loads","level":2,"start":1607},{"anchor":"connectors","title":"Connectors","level":2,"start":1692},{"anchor":"cut-list","title":"Cut list","level":2,"start":1938},{"anchor":"circuits","title":"Circuits","level":2,"start":2658},{"anchor":"checks","title":"Checks","level":2,"start":2790},{"anchor":"exporting-lists-as-csv","title":"Exporting lists as CSV","level":2,"start":2836},{"anchor":"printing-lists","title":"Printing lists","level":2,"start":3233}]},
{"id":"build-pack-and-labels","file":"14-build-pack-and-labels.md","title":"Build pack and wire labels","html":"<h1 id=\"lm-build-pack-and-labels\">Build pack and wire labels</h1>\n<p>When the drawing is done, LoomWright can put everything needed to build the loom into one printable document, and print a label for every wire.</p>\n<h2 id=\"lm-build-pack-and-labels--printing-the-drawings\" data-anchor=\"printing-the-drawings\">Printing the drawings</h2>\n<p><strong>File › Print or save PDF (all sheets)</strong> (<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>P</kbd>, or <strong>PDF</strong> on the tool rail) prints every sheet, each on its own page at its paper size, followed by the wire list. To get a PDF, choose <strong>Save as PDF</strong> (or <strong>Microsoft Print to PDF</strong>) as the printer in the print window.</p>\n<p>For a single sheet as a picture or for CAD, see <a href=\"#lm-import-and-export--exporting-drawings\" data-chapter=\"import-and-export\" data-anchor=\"exporting-drawings\">Import and export</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"lm-build-pack-and-labels--the-build-pack\" data-anchor=\"the-build-pack\">The build pack</h2>\n<p><strong>File › Build pack (PDF)</strong>, <strong>Reports › Build pack</strong>, or <strong>Pack</strong> on the tool rail, opens the build pack dialog. Tick the sections you want:</p>\n<div class=\"lm-table\"><table><thead><tr><th>Section</th><th>What it holds</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Drawings</td><td>Every sheet, one to a page.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Revision history</td><td>Every issued revision: letter, date, who issued it and the description.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Wire list</td><td>Every wire, with both ends, code, colour, size, length, current, circuit, harness and notes.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Cut lists by harness</td><td>One cut list per harness, with the length to cut and both ends&#39; terminals.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Connector tables</td><td>Every connector, cavity by cavity, with housing, wedge lock, terminal and the number of cavity plugs needed.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Fuses, relays and load budget</td><td>The fuse and relay schedule and the supplies.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Parts and costs</td><td>The parts to buy and the cable to buy, with prices and totals.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Multicore and screened cables</td><td>Each cable, its type and cores, how many are used and spare, and its wires.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Component locations</td><td>The vehicle picture with its markers, and where each part is fitted.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Harness formboards</td><td>The formboard drawing of each harness, with its branch lengths.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Colour usage</td><td>Every colour code and the circuits and harnesses it is used for.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Wire labels</td><td>A sheet of wire labels. Not ticked to start with.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Design checks</td><td>Any errors and checks still open. Information findings are left out.</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>The pack starts with a cover page giving the project title, vehicle, revision, date, who drew it and how many sheets and wires it has.</p>\n<p>A section with nothing in it is left out. The formboard section only includes harnesses you have laid out in the formboard view; see <a href=\"#lm-harness-formboard\" data-chapter=\"harness-formboard\" data-anchor=\"\">Harness formboard</a>.</p>\n<p><strong>Drawings in black and white (wire colours written as codes)</strong> prints the drawings for a mono printer, whatever the drawing setting is. See <a href=\"#lm-projects-and-sheets--black-and-white-drawings\" data-chapter=\"projects-and-sheets\" data-anchor=\"black-and-white-drawings\">Black and white drawings</a>.</p>\n<p>Then:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Print or save PDF</strong> sends the pack to the printer, where you can choose <strong>Save as PDF</strong>. The pack is laid out for A4.</li>\n<li><strong>Save as web page</strong> saves the whole pack as one HTML file. Open it in any browser to read it on a tablet in the workshop, or print it from there.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote class=\"lm-note\"><p>Read the build pack through before you cut any wire. Check part numbers against the makers&#39; catalogues, and check the design checks section is empty or that you understand everything in it.</p></blockquote>\n<h2 id=\"lm-build-pack-and-labels--wire-labels\" data-anchor=\"wire-labels\">Wire labels</h2>\n<p><strong>File › Wire labels</strong> (or <strong>Reports › Wire labels</strong>) prints a label for each end of every wire. Each label shows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>the wire number, in bold,</li>\n<li>where this end goes and where the other end goes, such as <code>F1:2 → U1:IN</code>,</li>\n<li>the colour code.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>So a wire end found in the middle of a loom says what it is and where it should go.</p>\n<p>Choose a <strong>Label size</strong>:</p>\n<div class=\"lm-table\"><table><thead><tr><th>Size</th><th>Suits</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>38 × 21 mm</td><td>A4 sheets of 65 labels.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>63.5 × 33.9 mm</td><td>A4 sheets of 24 labels.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>46 × 11.1 mm</td><td>A4 sheets of 84 wrap-around labels.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>25 × 10 mm</td><td>Heat-shrink sleeve printers.</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Tick <strong>One label per wire instead of one per end</strong> for one label per wire, showing both ends as <code>F1:2 ↔ U1:IN</code>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Print labels</strong> prints them on A4 at the size chosen. Print a test page on plain paper first and hold it against a label sheet: printers differ, and so do label sheets.</li>\n<li><strong>Save CSV for a label printer</strong> saves the labels as a spreadsheet file, with one row per label. Most label printer programs can import it. <strong>Reports › Export wire labels (CSV)</strong> does the same.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The <strong>Wire labels</strong> section of the build pack prints one label per wire end at 38 × 21 mm. For another size, print labels from <strong>File › Wire labels</strong>.</p>\n<p>Renumber wires before printing labels, not after; see <a href=\"#lm-drawing--renumbering-wires-and-parts\" data-chapter=\"drawing\" data-anchor=\"renumbering-wires-and-parts\">Renumbering</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"lm-build-pack-and-labels--when-printing-is-not-available\" data-anchor=\"when-printing-is-not-available\">When printing is not available</h2>\n<p>When LoomWright runs inside a preview that does not allow printing, the print commands are hidden or greyed out. Use <strong>Save as web page</strong> in the build pack dialog instead, open the saved page in a browser, and print from there.</p>","text":"Build pack and wire labels\nWhen the drawing is done, LoomWright can put everything needed to build the loom into one printable document, and print a label for every wire.\n\nPrinting the drawings\nFile › Print or save PDF (all sheets) (Ctrl+P, or PDF on the tool rail) prints every sheet, each on its own page at its paper size, followed by the wire list. To get a PDF, choose Save as PDF (or Microsoft Print to PDF) as the printer in the print window.\nFor a single sheet as a picture or for CAD, see Import and export.\n\nThe build pack\nFile › Build pack (PDF), Reports › Build pack, or Pack on the tool rail, opens the build pack dialog. Tick the sections you want:\nSection What it holds\nDrawings Every sheet, one to a page.\nRevision history Every issued revision: letter, date, who issued it and the description.\nWire list Every wire, with both ends, code, colour, size, length, current, circuit, harness and notes.\nCut lists by harness One cut list per harness, with the length to cut and both ends' terminals.\nConnector tables Every connector, cavity by cavity, with housing, wedge lock, terminal and the number of cavity plugs needed.\nFuses, relays and load budget The fuse and relay schedule and the supplies.\nParts and costs The parts to buy and the cable to buy, with prices and totals.\nMulticore and screened cables Each cable, its type and cores, how many are used and spare, and its wires.\nComponent locations The vehicle picture with its markers, and where each part is fitted.\nHarness formboards The formboard drawing of each harness, with its branch lengths.\nColour usage Every colour code and the circuits and harnesses it is used for.\nWire labels A sheet of wire labels. Not ticked to start with.\nDesign checks Any errors and checks still open. Information findings are left out.\nThe pack starts with a cover page giving the project title, vehicle, revision, date, who drew it and how many sheets and wires it has.\nA section with nothing in it is left out. The formboard section only includes harnesses you have laid out in the formboard view; see Harness formboard.\nDrawings in black and white (wire colours written as codes) prints the drawings for a mono printer, whatever the drawing setting is. See Black and white drawings.\nThen:\nPrint or save PDF sends the pack to the printer, where you can choose Save as PDF. The pack is laid out for A4.\nSave as web page saves the whole pack as one HTML file. Open it in any browser to read it on a tablet in the workshop, or print it from there.\nRead the build pack through before you cut any wire. Check part numbers against the makers' catalogues, and check the design checks section is empty or that you understand everything in it.\n\nWire labels\nFile › Wire labels (or Reports › Wire labels) prints a label for each end of every wire. Each label shows:\nthe wire number, in bold,\nwhere this end goes and where the other end goes, such as F1:2 → U1:IN,\nthe colour code.\nSo a wire end found in the middle of a loom says what it is and where it should go.\nChoose a Label size:\nSize Suits\n38 × 21 mm A4 sheets of 65 labels.\n63.5 × 33.9 mm A4 sheets of 24 labels.\n46 × 11.1 mm A4 sheets of 84 wrap-around labels.\n25 × 10 mm Heat-shrink sleeve printers.\nTick One label per wire instead of one per end for one label per wire, showing both ends as F1:2 ↔ U1:IN.\nPrint labels prints them on A4 at the size chosen. Print a test page on plain paper first and hold it against a label sheet: printers differ, and so do label sheets.\nSave CSV for a label printer saves the labels as a spreadsheet file, with one row per label. Most label printer programs can import it. Reports › Export wire labels (CSV) does the same.\nThe Wire labels section of the build pack prints one label per wire end at 38 × 21 mm. For another size, print labels from File › Wire labels.\nRenumber wires before printing labels, not after; see Renumbering.\n\nWhen printing is not available\nWhen LoomWright runs inside a preview that does not allow printing, the print commands are hidden or greyed out. Use Save as web page in the build pack dialog instead, open the saved page in a browser, and print from there.","sections":[{"anchor":"","title":"Build pack and wire labels","level":1,"start":0},{"anchor":"printing-the-drawings","title":"Printing the drawings","level":2,"start":172},{"anchor":"the-build-pack","title":"The build pack","level":2,"start":518},{"anchor":"wire-labels","title":"Wire labels","level":2,"start":2694},{"anchor":"when-printing-is-not-available","title":"When printing is not available","level":2,"start":3876}]},
{"id":"harness-formboard","file":"15-harness-formboard.md","title":"Harness formboard","html":"<h1 id=\"lm-harness-formboard\">Harness formboard</h1>\n<p>A wiring diagram shows how things connect, not where the wires run. To make up a harness on the bench you need its physical shape: a trunk, the branches that leave it, how long each is, and which connector sits at the end of each. That is a <strong>formboard</strong> layout, and LoomWright can draw one for each harness.</p>\n<p>The formboard also works out wire lengths from the route each wire takes, and can copy them back onto the wires.</p>\n<h2 id=\"lm-harness-formboard--opening-the-formboard\" data-anchor=\"opening-the-formboard\">Opening the formboard</h2>\n<p>Give your wires harness names first (see <a href=\"#lm-harnesses-connectors-and-ends--harnesses\" data-chapter=\"harnesses-connectors-and-ends\" data-anchor=\"harnesses\">Harnesses</a>). Then open <strong>Tools › Harness formboard</strong>, the <strong>Formboard</strong> button on the tool rail, or the formboard button beside a harness in the Project pane.</p>\n<p>Choose the harness from the list at the top left. Each harness has its own layout, saved in the project file.</p>\n<figure class=\"lm-fig\"><img src=\"manual/img/formboard.png\" alt=\"The harness formboard for the engine bay harness\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>The formboard for one harness, with a branch selected. Each branch is labelled with its length, how many wires it carries and the bundle diameter.</figcaption></figure>\n<h2 id=\"lm-harness-formboard--the-first-layout\" data-anchor=\"the-first-layout\">The first layout</h2>\n<p>The first time you open a harness, LoomWright draws it as a star: one centre point, with a branch of 500 mm out to each <strong>connector end</strong>.</p>\n<p>A connector end is a part that wires in this harness go to, named by its reference, such as <code>F1</code> or <code>K1</code>. A few things are joined into one end:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>a mated connector pair is one end,</li>\n<li>an off-sheet reference or net label is an end called by its link name, marked <code>(link)</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The star is only a starting point. Reshape it to match the vehicle.</p>\n<h2 id=\"lm-harness-formboard--shaping-the-layout\" data-anchor=\"shaping-the-layout\">Shaping the layout</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Drag</strong> a point or connector end to move it.</li>\n<li><strong>Click a branch</strong> to select it. In the panel on the right, set its <strong>Length (mm)</strong> and <strong>Sleeving</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Add a breakout point here</strong> splits the selected branch in two with a new point in the middle, sharing the length. Use breakout points where the harness divides.</li>\n<li>Select a point and use <strong>Join to another point…</strong>, then click the point or connector end to join it to. That adds a branch between them. <kbd>Esc</kbd> cancels.</li>\n<li>Give a breakout point a <strong>Name</strong>, such as <code>Bulkhead grommet</code>, so the layout is easy to read.</li>\n<li>Select a connector end and click <strong>Show on drawing</strong> to close the formboard and find that part on its sheet.</li>\n<li><strong>Delete branch</strong> removes the selected branch. <strong>Delete point</strong> removes a breakout point; if two branches met there, they are joined into one. Connector ends cannot be deleted, because they come from the wires; delete a branch instead.</li>\n<li><strong>Start layout again</strong> replaces the layout with a new star. It can be undone.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Z</kbd> and <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>Z</kbd> undo and redo, <kbd>Delete</kbd> deletes what is selected, and <kbd>0</kbd> fits the layout to the window. Scroll to pan, and hold <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> while scrolling to zoom.</p>\n<h3 id=\"lm-harness-formboard--drawing-to-scale\" data-anchor=\"drawing-to-scale\">Drawing to scale</h3>\n<p>While you arrange the layout, branches are drawn wherever you put the points. Tick <strong>Draw to scale</strong> to redraw it with every branch at its real length, which is what you want to print or export. Untick it to go back to arranging.</p>\n<h2 id=\"lm-harness-formboard--what-each-branch-shows\" data-anchor=\"what-each-branch-shows\">What each branch shows</h2>\n<p>Each branch is labelled with its length, the number of wires in it (such as <code>2w</code>), and an estimate of the bundle diameter (such as <code>Ø9.6</code>). Select a branch to see:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>the two points it joins,</li>\n<li>how many wires it carries, and their numbers,</li>\n<li>the <strong>Bundle diameter</strong>, estimated from the wire sizes and insulation,</li>\n<li>with a sleeving chosen, a suggested <strong>Sleeve size</strong> that leaves some room.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Sleeving types are convoluted conduit, split convoluted conduit, braided PET sleeve, heat-shrink sleeve (DR-25 type), cloth harness tape and PVC tape. LoomWright suggests a size for the first four.</p>\n<p>The bundle diameter is an estimate from typical cable sizes. Measure a test bundle, or check the cable maker&#39;s outside diameters, before ordering sleeving.</p>\n<h2 id=\"lm-harness-formboard--harness-totals\" data-anchor=\"harness-totals\">Harness totals</h2>\n<p>With nothing selected, the panel shows the harness&#39;s totals: the number of connector ends and branches, the total length of trunk and branches, and <strong>Wires routed</strong>: how many of the harness&#39;s wires have a path along the branches between their two ends.</p>\n<p>If some wires have no path, LoomWright says so. Join their connector ends to the layout.</p>\n<h2 id=\"lm-harness-formboard--wire-lengths\" data-anchor=\"wire-lengths\">Wire lengths</h2>\n<p>Each wire&#39;s length can be worked out from its route: the lengths of the branches between its two ends.</p>\n<p>The <strong>Wire lengths</strong> table lists each wire in the harness with its <strong>Routed m</strong> length and the length it has <strong>Now m</strong>. To use the routed lengths:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Set <strong>Extra per end (mm)</strong>: the length added at each end for reaching the terminal and making it off. It starts at 50 mm.</li>\n<li>Click <strong>Copy routed lengths to the wires</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Each wire then gets its routed length plus the extra at both ends. It can be undone with <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Z</kbd>.</p>\n<p>The cut list then adds its own cut allowance on top for stripping and terminating, as for any wire; see <a href=\"#lm-lists-and-reports--cut-list\" data-chapter=\"lists-and-reports\" data-anchor=\"cut-list\">Cut list</a>. If you set the extra per end generously, you can lower the cut allowance to match.</p>\n<h2 id=\"lm-harness-formboard--exporting-the-formboard\" data-anchor=\"exporting-the-formboard\">Exporting the formboard</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Export DXF (1:1)</strong> saves the layout as a DXF drawing in millimetres at full size, to print on a plotter or roll paper and pin to a board, or to open in a CAD program.</li>\n<li><strong>Export SVG</strong> saves it as a picture.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The build pack includes the formboard of every harness you have laid out. See <a href=\"#lm-build-pack-and-labels\" data-chapter=\"build-pack-and-labels\" data-anchor=\"\">Build pack and wire labels</a>.</p>\n<p><strong>Close</strong> (or <kbd>Esc</kbd>) goes back to the drawing.</p>","text":"Harness formboard\nA wiring diagram shows how things connect, not where the wires run. To make up a harness on the bench you need its physical shape: a trunk, the branches that leave it, how long each is, and which connector sits at the end of each. That is a formboard layout, and LoomWright can draw one for each harness.\nThe formboard also works out wire lengths from the route each wire takes, and can copy them back onto the wires.\n\nOpening the formboard\nGive your wires harness names first (see Harnesses). Then open Tools › Harness formboard, the Formboard button on the tool rail, or the formboard button beside a harness in the Project pane.\nChoose the harness from the list at the top left. Each harness has its own layout, saved in the project file.\nThe formboard for one harness, with a branch selected. Each branch is labelled with its length, how many wires it carries and the bundle diameter.\n\nThe first layout\nThe first time you open a harness, LoomWright draws it as a star: one centre point, with a branch of 500 mm out to each connector end.\nA connector end is a part that wires in this harness go to, named by its reference, such as F1 or K1. A few things are joined into one end:\na mated connector pair is one end,\nan off-sheet reference or net label is an end called by its link name, marked (link).\nThe star is only a starting point. Reshape it to match the vehicle.\n\nShaping the layout\nDrag a point or connector end to move it.\nClick a branch to select it. In the panel on the right, set its Length (mm) and Sleeving.\nAdd a breakout point here splits the selected branch in two with a new point in the middle, sharing the length. Use breakout points where the harness divides.\nSelect a point and use Join to another point…, then click the point or connector end to join it to. That adds a branch between them. Esc cancels.\nGive a breakout point a Name, such as Bulkhead grommet, so the layout is easy to read.\nSelect a connector end and click Show on drawing to close the formboard and find that part on its sheet.\nDelete branch removes the selected branch. Delete point removes a breakout point; if two branches met there, they are joined into one. Connector ends cannot be deleted, because they come from the wires; delete a branch instead.\nStart layout again replaces the layout with a new star. It can be undone.\nCtrl+Z and Ctrl+Shift+Z undo and redo, Delete deletes what is selected, and 0 fits the layout to the window. Scroll to pan, and hold Ctrl while scrolling to zoom.\n\nDrawing to scale\nWhile you arrange the layout, branches are drawn wherever you put the points. Tick Draw to scale to redraw it with every branch at its real length, which is what you want to print or export. Untick it to go back to arranging.\n\nWhat each branch shows\nEach branch is labelled with its length, the number of wires in it (such as 2w), and an estimate of the bundle diameter (such as Ø9.6). Select a branch to see:\nthe two points it joins,\nhow many wires it carries, and their numbers,\nthe Bundle diameter, estimated from the wire sizes and insulation,\nwith a sleeving chosen, a suggested Sleeve size that leaves some room.\nSleeving types are convoluted conduit, split convoluted conduit, braided PET sleeve, heat-shrink sleeve (DR-25 type), cloth harness tape and PVC tape. LoomWright suggests a size for the first four.\nThe bundle diameter is an estimate from typical cable sizes. Measure a test bundle, or check the cable maker's outside diameters, before ordering sleeving.\n\nHarness totals\nWith nothing selected, the panel shows the harness's totals: the number of connector ends and branches, the total length of trunk and branches, and Wires routed: how many of the harness's wires have a path along the branches between their two ends.\nIf some wires have no path, LoomWright says so. Join their connector ends to the layout.\n\nWire lengths\nEach wire's length can be worked out from its route: the lengths of the branches between its two ends.\nThe Wire lengths table lists each wire in the harness with its Routed m length and the length it has Now m. To use the routed lengths:\nSet Extra per end (mm): the length added at each end for reaching the terminal and making it off. It starts at 50 mm.\nClick Copy routed lengths to the wires.\nEach wire then gets its routed length plus the extra at both ends. It can be undone with Ctrl+Z.\nThe cut list then adds its own cut allowance on top for stripping and terminating, as for any wire; see Cut list. If you set the extra per end generously, you can lower the cut allowance to match.\n\nExporting the formboard\nExport DXF (1:1) saves the layout as a DXF drawing in millimetres at full size, to print on a plotter or roll paper and pin to a board, or to open in a CAD program.\nExport SVG saves it as a picture.\nThe build pack includes the formboard of every harness you have laid out. See Build pack and wire labels.\nClose (or Esc) goes back to the drawing.","sections":[{"anchor":"","title":"Harness formboard","level":1,"start":0},{"anchor":"opening-the-formboard","title":"Opening the formboard","level":2,"start":437},{"anchor":"the-first-layout","title":"The first layout","level":2,"start":908},{"anchor":"shaping-the-layout","title":"Shaping the layout","level":2,"start":1390},{"anchor":"drawing-to-scale","title":"Drawing to scale","level":3,"start":2504},{"anchor":"what-each-branch-shows","title":"What each branch shows","level":2,"start":2748},{"anchor":"harness-totals","title":"Harness totals","level":2,"start":3495},{"anchor":"wire-lengths","title":"Wire lengths","level":2,"start":3849},{"anchor":"exporting-the-formboard","title":"Exporting the formboard","level":2,"start":4553}]},
{"id":"vehicle-locations","file":"16-vehicle-locations.md","title":"Vehicle locations view","html":"<h1 id=\"lm-vehicle-locations\">Vehicle locations view</h1>\n<p>The vehicle locations view records where each part is fitted, both in words and as a marker on a picture of the vehicle. It answers the question every loom raises six months later: where did I put that relay?</p>\n<h2 id=\"lm-vehicle-locations--opening-the-view\" data-anchor=\"opening-the-view\">Opening the view</h2>\n<p>Choose <strong>Tools › Component locations (vehicle view)</strong>, or <strong>Vehicle</strong> on the tool rail. The view fills the window: the vehicle on the left, the list of parts on the right.</p>\n<p><strong>Close</strong> (or <kbd>Esc</kbd>) goes back to the drawing.</p>\n<h2 id=\"lm-vehicle-locations--the-picture\" data-anchor=\"the-picture\">The picture</h2>\n<p>To start with, LoomWright shows a plain outline of a vehicle seen from above, with the front to the left and the engine bay, cab and rear marked.</p>\n<p>For a better picture, click <strong>Use a picture…</strong> and choose a photo or drawing of your vehicle, ideally from above. PNG, JPEG, SVG and WebP pictures work. LoomWright saves the picture inside the project file, so it travels with the project. To keep the file small, it stores photos as JPEG no larger than 1600 pixels on the longer side; SVG drawings are kept as they are. <strong>Use the plain outline</strong> goes back to the outline.</p>\n<h2 id=\"lm-vehicle-locations--recording-where-parts-are\" data-anchor=\"recording-where-parts-are\">Recording where parts are</h2>\n<p>The list on the right shows every part with a reference: fuses, relays, switches, loads, connectors and so on. For each part:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>type where it lives in the box, such as <code>Under seat box</code> or <code>Dash centre</code>. LoomWright suggests places you have already typed. This is the same <strong>Location</strong> as in the part&#39;s Properties; see <a href=\"#lm-part-data--locations\" data-chapter=\"part-data\" data-anchor=\"locations\">Part data</a>.</li>\n<li>click <strong>Place</strong>, then click on the picture where it is fitted. A red marker with the reference appears there. <kbd>Esc</kbd> cancels placing.</li>\n<li>drag a marker to move it, or click <strong>Move</strong> and click the new spot.</li>\n<li>the remove button beside <strong>Move</strong> removes the marker.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The panel counts how many parts there are and how many are placed. <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Z</kbd> undoes changes in this view too.</p>\n<p>Markers belong to a part&#39;s reference and type, so a part drawn on several sheets has one marker.</p>\n<h2 id=\"lm-vehicle-locations--using-the-locations\" data-anchor=\"using-the-locations\">Using the locations</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Export SVG</strong> and <strong>Export PNG</strong> save the picture with its markers.</li>\n<li>The build pack&#39;s <strong>Component locations</strong> section prints the picture with its markers and a table of every part&#39;s location. See <a href=\"#lm-build-pack-and-labels\" data-chapter=\"build-pack-and-labels\" data-anchor=\"\">Build pack and wire labels</a>.</li>\n<li>The parts list shows each line&#39;s locations, which helps when ordering for one area of the vehicle at a time.</li>\n</ul>","text":"Vehicle locations view\nThe vehicle locations view records where each part is fitted, both in words and as a marker on a picture of the vehicle. It answers the question every loom raises six months later: where did I put that relay?\n\nOpening the view\nChoose Tools › Component locations (vehicle view), or Vehicle on the tool rail. The view fills the window: the vehicle on the left, the list of parts on the right.\nClose (or Esc) goes back to the drawing.\n\nThe picture\nTo start with, LoomWright shows a plain outline of a vehicle seen from above, with the front to the left and the engine bay, cab and rear marked.\nFor a better picture, click Use a picture… and choose a photo or drawing of your vehicle, ideally from above. PNG, JPEG, SVG and WebP pictures work. LoomWright saves the picture inside the project file, so it travels with the project. To keep the file small, it stores photos as JPEG no larger than 1600 pixels on the longer side; SVG drawings are kept as they are. Use the plain outline goes back to the outline.\n\nRecording where parts are\nThe list on the right shows every part with a reference: fuses, relays, switches, loads, connectors and so on. For each part:\ntype where it lives in the box, such as Under seat box or Dash centre. LoomWright suggests places you have already typed. This is the same Location as in the part's Properties; see Part data.\nclick Place, then click on the picture where it is fitted. A red marker with the reference appears there. Esc cancels placing.\ndrag a marker to move it, or click Move and click the new spot.\nthe remove button beside Move removes the marker.\nThe panel counts how many parts there are and how many are placed. Ctrl+Z undoes changes in this view too.\nMarkers belong to a part's reference and type, so a part drawn on several sheets has one marker.\n\nUsing the locations\nExport SVG and Export PNG save the picture with its markers.\nThe build pack's Component locations section prints the picture with its markers and a table of every part's location. See Build pack and wire labels.\nThe parts list shows each line's locations, which helps when ordering for one area of the vehicle at a time.","sections":[{"anchor":"","title":"Vehicle locations view","level":1,"start":0},{"anchor":"opening-the-view","title":"Opening the view","level":2,"start":233},{"anchor":"the-picture","title":"The picture","level":2,"start":456},{"anchor":"recording-where-parts-are","title":"Recording where parts are","level":2,"start":1029},{"anchor":"using-the-locations","title":"Using the locations","level":2,"start":1819}]},
{"id":"revisions-and-save-points","file":"17-revisions-and-save-points.md","title":"Revision history and save points","html":"<h1 id=\"lm-revisions-and-save-points\">Revision history and save points</h1>\n<p>LoomWright keeps two kinds of history:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Revisions</strong> are versions you issue on purpose, such as when you print a drawing set to build from. They are stored in the project file, listed in the revision table on every sheet, and can be compared and gone back to.</li>\n<li><strong>Save points</strong> are copies LoomWright keeps by itself, on this computer, as you work. They are a safety net, like the history of a word processor.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Both are in the <strong>History</strong> pane (the <strong>History</strong> button on the tool rail) and the <strong>Revision</strong> menu.</p>\n<h2 id=\"lm-revisions-and-save-points--issuing-a-revision\" data-anchor=\"issuing-a-revision\">Issuing a revision</h2>\n<p>Issue a revision whenever a drawing set leaves your hands: when you print it to build from, send it to someone, or finish a stage of the build.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Choose <strong>Revision › Issue revision</strong>, or <strong>Issue revision…</strong> in the History pane.</li>\n<li>Check the <strong>Revision</strong> letter. LoomWright suggests the next one: A, then B, C… Z, then AA. If you number revisions instead, such as <code>P3</code>, it suggests <code>P4</code>.</li>\n<li>Check the <strong>Date</strong> and <strong>By</strong>, and write a <strong>Description</strong> of what changed, such as <code>Fridge feed moved to 4 mm²</code>.</li>\n<li>From the second revision on, the dialog lists the <strong>Changes since</strong> the last revision, and offers <strong>Cloud the changes since…</strong> (see <a href=\"#lm-revisions-and-save-points--revision-clouds\" data-chapter=\"revisions-and-save-points\" data-anchor=\"revision-clouds\">Revision clouds</a>).</li>\n<li>Click <strong>Issue revision</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>LoomWright records the whole project as it is now: every sheet, part, wire and setting. The project&#39;s revision letter and date (as shown in the title block) are set to match, the revision table on each sheet lists it, and a save point is kept.</p>\n<p>The revision is stored in the project, so save the project afterwards.</p>\n<h2 id=\"lm-revisions-and-save-points--the-history-pane\" data-anchor=\"the-history-pane\">The History pane</h2>\n<p>At the top, under <strong>Revision</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Current revision</strong>, how many revisions have been issued, and how many changes have been made since the last one.</li>\n<li><strong>Issue revision…</strong> and <strong>Compare…</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Revision clouds on drawings</strong>: see below.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Under <strong>Issued revisions</strong> each revision is listed, newest first, with its date, who issued it and its description, and three buttons:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Compare with now</strong> shows what has changed since that revision.</li>\n<li><strong>Open copy</strong> opens the project as it was at that revision in a new tab, to look at or print. It is a separate copy; close its tab when you are done.</li>\n<li><strong>Restore</strong> puts the drawings back to how they were at that revision. The list of revisions is kept, so you can go forward again by restoring a later one, and <strong>Restore</strong> can be undone with <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Z</kbd>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"lm-revisions-and-save-points--comparing-revisions\" data-anchor=\"comparing-revisions\">Comparing revisions</h2>\n<p><strong>Revision › Compare revisions</strong>, or <strong>Compare…</strong> in the History pane, compares any two revisions, or a revision and <strong>Now (unissued)</strong>. Choose <strong>From</strong> and <strong>To</strong>. LoomWright lists every difference in plain words: project details changed; harnesses and sheets added, removed or renamed; parts and wires added and removed; and changes to part fields and part data, and to wire ends, colours, sizes, lengths, circuits and harnesses.</p>\n<p><strong>Export list (CSV)</strong> saves the list of changes, for example to send with a revised drawing set.</p>\n<h2 id=\"lm-revisions-and-save-points--revision-clouds\" data-anchor=\"revision-clouds\">Revision clouds</h2>\n<p>On a drawing, a revision cloud is a scalloped outline drawn round what changed, so whoever builds from a revised drawing can see what is new.</p>\n<p>Under <strong>Revision clouds on drawings</strong> in the History pane, pick a revision. LoomWright draws clouds round the parts and wires that have changed since it, on every sheet, on screen, in print and in exports. Choose <strong>None</strong> to turn them off.</p>\n<p>When you issue a revision with <strong>Cloud the changes since…</strong> ticked, the drawings show clouds round what changed between the previous revision and this one.</p>\n<h2 id=\"lm-revisions-and-save-points--save-points\" data-anchor=\"save-points\">Save points</h2>\n<p>A save point is a copy of the whole project, kept by LoomWright in its own storage on this computer. They are kept:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>every time you save,</li>\n<li>every ten minutes while you have unsaved changes,</li>\n<li>when you issue a revision,</li>\n<li>when you choose <strong>Revision › Keep a save point now</strong> or click <strong>Keep a save point now</strong> in the History pane.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Save points on this computer</strong>, at the bottom of the History pane, lists them, newest first, with when and why each was kept. The last 40 for each project are kept; older ones are deleted.</p>\n<p>For each save point, <strong>Open copy</strong> opens it in a new tab and <strong>Restore</strong> puts the project back to how it was then. Restoring can be undone, and keeps the project&#39;s issued revisions.</p>\n<p>Save points are not stored in the project file. They stay on the computer where they were made, in the app&#39;s own storage (see <a href=\"#lm-files-and-saving--what-loomwright-remembers-between-sessions\" data-chapter=\"files-and-saving\" data-anchor=\"what-loomwright-remembers-between-sessions\">What LoomWright remembers between sessions</a>). In a browser, clearing site data deletes them. They do not replace saving your project, or keeping a backup copy of it somewhere else.</p>","text":"Revision history and save points\nLoomWright keeps two kinds of history:\nRevisions are versions you issue on purpose, such as when you print a drawing set to build from. They are stored in the project file, listed in the revision table on every sheet, and can be compared and gone back to.\nSave points are copies LoomWright keeps by itself, on this computer, as you work. They are a safety net, like the history of a word processor.\nBoth are in the History pane (the History button on the tool rail) and the Revision menu.\n\nIssuing a revision\nIssue a revision whenever a drawing set leaves your hands: when you print it to build from, send it to someone, or finish a stage of the build.\nChoose Revision › Issue revision, or Issue revision… in the History pane.\nCheck the Revision letter. LoomWright suggests the next one: A, then B, C… Z, then AA. If you number revisions instead, such as P3, it suggests P4.\nCheck the Date and By, and write a Description of what changed, such as Fridge feed moved to 4 mm².\nFrom the second revision on, the dialog lists the Changes since the last revision, and offers Cloud the changes since… (see Revision clouds).\nClick Issue revision.\nLoomWright records the whole project as it is now: every sheet, part, wire and setting. The project's revision letter and date (as shown in the title block) are set to match, the revision table on each sheet lists it, and a save point is kept.\nThe revision is stored in the project, so save the project afterwards.\n\nThe History pane\nAt the top, under Revision:\nCurrent revision, how many revisions have been issued, and how many changes have been made since the last one.\nIssue revision… and Compare….\nRevision clouds on drawings: see below.\nUnder Issued revisions each revision is listed, newest first, with its date, who issued it and its description, and three buttons:\nCompare with now shows what has changed since that revision.\nOpen copy opens the project as it was at that revision in a new tab, to look at or print. It is a separate copy; close its tab when you are done.\nRestore puts the drawings back to how they were at that revision. The list of revisions is kept, so you can go forward again by restoring a later one, and Restore can be undone with Ctrl+Z.\n\nComparing revisions\nRevision › Compare revisions, or Compare… in the History pane, compares any two revisions, or a revision and Now (unissued). Choose From and To. LoomWright lists every difference in plain words: project details changed; harnesses and sheets added, removed or renamed; parts and wires added and removed; and changes to part fields and part data, and to wire ends, colours, sizes, lengths, circuits and harnesses.\nExport list (CSV) saves the list of changes, for example to send with a revised drawing set.\n\nRevision clouds\nOn a drawing, a revision cloud is a scalloped outline drawn round what changed, so whoever builds from a revised drawing can see what is new.\nUnder Revision clouds on drawings in the History pane, pick a revision. LoomWright draws clouds round the parts and wires that have changed since it, on every sheet, on screen, in print and in exports. Choose None to turn them off.\nWhen you issue a revision with Cloud the changes since… ticked, the drawings show clouds round what changed between the previous revision and this one.\n\nSave points\nA save point is a copy of the whole project, kept by LoomWright in its own storage on this computer. They are kept:\nevery time you save,\nevery ten minutes while you have unsaved changes,\nwhen you issue a revision,\nwhen you choose Revision › Keep a save point now or click Keep a save point now in the History pane.\nSave points on this computer, at the bottom of the History pane, lists them, newest first, with when and why each was kept. The last 40 for each project are kept; older ones are deleted.\nFor each save point, Open copy opens it in a new tab and Restore puts the project back to how it was then. Restoring can be undone, and keeps the project's issued revisions.\nSave points are not stored in the project file. They stay on the computer where they were made, in the app's own storage (see What LoomWright remembers between sessions). In a browser, clearing site data deletes them. They do not replace saving your project, or keeping a backup copy of it somewhere else.","sections":[{"anchor":"","title":"Revision history and save points","level":1,"start":0},{"anchor":"issuing-a-revision","title":"Issuing a revision","level":2,"start":523},{"anchor":"the-history-pane","title":"The History pane","level":2,"start":1488},{"anchor":"comparing-revisions","title":"Comparing revisions","level":2,"start":2243},{"anchor":"revision-clouds","title":"Revision clouds","level":2,"start":2769},{"anchor":"save-points","title":"Save points","level":2,"start":3312}]},
{"id":"import-and-export","file":"18-import-and-export.md","title":"Import and export","html":"<h1 id=\"lm-import-and-export\">Import and export</h1>\n<p>LoomWright can bring in wire lists and netlists from other programs, and write its drawings and lists in forms other programs can use. Everything is in the <strong>File</strong> and <strong>Reports</strong> menus.</p>\n<h2 id=\"lm-import-and-export--importing\" data-anchor=\"importing\">Importing</h2>\n<p>Every import can be undone with <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Z</kbd>. If LoomWright finds something it cannot use, it says what, and imports the rest.</p>\n<h3 id=\"lm-import-and-export--a-wire-list-from-a-spreadsheet\" data-anchor=\"a-wire-list-from-a-spreadsheet\">A wire list from a spreadsheet</h3>\n<p><strong>File › Import wire list (spreadsheet)</strong> turns a table of wires into a drawing. Save the spreadsheet as CSV and choose <strong>Choose a CSV file…</strong>, or select the cells in your spreadsheet, copy them, and paste them into the box.</p>\n<p>The first row must name the columns. LoomWright understands:</p>\n<div class=\"lm-table\"><table><thead><tr><th>Column</th><th>What it holds</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Wire</td><td>The wire number.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>From, To</td><td>Each end, written as part and pin, such as <code>F1:2</code>. Or use separate <strong>From part</strong>, <strong>From pin</strong>, <strong>To part</strong> and <strong>To pin</strong> columns.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Colour, Tracer</td><td>A colour name or code, such as <code>red</code>, <code>R</code>, <code>RD</code> or <code>NR</code>, or main colour and tracer together, such as <code>BK/RD</code> or <code>red/white</code>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Size</td><td>In mm² or AWG.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Length</td><td>In metres or millimetres.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Current</td><td>In amps.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Circuit, Harness, Notes</td><td>As on a wire.</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Save a template</strong> saves an example file with the right columns to start from.</p>\n<p>The wires go onto a <strong>new sheet</strong>, named in <strong>New sheet name</strong>. For each part named in the list:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>if a part with that reference is already on another sheet, and has the pins the list uses, a copy of it is placed on the new sheet (the same reference means the same part);</li>\n<li>otherwise LoomWright makes a plain box with that reference and the pins the list uses. It appears in the parts list as <code>F9 (imported)</code>; replace it with a proper part when you can.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>LoomWright arranges the new parts in rows. Tidy the sheet afterwards.</p>\n<h3 id=\"lm-import-and-export--wireviz\" data-anchor=\"wireviz\">WireViz</h3>\n<p><strong>File › Import WireViz (YAML)</strong> reads a WireViz harness file (<code>.yml</code> or <code>.yaml</code>): its connectors, cables and connections become parts and wires on a new sheet named after the file.</p>\n<h3 id=\"lm-import-and-export--a-kicad-netlist\" data-anchor=\"a-kicad-netlist\">A KiCad netlist</h3>\n<p><strong>File › Import KiCad netlist</strong> reads a netlist exported from KiCad (in KiCad, <strong>File › Export › Netlist</strong>, KiCad format, which gives a <code>.net</code> file).</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Two-pin resistors, capacitors, inductors and fuses (references starting R, C, L and F) become LoomWright&#39;s own symbols.</li>\n<li>Every other component becomes a box with its pins and reference.</li>\n<li>Each net is drawn as a chain of wires from pin to pin, 0.5 mm², in black for nets named like ground (<code>GND</code>, <code>0V</code>), red for nets named like a supply (<code>+12V</code>, <code>VCC</code>), and blue for the rest. Named nets give the wires their circuit name.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A circuit board netlist does not know about wire colours, sizes or harnesses, so expect to set those afterwards.</p>\n<h3 id=\"lm-import-and-export--measured-wire-lengths\" data-anchor=\"measured-wire-lengths\">Measured wire lengths</h3>\n<p><strong>File › Import wire lengths (CAD)</strong> sets the lengths of existing wires from a list, for example lengths measured in a 3D model of the vehicle, or on the vehicle with a piece of string. Paste the list, or choose a file. Each row holds a wire number and its length:</p>\n<pre class=\"lm-code\"><code>Wire,Length mm\nW1,450\nW2,4300</code></pre>\n<p>How LoomWright reads the units:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>if the first row is a header that mentions <code>mm</code>, every length is in millimetres;</li>\n<li>otherwise, if every length in the list is more than 30, they are read as millimetres;</li>\n<li>otherwise every length is read as metres.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A header row saying which unit you mean avoids surprises. After the import, LoomWright says how many wires it updated, whether it read the lengths as metres or millimetres, and which wire numbers it could not find in the project.</p>\n<h2 id=\"lm-import-and-export--exporting-drawings\" data-anchor=\"exporting-drawings\">Exporting drawings</h2>\n<p>These export the sheet you are looking at:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>File › Export sheet as SVG</strong>: a scalable picture, sharp at any size. Good for documents and web pages.</li>\n<li><strong>File › Export sheet as PNG</strong>: a picture at twice the drawing&#39;s size; an A4 sheet comes out 2376 × 1680 pixels.</li>\n<li><strong>File › Export sheet as DXF (CAD)</strong>: an AutoCAD R12 DXF drawing in millimetres at paper size, with the frame, parts, pins, wires, text and wire labels on separate layers (<code>FRAME</code>, <code>PARTS</code>, <code>PINS</code>, <code>WIRES</code>, <code>TEXT</code>, <code>LABELS</code>).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A sheet with a fixed paper size exports at that size, with its frame. A <strong>Fit to drawing</strong> sheet exports with a border, the colour key and the title block round the drawing.</p>\n<p>To print or save every sheet as PDF, see <a href=\"#lm-build-pack-and-labels--printing-the-drawings\" data-chapter=\"build-pack-and-labels\" data-anchor=\"printing-the-drawings\">Printing the drawings</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"lm-import-and-export--exporting-for-wireviz\" data-anchor=\"exporting-for-wireviz\">Exporting for WireViz</h2>\n<p><strong>File › Export for WireViz (YAML)</strong> writes the project&#39;s connectors, cables and connections as a WireViz file. Choose <strong>Whole project</strong>, or one harness. WireViz is a separate free tool that draws harness documents from such a file.</p>\n<h2 id=\"lm-import-and-export--a-read-only-web-page\" data-anchor=\"a-read-only-web-page\">A read-only web page</h2>\n<p><strong>File › Save read-only web page</strong> saves the whole project as one HTML file that anyone can open in a web browser, with nothing to install. It has:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>each sheet, on its own tab,</li>\n<li>the wire list,</li>\n<li>tracing: click any wire to highlight its circuit and list every wire on it, on every sheet.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>It cannot be edited, which makes it a safe way to show a design to someone or keep a copy on a phone.</p>\n<h2 id=\"lm-import-and-export--lists-as-csv\" data-anchor=\"lists-as-csv\">Lists as CSV</h2>\n<p>Every list can be saved as a CSV file from the <strong>Reports</strong> menu. See <a href=\"#lm-lists-and-reports--exporting-lists-as-csv\" data-chapter=\"lists-and-reports\" data-anchor=\"exporting-lists-as-csv\">Exporting lists as CSV</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"lm-import-and-export--other-exports\" data-anchor=\"other-exports\">Other exports</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>The build pack can be saved as a web page. See <a href=\"#lm-build-pack-and-labels--the-build-pack\" data-chapter=\"build-pack-and-labels\" data-anchor=\"the-build-pack\">The build pack</a>.</li>\n<li>The harness formboard exports DXF at 1:1 and SVG. See <a href=\"#lm-harness-formboard--exporting-the-formboard\" data-chapter=\"harness-formboard\" data-anchor=\"exporting-the-formboard\">Exporting the formboard</a>.</li>\n<li>The vehicle view exports SVG and PNG. See <a href=\"#lm-vehicle-locations--using-the-locations\" data-chapter=\"vehicle-locations\" data-anchor=\"using-the-locations\">Using the locations</a>.</li>\n<li>Your components and blocks export as a library file. See <a href=\"#lm-component-builder--sharing-components-and-blocks\" data-chapter=\"component-builder\" data-anchor=\"sharing-components-and-blocks\">Sharing components and blocks</a>.</li>\n<li>The revision comparison exports CSV. See <a href=\"#lm-revisions-and-save-points--comparing-revisions\" data-chapter=\"revisions-and-save-points\" data-anchor=\"comparing-revisions\">Comparing revisions</a>.</li>\n</ul>","text":"Import and export\nLoomWright can bring in wire lists and netlists from other programs, and write its drawings and lists in forms other programs can use. Everything is in the File and Reports menus.\n\nImporting\nEvery import can be undone with Ctrl+Z. If LoomWright finds something it cannot use, it says what, and imports the rest.\n\nA wire list from a spreadsheet\nFile › Import wire list (spreadsheet) turns a table of wires into a drawing. Save the spreadsheet as CSV and choose Choose a CSV file…, or select the cells in your spreadsheet, copy them, and paste them into the box.\nThe first row must name the columns. LoomWright understands:\nColumn What it holds\nWire The wire number.\nFrom, To Each end, written as part and pin, such as F1:2. Or use separate From part, From pin, To part and To pin columns.\nColour, Tracer A colour name or code, such as red, R, RD or NR, or main colour and tracer together, such as BK/RD or red/white.\nSize In mm² or AWG.\nLength In metres or millimetres.\nCurrent In amps.\nCircuit, Harness, Notes As on a wire.\nSave a template saves an example file with the right columns to start from.\nThe wires go onto a new sheet, named in New sheet name. For each part named in the list:\nif a part with that reference is already on another sheet, and has the pins the list uses, a copy of it is placed on the new sheet (the same reference means the same part);\notherwise LoomWright makes a plain box with that reference and the pins the list uses. It appears in the parts list as F9 (imported); replace it with a proper part when you can.\nLoomWright arranges the new parts in rows. Tidy the sheet afterwards.\n\nWireViz\nFile › Import WireViz (YAML) reads a WireViz harness file (.yml or .yaml): its connectors, cables and connections become parts and wires on a new sheet named after the file.\n\nA KiCad netlist\nFile › Import KiCad netlist reads a netlist exported from KiCad (in KiCad, File › Export › Netlist, KiCad format, which gives a .net file).\nTwo-pin resistors, capacitors, inductors and fuses (references starting R, C, L and F) become LoomWright's own symbols.\nEvery other component becomes a box with its pins and reference.\nEach net is drawn as a chain of wires from pin to pin, 0.5 mm², in black for nets named like ground (GND, 0V), red for nets named like a supply (+12V, VCC), and blue for the rest. Named nets give the wires their circuit name.\nA circuit board netlist does not know about wire colours, sizes or harnesses, so expect to set those afterwards.\n\nMeasured wire lengths\nFile › Import wire lengths (CAD) sets the lengths of existing wires from a list, for example lengths measured in a 3D model of the vehicle, or on the vehicle with a piece of string. Paste the list, or choose a file. Each row holds a wire number and its length:\nWire,Length mm\nW1,450\nW2,4300\nHow LoomWright reads the units:\nif the first row is a header that mentions mm, every length is in millimetres;\notherwise, if every length in the list is more than 30, they are read as millimetres;\notherwise every length is read as metres.\nA header row saying which unit you mean avoids surprises. After the import, LoomWright says how many wires it updated, whether it read the lengths as metres or millimetres, and which wire numbers it could not find in the project.\n\nExporting drawings\nThese export the sheet you are looking at:\nFile › Export sheet as SVG: a scalable picture, sharp at any size. Good for documents and web pages.\nFile › Export sheet as PNG: a picture at twice the drawing's size; an A4 sheet comes out 2376 × 1680 pixels.\nFile › Export sheet as DXF (CAD): an AutoCAD R12 DXF drawing in millimetres at paper size, with the frame, parts, pins, wires, text and wire labels on separate layers (FRAME, PARTS, PINS, WIRES, TEXT, LABELS).\nA sheet with a fixed paper size exports at that size, with its frame. A Fit to drawing sheet exports with a border, the colour key and the title block round the drawing.\nTo print or save every sheet as PDF, see Printing the drawings.\n\nExporting for WireViz\nFile › Export for WireViz (YAML) writes the project's connectors, cables and connections as a WireViz file. Choose Whole project, or one harness. WireViz is a separate free tool that draws harness documents from such a file.\n\nA read-only web page\nFile › Save read-only web page saves the whole project as one HTML file that anyone can open in a web browser, with nothing to install. It has:\neach sheet, on its own tab,\nthe wire list,\ntracing: click any wire to highlight its circuit and list every wire on it, on every sheet.\nIt cannot be edited, which makes it a safe way to show a design to someone or keep a copy on a phone.\n\nLists as CSV\nEvery list can be saved as a CSV file from the Reports menu. See Exporting lists as CSV.\n\nOther exports\nThe build pack can be saved as a web page. See The build pack.\nThe harness formboard exports DXF at 1:1 and SVG. See Exporting the formboard.\nThe vehicle view exports SVG and PNG. See Using the locations.\nYour components and blocks export as a library file. See Sharing components and blocks.\nThe revision comparison exports CSV. See Comparing revisions.","sections":[{"anchor":"","title":"Import and export","level":1,"start":0},{"anchor":"importing","title":"Importing","level":2,"start":199},{"anchor":"a-wire-list-from-a-spreadsheet","title":"A wire list from a spreadsheet","level":3,"start":331},{"anchor":"wireviz","title":"WireViz","level":3,"start":1629},{"anchor":"a-kicad-netlist","title":"A KiCad netlist","level":3,"start":1812},{"anchor":"measured-wire-lengths","title":"Measured wire lengths","level":3,"start":2493},{"anchor":"exporting-drawings","title":"Exporting drawings","level":2,"start":3276},{"anchor":"exporting-for-wireviz","title":"Exporting for WireViz","level":2,"start":3993},{"anchor":"a-read-only-web-page","title":"A read-only web page","level":2,"start":4241},{"anchor":"lists-as-csv","title":"Lists as CSV","level":2,"start":4644},{"anchor":"other-exports","title":"Other exports","level":2,"start":4747}]},
{"id":"language-and-theme","file":"19-language-and-theme.md","title":"Language and theme","html":"<h1 id=\"lm-language-and-theme\">Language and theme</h1>\n<h2 id=\"lm-language-and-theme--language\" data-anchor=\"language\">Language</h2>\n<p>LoomWright&#39;s menus, panels and messages are available in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>English</li>\n<li>Deutsch (German)</li>\n<li>Français (French)</li>\n<li>Español (Spanish)</li>\n<li>Nederlands (Dutch)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The first time it starts, LoomWright uses the language of your computer or browser if it is one of these, and English otherwise.</p>\n<p>To change it, choose <strong>View › Language</strong>, pick a language and click <strong>Apply</strong>. LoomWright reloads its window to switch. The projects you have open come back as they were, including unsaved changes, but the undo history starts afresh, so finish anything you might want to undo first.</p>\n<p>What changes language, and what does not:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Menus, buttons, panels, lists on screen and messages change.</li>\n<li>The words you type, such as part names, circuit names, harnesses and notes, stay as you typed them.</li>\n<li>Drawings (the title block and colour key), the build pack, CSV exports and the read-only web page are in English.</li>\n<li>The component builder and this manual are in English only.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote class=\"lm-note\"><p>The translations are a first pass and will have mistakes. Corrections are welcome: see the contributing guide in the LoomWright source.</p></blockquote>\n<h2 id=\"lm-language-and-theme--light-and-dark-theme\" data-anchor=\"light-and-dark-theme\">Light and dark theme</h2>\n<p>LoomWright has a light theme and a dark theme. To start with, it follows your computer&#39;s setting. To choose one yourself, click the moon button at the right-hand end of the title bar, or choose <strong>View › Dark theme</strong>. LoomWright remembers your choice.</p>\n<p>The drawing sheet stays white in both themes, so wire colours look the same as they will on paper.</p>","text":"Language and theme\n\nLanguage\nLoomWright's menus, panels and messages are available in:\nEnglish\nDeutsch (German)\nFrançais (French)\nEspañol (Spanish)\nNederlands (Dutch)\nThe first time it starts, LoomWright uses the language of your computer or browser if it is one of these, and English otherwise.\nTo change it, choose View › Language, pick a language and click Apply. LoomWright reloads its window to switch. The projects you have open come back as they were, including unsaved changes, but the undo history starts afresh, so finish anything you might want to undo first.\nWhat changes language, and what does not:\nMenus, buttons, panels, lists on screen and messages change.\nThe words you type, such as part names, circuit names, harnesses and notes, stay as you typed them.\nDrawings (the title block and colour key), the build pack, CSV exports and the read-only web page are in English.\nThe component builder and this manual are in English only.\nThe translations are a first pass and will have mistakes. Corrections are welcome: see the contributing guide in the LoomWright source.\n\nLight and dark theme\nLoomWright has a light theme and a dark theme. To start with, it follows your computer's setting. To choose one yourself, click the moon button at the right-hand end of the title bar, or choose View › Dark theme. LoomWright remembers your choice.\nThe drawing sheet stays white in both themes, so wire colours look the same as they will on paper.","sections":[{"anchor":"","title":"Language and theme","level":1,"start":0},{"anchor":"language","title":"Language","level":2,"start":20},{"anchor":"light-and-dark-theme","title":"Light and dark theme","level":2,"start":1084}]},
{"id":"keyboard-and-mouse","file":"20-keyboard-and-mouse.md","title":"Keyboard and mouse","html":"<h1 id=\"lm-keyboard-and-mouse\">Keyboard and mouse</h1>\n<p>This chapter lists every shortcut in LoomWright. <strong>Help › Keyboard and mouse</strong> shows the most used ones inside the app.</p>\n<p>On a Mac, use <kbd>Cmd</kbd> wherever this manual says <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>; both work.</p>\n<p>Single-letter shortcuts such as <kbd>R</kbd> and <kbd>T</kbd> do nothing while you are typing in a box; click an empty part of the sheet first.</p>\n<h2 id=\"lm-keyboard-and-mouse--files-and-windows\" data-anchor=\"files-and-windows\">Files and windows</h2>\n<div class=\"lm-table\"><table><thead><tr><th>Keys</th><th>What they do</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td><kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>S</kbd></td><td>Save</td></tr>\n<tr><td><kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>S</kbd></td><td>Save as</td></tr>\n<tr><td><kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>O</kbd></td><td>Open a project</td></tr>\n<tr><td><kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>P</kbd></td><td>Print or save PDF (all sheets)</td></tr>\n<tr><td><kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>K</kbd></td><td>Search commands, sheets, parts and wires</td></tr>\n<tr><td><kbd>F1</kbd></td><td>Open this manual</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"lm-keyboard-and-mouse--editing\" data-anchor=\"editing\">Editing</h2>\n<div class=\"lm-table\"><table><thead><tr><th>Keys</th><th>What they do</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td><kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Z</kbd></td><td>Undo</td></tr>\n<tr><td><kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>Z</kbd> or <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Y</kbd></td><td>Redo</td></tr>\n<tr><td><kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>C</kbd></td><td>Copy the selected parts and the wires between them</td></tr>\n<tr><td><kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>X</kbd></td><td>Cut</td></tr>\n<tr><td><kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>V</kbd></td><td>Paste</td></tr>\n<tr><td><kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>D</kbd></td><td>Duplicate</td></tr>\n<tr><td><kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>A</kbd></td><td>Select everything on the sheet</td></tr>\n<tr><td><kbd>Delete</kbd> or <kbd>Backspace</kbd></td><td>Delete the selection</td></tr>\n<tr><td><kbd>R</kbd></td><td>Rotate the selection a quarter turn clockwise</td></tr>\n<tr><td><kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>R</kbd></td><td>Rotate the other way</td></tr>\n<tr><td><kbd>F</kbd></td><td>Flip the selection</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Arrow keys</td><td>Move the selection 10 units</td></tr>\n<tr><td><kbd>Shift</kbd>+arrow keys</td><td>Move the selection 50 units</td></tr>\n<tr><td><kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>F</kbd></td><td>Find</td></tr>\n<tr><td><kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>H</kbd></td><td>Find and replace</td></tr>\n<tr><td><kbd>Esc</kbd></td><td>Stop drawing a wire; otherwise clear the trace; otherwise clear the selection</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"lm-keyboard-and-mouse--circuits\" data-anchor=\"circuits\">Circuits</h2>\n<div class=\"lm-table\"><table><thead><tr><th>Keys</th><th>What they do</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td><kbd>T</kbd></td><td>Trace the selected wire&#39;s circuit</td></tr>\n<tr><td><kbd>Esc</kbd></td><td>Clear the trace, or stop the live test</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"lm-keyboard-and-mouse--moving-around\" data-anchor=\"moving-around\">Moving around</h2>\n<div class=\"lm-table\"><table><thead><tr><th>Keys</th><th>What they do</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td><kbd>+</kbd> or <kbd>=</kbd></td><td>Zoom in</td></tr>\n<tr><td><kbd>-</kbd></td><td>Zoom out</td></tr>\n<tr><td><kbd>0</kbd></td><td>Fit the sheet to the window</td></tr>\n<tr><td><kbd>PgDn</kbd></td><td>Next sheet</td></tr>\n<tr><td><kbd>PgUp</kbd></td><td>Previous sheet</td></tr>\n<tr><td><kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Tab</kbd>, <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>Tab</kbd></td><td>Next and previous sheet (in the desktop app; a browser keeps these keys for its own tabs)</td></tr>\n<tr><td><kbd>Space</kbd> + drag</td><td>Pan</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Zoom, pan and the sheet keys also work during the live test.</p>\n<h2 id=\"lm-keyboard-and-mouse--mouse-on-the-sheet\" data-anchor=\"mouse-on-the-sheet\">Mouse on the sheet</h2>\n<div class=\"lm-table\"><table><thead><tr><th>Action</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Click a pin, then another pin</td><td>Draw a wire</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Click empty space while drawing a wire</td><td>Add a bend point</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Click the starting pin again while drawing</td><td>Stop drawing the wire</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Press on a pin and drag</td><td>Move the part</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Click a part or wire</td><td>Select it</td></tr>\n<tr><td><kbd>Shift</kbd>+click</td><td>Add to or remove from the selection</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Drag across empty space</td><td>Select the parts inside the box, and the wires between them</td></tr>\n<tr><td><kbd>Shift</kbd>+drag across empty space</td><td>Add to the selection</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Drag a part</td><td>Move the selection</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Double-click a part</td><td>Go to its first field in Properties</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Double-click a wire</td><td>Add a bend point there</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Drag a bend point</td><td>Move it</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Double-click a bend point</td><td>Remove it</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Drag the square handle on a selected wire</td><td>Move the middle run of the wire</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Drag a wire label</td><td>Move the label; a leader line points back to the wire</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Double-click a moved wire label</td><td>Put it back</td></tr>\n<tr><td><kbd>Alt</kbd>+click a wire or pin</td><td>Trace the circuit across every sheet</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Click a cross-reference such as <code>→ 2/B4</code></td><td>Go to the other end</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Scroll</td><td>Pan up and down (sideways too, on a trackpad)</td></tr>\n<tr><td><kbd>Shift</kbd>+scroll</td><td>Pan sideways</td></tr>\n<tr><td><kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+scroll, or pinch on a trackpad</td><td>Zoom around the pointer</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Drag with the middle button</td><td>Pan</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Click a part during the live test</td><td>Move it to its next position</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"lm-keyboard-and-mouse--parts-pane\" data-anchor=\"parts-pane\">Parts pane</h2>\n<div class=\"lm-table\"><table><thead><tr><th>Action</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Drag a part or block onto the sheet</td><td>Place it there</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Click a part or block</td><td>Place it in the middle of the view</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"lm-keyboard-and-mouse--search-ctrlk\" data-anchor=\"search-ctrlk\">Search (Ctrl+K)</h2>\n<div class=\"lm-table\"><table><thead><tr><th>Keys</th><th>What they do</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td><kbd>↑</kbd> <kbd>↓</kbd></td><td>Move through the list</td></tr>\n<tr><td><kbd>Enter</kbd></td><td>Choose</td></tr>\n<tr><td><kbd>Esc</kbd></td><td>Close</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"lm-keyboard-and-mouse--component-builder\" data-anchor=\"component-builder\">Component builder</h2>\n<div class=\"lm-table\"><table><thead><tr><th>Keys</th><th>What they do</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td><kbd>V</kbd></td><td>Select and move</td></tr>\n<tr><td><kbd>L</kbd></td><td>Line</td></tr>\n<tr><td><kbd>B</kbd></td><td>Rectangle</td></tr>\n<tr><td><kbd>C</kbd></td><td>Circle</td></tr>\n<tr><td><kbd>T</kbd></td><td>Text</td></tr>\n<tr><td><kbd>P</kbd></td><td>Pin</td></tr>\n<tr><td><kbd>Delete</kbd> or <kbd>Backspace</kbd></td><td>Delete the selected shape or pin</td></tr>\n<tr><td><kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Z</kbd></td><td>Undo</td></tr>\n<tr><td><kbd>Esc</kbd></td><td>Back to Select and move</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"lm-keyboard-and-mouse--formboard-and-vehicle-views\" data-anchor=\"formboard-and-vehicle-views\">Formboard and vehicle views</h2>\n<div class=\"lm-table\"><table><thead><tr><th>Keys or action</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td><kbd>Esc</kbd></td><td>Cancel joining or placing; otherwise close the view</td></tr>\n<tr><td><kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Z</kbd>, <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>Z</kbd></td><td>Undo, redo</td></tr>\n<tr><td><kbd>Delete</kbd></td><td>Delete the selected branch or point (formboard)</td></tr>\n<tr><td><kbd>0</kbd></td><td>Fit to the window</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Drag empty space, or scroll</td><td>Pan</td></tr>\n<tr><td><kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+scroll</td><td>Zoom</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"lm-keyboard-and-mouse--this-manual\" data-anchor=\"this-manual\">This manual</h2>\n<div class=\"lm-table\"><table><thead><tr><th>Keys</th><th>What they do</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td><kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>F</kbd>, <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>K</kbd> or <kbd>/</kbd></td><td>Search the manual</td></tr>\n<tr><td><kbd>Enter</kbd> in the search box</td><td>Open the first result</td></tr>\n<tr><td><kbd>↑</kbd> <kbd>↓</kbd></td><td>Move through the contents or the results</td></tr>\n<tr><td><kbd>Alt</kbd>+<kbd>←</kbd>, <kbd>Alt</kbd>+<kbd>→</kbd></td><td>Back and forward</td></tr>\n<tr><td><kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>P</kbd></td><td>Print the chapter</td></tr>\n<tr><td><kbd>Esc</kbd></td><td>In the search box, clear the search; otherwise close the manual</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>","text":"Keyboard and mouse\nThis chapter lists every shortcut in LoomWright. Help › Keyboard and mouse shows the most used ones inside the app.\nOn a Mac, use Cmd wherever this manual says Ctrl; both work.\nSingle-letter shortcuts such as R and T do nothing while you are typing in a box; click an empty part of the sheet first.\n\nFiles and windows\nKeys What they do\nCtrl+S Save\nCtrl+Shift+S Save as\nCtrl+O Open a project\nCtrl+P Print or save PDF (all sheets)\nCtrl+K Search commands, sheets, parts and wires\nF1 Open this manual\n\nEditing\nKeys What they do\nCtrl+Z Undo\nCtrl+Shift+Z or Ctrl+Y Redo\nCtrl+C Copy the selected parts and the wires between them\nCtrl+X Cut\nCtrl+V Paste\nCtrl+D Duplicate\nCtrl+A Select everything on the sheet\nDelete or Backspace Delete the selection\nR Rotate the selection a quarter turn clockwise\nShift+R Rotate the other way\nF Flip the selection\nArrow keys Move the selection 10 units\nShift+arrow keys Move the selection 50 units\nCtrl+F Find\nCtrl+H Find and replace\nEsc Stop drawing a wire; otherwise clear the trace; otherwise clear the selection\n\nCircuits\nKeys What they do\nT Trace the selected wire's circuit\nEsc Clear the trace, or stop the live test\n\nMoving around\nKeys What they do\n+ or = Zoom in\n- Zoom out\n0 Fit the sheet to the window\nPgDn Next sheet\nPgUp Previous sheet\nCtrl+Tab, Ctrl+Shift+Tab Next and previous sheet (in the desktop app; a browser keeps these keys for its own tabs)\nSpace + drag Pan\nZoom, pan and the sheet keys also work during the live test.\n\nMouse on the sheet\nAction What it does\nClick a pin, then another pin Draw a wire\nClick empty space while drawing a wire Add a bend point\nClick the starting pin again while drawing Stop drawing the wire\nPress on a pin and drag Move the part\nClick a part or wire Select it\nShift+click Add to or remove from the selection\nDrag across empty space Select the parts inside the box, and the wires between them\nShift+drag across empty space Add to the selection\nDrag a part Move the selection\nDouble-click a part Go to its first field in Properties\nDouble-click a wire Add a bend point there\nDrag a bend point Move it\nDouble-click a bend point Remove it\nDrag the square handle on a selected wire Move the middle run of the wire\nDrag a wire label Move the label; a leader line points back to the wire\nDouble-click a moved wire label Put it back\nAlt+click a wire or pin Trace the circuit across every sheet\nClick a cross-reference such as → 2/B4 Go to the other end\nScroll Pan up and down (sideways too, on a trackpad)\nShift+scroll Pan sideways\nCtrl+scroll, or pinch on a trackpad Zoom around the pointer\nDrag with the middle button Pan\nClick a part during the live test Move it to its next position\n\nParts pane\nAction What it does\nDrag a part or block onto the sheet Place it there\nClick a part or block Place it in the middle of the view\n\nSearch (Ctrl+K)\nKeys What they do\n↑ ↓ Move through the list\nEnter Choose\nEsc Close\n\nComponent builder\nKeys What they do\nV Select and move\nL Line\nB Rectangle\nC Circle\nT Text\nP Pin\nDelete or Backspace Delete the selected shape or pin\nCtrl+Z Undo\nEsc Back to Select and move\n\nFormboard and vehicle views\nKeys or action What it does\nEsc Cancel joining or placing; otherwise close the view\nCtrl+Z, Ctrl+Shift+Z Undo, redo\nDelete Delete the selected branch or point (formboard)\n0 Fit to the window\nDrag empty space, or scroll Pan\nCtrl+scroll Zoom\n\nThis manual\nKeys What they do\nCtrl+F, Ctrl+K or / Search the manual\nEnter in the search box Open the first result\n↑ ↓ Move through the contents or the results\nAlt+←, Alt+→ Back and forward\nCtrl+P Print the chapter\nEsc In the search box, clear the search; otherwise close the manual","sections":[{"anchor":"","title":"Keyboard and mouse","level":1,"start":0},{"anchor":"files-and-windows","title":"Files and windows","level":2,"start":319},{"anchor":"editing","title":"Editing","level":2,"start":517},{"anchor":"circuits","title":"Circuits","level":2,"start":1062},{"anchor":"moving-around","title":"Moving around","level":2,"start":1169},{"anchor":"mouse-on-the-sheet","title":"Mouse on the sheet","level":2,"start":1487},{"anchor":"parts-pane","title":"Parts pane","level":2,"start":2678},{"anchor":"search-ctrlk","title":"Search (Ctrl+K)","level":2,"start":2818},{"anchor":"component-builder","title":"Component builder","level":2,"start":2902},{"anchor":"formboard-and-vehicle-views","title":"Formboard and vehicle views","level":2,"start":3091},{"anchor":"this-manual","title":"This manual","level":2,"start":3360}]},
{"id":"claude-add-on","file":"21-claude-add-on.md","title":"The Claude add-on","html":"<h1 id=\"lm-claude-add-on\">The Claude add-on</h1>\n<p>LoomWright works fully on its own, offline. If you use Claude, an optional add-on lets Claude read and edit your projects for you: you describe a circuit in plain English and Claude draws it, checks it, or answers questions about it.</p>\n<p><strong>Help › Use with Claude</strong> (or the <strong>Claude</strong> button at the bottom of the tool rail) shows example requests and how to set it up. The full instructions, and the list of everything Claude can do, are in the LoomWright source in <span class=\"lm-path\" title=\"In the LoomWright source: docs/claude-addon.md\">the Claude add-on guide <code>docs/claude-addon.md</code></span>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"lm-claude-add-on--what-it-can-do\" data-anchor=\"what-it-can-do\">What it can do</h2>\n<p>Some examples of what you can ask:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>&quot;Add a second sheet for the rear work lights: two LED floods on a relay, switched from the dash, 15 A fuse from FB1 way 5.&quot;</li>\n<li>&quot;Here is a picture of my switch panel&#39;s pinout. Make it a custom component and save it to my library.&quot; (with the picture attached)</li>\n<li>&quot;I only stock red, brown/red, black, green and purple/white. Recolour sheet 2 using just those and tell me where colours are reused.&quot;</li>\n<li>&quot;Check the load on every fuse and tell me which are over 80%.&quot;</li>\n<li>&quot;Trace the AUX FEED circuit and list every wire on it.&quot;</li>\n<li>&quot;Issue revision B, &#39;fridge feed moved to 6 mm²&#39;, and tell me what changed since A.&quot;</li>\n<li>&quot;Give me the cut list for the engine bay harness.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Claude uses the same component library, checks, trace, live test, reports and revision history as the app.</p>\n<h2 id=\"lm-claude-add-on--how-it-works-with-the-app\" data-anchor=\"how-it-works-with-the-app\">How it works with the app</h2>\n<p>The add-on is a small program (an MCP server) in the <code>mcp</code> folder of the LoomWright source. It is not part of the installers. It needs Node.js 18 or newer.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Claude reads and writes the same <code>.loom</code> files you open in LoomWright.</li>\n<li>If the project is open in the <strong>desktop app</strong>, the app reloads it as soon as Claude saves, and says <strong>Updated from file. Ctrl+Z undoes the change.</strong> <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Z</kbd> undoes everything Claude changed in that save. See <a href=\"#lm-files-and-saving--when-the-file-changes-on-disk\" data-chapter=\"files-and-saving\" data-anchor=\"when-the-file-changes-on-disk\">When the file changes on disk</a>.</li>\n<li>Components Claude makes are saved to the same library as <strong>My components</strong>, and appear in the Parts pane when you switch back to LoomWright.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The browser version cannot notice when a file changes, so use the desktop app with the add-on.</p>\n<h2 id=\"lm-claude-add-on--checking-claudes-work\" data-anchor=\"checking-claudes-work\">Checking Claude&#39;s work</h2>\n<p>Treat what Claude draws as you would a drawing from someone else: look it over before you build from it. Trace the circuits it added, run the live test, read the design checks, and check any component made from a picture against the real part&#39;s pinout.</p>","text":"The Claude add-on\nLoomWright works fully on its own, offline. If you use Claude, an optional add-on lets Claude read and edit your projects for you: you describe a circuit in plain English and Claude draws it, checks it, or answers questions about it.\nHelp › Use with Claude (or the Claude button at the bottom of the tool rail) shows example requests and how to set it up. The full instructions, and the list of everything Claude can do, are in the LoomWright source in the Claude add-on guide docs/claude-addon.md.\n\nWhat it can do\nSome examples of what you can ask:\n\"Add a second sheet for the rear work lights: two LED floods on a relay, switched from the dash, 15 A fuse from FB1 way 5.\"\n\"Here is a picture of my switch panel's pinout. Make it a custom component and save it to my library.\" (with the picture attached)\n\"I only stock red, brown/red, black, green and purple/white. Recolour sheet 2 using just those and tell me where colours are reused.\"\n\"Check the load on every fuse and tell me which are over 80%.\"\n\"Trace the AUX FEED circuit and list every wire on it.\"\n\"Issue revision B, 'fridge feed moved to 6 mm²', and tell me what changed since A.\"\n\"Give me the cut list for the engine bay harness.\"\nClaude uses the same component library, checks, trace, live test, reports and revision history as the app.\n\nHow it works with the app\nThe add-on is a small program (an MCP server) in the mcp folder of the LoomWright source. It is not part of the installers. It needs Node.js 18 or newer.\nClaude reads and writes the same .loom files you open in LoomWright.\nIf the project is open in the desktop app, the app reloads it as soon as Claude saves, and says Updated from file. Ctrl+Z undoes the change. Ctrl+Z undoes everything Claude changed in that save. See When the file changes on disk.\nComponents Claude makes are saved to the same library as My components, and appear in the Parts pane when you switch back to LoomWright.\nThe browser version cannot notice when a file changes, so use the desktop app with the add-on.\n\nChecking Claude's work\nTreat what Claude draws as you would a drawing from someone else: look it over before you build from it. Trace the circuits it added, run the live test, read the design checks, and check any component made from a picture against the real part's pinout.","sections":[{"anchor":"","title":"The Claude add-on","level":1,"start":0},{"anchor":"what-it-can-do","title":"What it can do","level":2,"start":518},{"anchor":"how-it-works-with-the-app","title":"How it works with the app","level":2,"start":1319},{"anchor":"checking-claudes-work","title":"Checking Claude's work","level":2,"start":2031}]},
{"id":"troubleshooting","file":"22-troubleshooting.md","title":"Troubleshooting and questions","html":"<h1 id=\"lm-troubleshooting\">Troubleshooting and questions</h1>\n<h2 id=\"lm-troubleshooting--installing-and-starting\" data-anchor=\"installing-and-starting\">Installing and starting</h2>\n<h3 id=\"lm-troubleshooting--windows-says-it-protected-my-pc\" data-anchor=\"windows-says-it-protected-my-pc\">Windows says it protected my PC</h3>\n<p>The installers are not code-signed yet, so Windows does not recognise the publisher. Click <strong>More info</strong>, then <strong>Run anyway</strong>. You only need to do this once.</p>\n<h3 id=\"lm-troubleshooting--the-mac-says-loomwright-cannot-be-opened\" data-anchor=\"the-mac-says-loomwright-cannot-be-opened\">The Mac says LoomWright cannot be opened</h3>\n<p>Right-click (or Control-click) LoomWright in Applications, choose <strong>Open</strong>, then <strong>Open</strong> again. After the first time it opens normally.</p>\n<h3 id=\"lm-troubleshooting--the-portable-copy-does-not-start\" data-anchor=\"the-portable-copy-does-not-start\">The portable copy does not start</h3>\n<p>It needs the Microsoft Edge WebView2 runtime. Windows 10 and 11 normally have it; if yours does not, install the WebView2 runtime from Microsoft and try again. Make sure you unzipped the whole folder somewhere you can write to, not inside the zip file.</p>\n<h3 id=\"lm-troubleshooting--double-clicking-a-loom-file-on-a-mac-does-not-open-it\" data-anchor=\"double-clicking-a-loom-file-on-a-mac-does-not-open-it\">Double-clicking a .loom file on a Mac does not open it</h3>\n<p>On a Mac, open projects from inside LoomWright with <strong>File › Open</strong> (<kbd>Cmd</kbd>+<kbd>O</kbd>).</p>\n<h3 id=\"lm-troubleshooting--loomwright-opened-the-example-project\" data-anchor=\"loomwright-opened-the-example-project\">LoomWright opened the example project</h3>\n<p>It does that the first time it starts, and when no other project is open. Your own projects are where you saved them; open them with <strong>File › Open</strong>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"lm-troubleshooting--saving-and-files\" data-anchor=\"saving-and-files\">Saving and files</h2>\n<h3 id=\"lm-troubleshooting--i-saved-in-the-browser-but-cannot-find-the-file\" data-anchor=\"i-saved-in-the-browser-but-cannot-find-the-file\">I saved in the browser, but cannot find the file</h3>\n<p>In Firefox and Safari, Save downloads the file to your Downloads folder. In Chrome and Edge, Save asks where to put it each time. See <a href=\"#lm-files-and-saving--saving\" data-chapter=\"files-and-saving\" data-anchor=\"saving\">Saving</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"lm-troubleshooting--my-components-blocks-or-remembered-projects-have-gone\" data-anchor=\"my-components-blocks-or-remembered-projects-have-gone\">My components, blocks or remembered projects have gone</h3>\n<p>They are kept on the computer, not in your project files. Check that you are using the same copy of LoomWright as before:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>the desktop app and a browser keep separate libraries,</li>\n<li>each browser, and each private window, has its own storage, and clearing browsing data clears it,</li>\n<li>the portable copy keeps its own library in <code>LoomWright-data</code> beside the program.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Help › About LoomWright</strong> shows where the desktop app keeps your library. Use <strong>Tools › Export my components and blocks</strong> now and then to keep a copy. Components used in a project are also stored in that project&#39;s file.</p>\n<h3 id=\"lm-troubleshooting--i-need-an-earlier-version-of-my-project\" data-anchor=\"i-need-an-earlier-version-of-my-project\">I need an earlier version of my project</h3>\n<p>In order of how far back they go:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Z</kbd> undoes the last 200 changes in this session.</li>\n<li><strong>Save points</strong> in the History pane hold copies from every save and every ten minutes of work, on this computer.</li>\n<li><strong>Issued revisions</strong> in the History pane hold every revision you issued, in the project file.</li>\n<li>Your own backups, or version control such as Git.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>See <a href=\"#lm-revisions-and-save-points\" data-chapter=\"revisions-and-save-points\" data-anchor=\"\">Revision history and save points</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"lm-troubleshooting--changes-made-by-claude-do-not-appear\" data-anchor=\"changes-made-by-claude-do-not-appear\">Changes made by Claude do not appear</h3>\n<p>The app only notices changes to the file it has open, and only in the desktop app. Check that Claude saved to the same file you have open. If you had unsaved changes, LoomWright offers <strong>Reload</strong> instead of reloading by itself.</p>\n<h2 id=\"lm-troubleshooting--drawing\" data-anchor=\"drawing\">Drawing</h2>\n<h3 id=\"lm-troubleshooting--clicking-a-pin-moves-the-part-instead-of-starting-a-wire\" data-anchor=\"clicking-a-pin-moves-the-part-instead-of-starting-a-wire\">Clicking a pin moves the part instead of starting a wire</h3>\n<p>If the pointer moves while the button is down, LoomWright takes it as a drag and moves the part. Click without moving. Point at the pin first and wait for the blue circle.</p>\n<h3 id=\"lm-troubleshooting--clicking-on-the-sheet-adds-bends-instead-of-selecting\" data-anchor=\"clicking-on-the-sheet-adds-bends-instead-of-selecting\">Clicking on the sheet adds bends instead of selecting</h3>\n<p>You are still drawing a wire: each click on empty space adds a bend point. Click a pin to finish it, or press <kbd>Esc</kbd> to stop.</p>\n<h3 id=\"lm-troubleshooting--keyboard-shortcuts-do-nothing\" data-anchor=\"keyboard-shortcuts-do-nothing\">Keyboard shortcuts do nothing</h3>\n<p>The cursor is probably in a text box, where letters are typed instead. Click an empty part of the sheet and try again. In a browser, <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Tab</kbd> switches the browser&#39;s own tabs; use <kbd>PgDn</kbd> and <kbd>PgUp</kbd> for sheets.</p>\n<h3 id=\"lm-troubleshooting--wires-are-grey-and-tracers-have-disappeared\" data-anchor=\"wires-are-grey-and-tracers-have-disappeared\">Wires are grey and tracers have disappeared</h3>\n<p>Black and white drawing is on. Turn it off with <strong>View › Black and white drawings</strong>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"lm-troubleshooting--wire-numbers-and-codes-are-not-shown-on-the-drawing\" data-anchor=\"wire-numbers-and-codes-are-not-shown-on-the-drawing\">Wire numbers and codes are not shown on the drawing</h3>\n<p><strong>Wire labels</strong> is set to <strong>None</strong> in the drawing settings. Click an empty part of the sheet and choose another setting.</p>\n<h3 id=\"lm-troubleshooting--the-colour-i-want-is-not-in-the-colour-list\" data-anchor=\"the-colour-i-want-is-not-in-the-colour-list\">The colour I want is not in the colour list</h3>\n<p><strong>Only offer colours I stock</strong> is on, and that colour is not ticked as in stock. Tick it in <strong>Tools › Wire colours and stock</strong>, or turn the setting off.</p>\n<h3 id=\"lm-troubleshooting--pasted-parts-have-different-references\" data-anchor=\"pasted-parts-have-different-references\">Pasted parts have different references</h3>\n<p>That is deliberate: pasted parts and blocks get new references and wire numbers, so nothing clashes. To draw the same part on another sheet, give the copy the same reference by hand. See <a href=\"#lm-projects-and-sheets--the-same-part-on-several-sheets\" data-chapter=\"projects-and-sheets\" data-anchor=\"the-same-part-on-several-sheets\">The same part on several sheets</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"lm-troubleshooting--circuits-and-checks\" data-anchor=\"circuits-and-checks\">Circuits and checks</h2>\n<h3 id=\"lm-troubleshooting--a-trace-stops-where-the-circuit-goes-to-another-sheet\" data-anchor=\"a-trace-stops-where-the-circuit-goes-to-another-sheet\">A trace stops where the circuit goes to another sheet</h3>\n<p>The two off-sheet references need exactly the same <strong>Link name</strong>, and two drawings of the same part need the same reference and the same kind of part. Check the <strong>Continues on</strong> or <strong>Also drawn on</strong> list in the part&#39;s Properties.</p>\n<h3 id=\"lm-troubleshooting--a-trace-stops-at-a-fuse-or-switch\" data-anchor=\"a-trace-stops-at-a-fuse-or-switch\">A trace stops at a fuse or switch</h3>\n<p>That is how the trace works: it shows one circuit, and fuses, switches, relays and loads divide circuits. Trace the other side too, or use the live test to see a whole path from the battery. See <a href=\"#lm-trace-and-live-test--what-counts-as-one-circuit\" data-chapter=\"trace-and-live-test\" data-anchor=\"what-counts-as-one-circuit\">What counts as one circuit</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"lm-troubleshooting--i-cannot-switch-a-relay-in-the-live-test\" data-anchor=\"i-cannot-switch-a-relay-in-the-live-test\">I cannot switch a relay in the live test</h3>\n<p>Relays operate themselves when their coil has a feed and an earth. Operate the switch that feeds the coil.</p>\n<h3 id=\"lm-troubleshooting--a-load-does-not-come-on-in-the-live-test\" data-anchor=\"a-load-does-not-come-on-in-the-live-test\">A load does not come on in the live test</h3>\n<p>It needs a path from a battery through closed switches and good fuses to its feed pin, and a path from its earth pin to earth. Trace both sides to find the gap. A battery isolator starts <strong>On</strong>; an alternator or solar panel only supplies power when set to <strong>Engine running</strong> or <strong>Sun</strong>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"lm-troubleshooting--the-fuse-schedule-shows-0-a-loads\" data-anchor=\"the-fuse-schedule-shows-0-a-loads\">The fuse schedule shows 0 A loads</h3>\n<p>LoomWright only knows a load&#39;s current if the part has a current or wattage. Fill in lamps&#39; wattage and loads&#39; current. See <a href=\"#lm-fuses-and-loads--how-loomwright-works-out-a-load\" data-chapter=\"fuses-and-loads\" data-anchor=\"how-loomwright-works-out-a-load\">How LoomWright works out a load</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"lm-troubleshooting--imported-lengths-are-a-thousand-times-too-big-or-too-small\" data-anchor=\"imported-lengths-are-a-thousand-times-too-big-or-too-small\">Imported lengths are a thousand times too big or too small</h3>\n<p>LoomWright guessed the unit. Undo the import, add a header row such as <code>Wire,Length mm</code> or <code>Wire,Length m</code>, and import again. See <a href=\"#lm-import-and-export--measured-wire-lengths\" data-chapter=\"import-and-export\" data-anchor=\"measured-wire-lengths\">Measured wire lengths</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"lm-troubleshooting--printing\" data-anchor=\"printing\">Printing</h2>\n<h3 id=\"lm-troubleshooting--print-is-missing-or-greyed-out\" data-anchor=\"print-is-missing-or-greyed-out\">Print is missing or greyed out</h3>\n<p>When LoomWright runs inside a preview that cannot print, the print commands are not available. Use <strong>Save as web page</strong> in the build pack dialog, open the page in a browser and print from there.</p>\n<h3 id=\"lm-troubleshooting--the-labels-do-not-line-up-with-my-label-sheet\" data-anchor=\"the-labels-do-not-line-up-with-my-label-sheet\">The labels do not line up with my label sheet</h3>\n<p>Printers and label sheets differ. Print a test page on plain paper and hold it against a label sheet; check the printer is not scaling the page (choose 100% or <strong>Actual size</strong> in the print window).</p>\n<h2 id=\"lm-troubleshooting--reporting-a-problem\" data-anchor=\"reporting-a-problem\">Reporting a problem</h2>\n<p>If something does not work as this manual says, please report it on the project&#39;s GitHub page. Say what you did, what you expected and what happened, which version you have (it is shown at the right-hand end of the status bar and in <strong>Help › About LoomWright</strong>), and whether you use the desktop app or a browser. A small <code>.loom</code> file that shows the problem helps a great deal.</p>","text":"Troubleshooting and questions\n\nInstalling and starting\n\nWindows says it protected my PC\nThe installers are not code-signed yet, so Windows does not recognise the publisher. Click More info, then Run anyway. You only need to do this once.\n\nThe Mac says LoomWright cannot be opened\nRight-click (or Control-click) LoomWright in Applications, choose Open, then Open again. After the first time it opens normally.\n\nThe portable copy does not start\nIt needs the Microsoft Edge WebView2 runtime. Windows 10 and 11 normally have it; if yours does not, install the WebView2 runtime from Microsoft and try again. Make sure you unzipped the whole folder somewhere you can write to, not inside the zip file.\n\nDouble-clicking a .loom file on a Mac does not open it\nOn a Mac, open projects from inside LoomWright with File › Open (Cmd+O).\n\nLoomWright opened the example project\nIt does that the first time it starts, and when no other project is open. Your own projects are where you saved them; open them with File › Open.\n\nSaving and files\n\nI saved in the browser, but cannot find the file\nIn Firefox and Safari, Save downloads the file to your Downloads folder. In Chrome and Edge, Save asks where to put it each time. See Saving.\n\nMy components, blocks or remembered projects have gone\nThey are kept on the computer, not in your project files. Check that you are using the same copy of LoomWright as before:\nthe desktop app and a browser keep separate libraries,\neach browser, and each private window, has its own storage, and clearing browsing data clears it,\nthe portable copy keeps its own library in LoomWright-data beside the program.\nHelp › About LoomWright shows where the desktop app keeps your library. Use Tools › Export my components and blocks now and then to keep a copy. Components used in a project are also stored in that project's file.\n\nI need an earlier version of my project\nIn order of how far back they go:\nCtrl+Z undoes the last 200 changes in this session.\nSave points in the History pane hold copies from every save and every ten minutes of work, on this computer.\nIssued revisions in the History pane hold every revision you issued, in the project file.\nYour own backups, or version control such as Git.\nSee Revision history and save points.\n\nChanges made by Claude do not appear\nThe app only notices changes to the file it has open, and only in the desktop app. Check that Claude saved to the same file you have open. If you had unsaved changes, LoomWright offers Reload instead of reloading by itself.\n\nDrawing\n\nClicking a pin moves the part instead of starting a wire\nIf the pointer moves while the button is down, LoomWright takes it as a drag and moves the part. Click without moving. Point at the pin first and wait for the blue circle.\n\nClicking on the sheet adds bends instead of selecting\nYou are still drawing a wire: each click on empty space adds a bend point. Click a pin to finish it, or press Esc to stop.\n\nKeyboard shortcuts do nothing\nThe cursor is probably in a text box, where letters are typed instead. Click an empty part of the sheet and try again. In a browser, Ctrl+Tab switches the browser's own tabs; use PgDn and PgUp for sheets.\n\nWires are grey and tracers have disappeared\nBlack and white drawing is on. Turn it off with View › Black and white drawings.\n\nWire numbers and codes are not shown on the drawing\nWire labels is set to None in the drawing settings. Click an empty part of the sheet and choose another setting.\n\nThe colour I want is not in the colour list\nOnly offer colours I stock is on, and that colour is not ticked as in stock. Tick it in Tools › Wire colours and stock, or turn the setting off.\n\nPasted parts have different references\nThat is deliberate: pasted parts and blocks get new references and wire numbers, so nothing clashes. To draw the same part on another sheet, give the copy the same reference by hand. See The same part on several sheets.\n\nCircuits and checks\n\nA trace stops where the circuit goes to another sheet\nThe two off-sheet references need exactly the same Link name, and two drawings of the same part need the same reference and the same kind of part. Check the Continues on or Also drawn on list in the part's Properties.\n\nA trace stops at a fuse or switch\nThat is how the trace works: it shows one circuit, and fuses, switches, relays and loads divide circuits. Trace the other side too, or use the live test to see a whole path from the battery. See What counts as one circuit.\n\nI cannot switch a relay in the live test\nRelays operate themselves when their coil has a feed and an earth. Operate the switch that feeds the coil.\n\nA load does not come on in the live test\nIt needs a path from a battery through closed switches and good fuses to its feed pin, and a path from its earth pin to earth. Trace both sides to find the gap. A battery isolator starts On; an alternator or solar panel only supplies power when set to Engine running or Sun.\n\nThe fuse schedule shows 0 A loads\nLoomWright only knows a load's current if the part has a current or wattage. Fill in lamps' wattage and loads' current. See How LoomWright works out a load.\n\nImported lengths are a thousand times too big or too small\nLoomWright guessed the unit. Undo the import, add a header row such as Wire,Length mm or Wire,Length m, and import again. See Measured wire lengths.\n\nPrinting\n\nPrint is missing or greyed out\nWhen LoomWright runs inside a preview that cannot print, the print commands are not available. Use Save as web page in the build pack dialog, open the page in a browser and print from there.\n\nThe labels do not line up with my label sheet\nPrinters and label sheets differ. Print a test page on plain paper and hold it against a label sheet; check the printer is not scaling the page (choose 100% or Actual size in the print window).\n\nReporting a problem\nIf something does not work as this manual says, please report it on the project's GitHub page. Say what you did, what you expected and what happened, which version you have (it is shown at the right-hand end of the status bar and in Help › About LoomWright), and whether you use the desktop app or a browser. A small .loom file that shows the problem helps a great deal.","sections":[{"anchor":"","title":"Troubleshooting and questions","level":1,"start":0},{"anchor":"installing-and-starting","title":"Installing and starting","level":2,"start":31},{"anchor":"windows-says-it-protected-my-pc","title":"Windows says it protected my PC","level":3,"start":56},{"anchor":"the-mac-says-loomwright-cannot-be-opened","title":"The Mac says LoomWright cannot be opened","level":3,"start":239},{"anchor":"the-portable-copy-does-not-start","title":"The portable copy does not start","level":3,"start":410},{"anchor":"double-clicking-a-loom-file-on-a-mac-does-not-open-it","title":"Double-clicking a .loom file on a Mac does not open it","level":3,"start":697},{"anchor":"loomwright-opened-the-example-project","title":"LoomWright opened the example project","level":3,"start":826},{"anchor":"saving-and-files","title":"Saving and files","level":2,"start":1011},{"anchor":"i-saved-in-the-browser-but-cannot-find-the-file","title":"I saved in the browser, but cannot find the file","level":3,"start":1029},{"anchor":"my-components-blocks-or-remembered-projects-have-gone","title":"My components, blocks or remembered projects have gone","level":3,"start":1221},{"anchor":"i-need-an-earlier-version-of-my-project","title":"I need an earlier version of my project","level":3,"start":1845},{"anchor":"changes-made-by-claude-do-not-appear","title":"Changes made by Claude do not appear","level":3,"start":2259},{"anchor":"drawing","title":"Drawing","level":2,"start":2521},{"anchor":"clicking-a-pin-moves-the-part-instead-of-starting-a-wire","title":"Clicking a pin moves the part instead of starting a wire","level":3,"start":2530},{"anchor":"clicking-on-the-sheet-adds-bends-instead-of-selecting","title":"Clicking on the sheet adds bends instead of selecting","level":3,"start":2760},{"anchor":"keyboard-shortcuts-do-nothing","title":"Keyboard shortcuts do nothing","level":3,"start":2938},{"anchor":"wires-are-grey-and-tracers-have-disappeared","title":"Wires are grey and tracers have disappeared","level":3,"start":3174},{"anchor":"wire-numbers-and-codes-are-not-shown-on-the-drawing","title":"Wire numbers and codes are not shown on the drawing","level":3,"start":3300},{"anchor":"the-colour-i-want-is-not-in-the-colour-list","title":"The colour I want is not in the colour list","level":3,"start":3466},{"anchor":"pasted-parts-have-different-references","title":"Pasted parts have different references","level":3,"start":3656},{"anchor":"circuits-and-checks","title":"Circuits and checks","level":2,"start":3916},{"anchor":"a-trace-stops-where-the-circuit-goes-to-another-sheet","title":"A trace stops where the circuit goes to another sheet","level":3,"start":3937},{"anchor":"a-trace-stops-at-a-fuse-or-switch","title":"A trace stops at a fuse or switch","level":3,"start":4210},{"anchor":"i-cannot-switch-a-relay-in-the-live-test","title":"I cannot switch a relay in the live test","level":3,"start":4468},{"anchor":"a-load-does-not-come-on-in-the-live-test","title":"A load does not come on in the live test","level":3,"start":4617},{"anchor":"the-fuse-schedule-shows-0-a-loads","title":"The fuse schedule shows 0 A loads","level":3,"start":4934},{"anchor":"imported-lengths-are-a-thousand-times-too-big-or-too-small","title":"Imported lengths are a thousand times too big or too small","level":3,"start":5126},{"anchor":"printing","title":"Printing","level":2,"start":5335},{"anchor":"print-is-missing-or-greyed-out","title":"Print is missing or greyed out","level":3,"start":5345},{"anchor":"the-labels-do-not-line-up-with-my-label-sheet","title":"The labels do not line up with my label sheet","level":3,"start":5568},{"anchor":"reporting-a-problem","title":"Reporting a problem","level":2,"start":5809}]},
{"id":"disclaimer","file":"23-disclaimer.md","title":"Disclaimer","html":"<h1 id=\"lm-disclaimer\">Disclaimer</h1>\n<p>Please read this before you build anything from a drawing made with LoomWright.</p>\n<h2 id=\"lm-disclaimer--a-planning-and-drawing-tool\" data-anchor=\"a-planning-and-drawing-tool\">A planning and drawing tool</h2>\n<p>LoomWright is a basic tool for planning wiring and drawing it. It helps you keep track of wires, colours, sizes, fuses and parts, and turns your drawing into lists for the bench. It does not design the wiring for you, and it does not know your vehicle, your cable or your installation.</p>\n<h2 id=\"lm-disclaimer--checks-and-hints-are-guidance-only\" data-anchor=\"checks-and-hints-are-guidance-only\">Checks and hints are guidance only</h2>\n<p>The design checks, the fuse and load figures, the wire size guide figures, the live test and every other hint in LoomWright are guidance only. They are rules of thumb based on the numbers you type in, and they can be wrong or miss things.</p>\n<p>They do not show that a design or an installation meets BS 7671, IEC 60204, the Building Regulations or any other standard, regulation or code.</p>\n<p>Check every wire size, fuse rating and part number against the cable, fuse and component makers&#39; data before you build.</p>\n<h2 id=\"lm-disclaimer--not-a-replacement-for-an-electrician-or-engineer\" data-anchor=\"not-a-replacement-for-an-electrician-or-engineer\">Not a replacement for an electrician or engineer</h2>\n<p>LoomWright does not replace a qualified electrician or engineer. If you are not sure that a circuit is safe, ask someone who is qualified to check it.</p>\n<h2 id=\"lm-disclaimer--mains-electricity-and-compressed-air\" data-anchor=\"mains-electricity-and-compressed-air\">Mains electricity and compressed air</h2>\n<p>Work on mains electricity, and work on compressed air systems, must be designed, installed, inspected and tested by a competent person. Do not rely on LoomWright for it.</p>\n<h2 id=\"lm-disclaimer--no-warranty\" data-anchor=\"no-warranty\">No warranty</h2>\n<p>LoomWright is free software, released under the GNU General Public License, version 3 or later. It comes with no warranty, as far as the law allows. You use it at your own risk.</p>\n<p>The authors and contributors accept no responsibility for any loss, damage, injury or death arising from the use of LoomWright or of anything drawn with it.</p>\n<p>The full terms are in the licence, the <code>LICENSE</code> file in the LoomWright source (<code>LICENSE.txt</code> in the portable Windows copy).</p>","text":"Disclaimer\nPlease read this before you build anything from a drawing made with LoomWright.\n\nA planning and drawing tool\nLoomWright is a basic tool for planning wiring and drawing it. It helps you keep track of wires, colours, sizes, fuses and parts, and turns your drawing into lists for the bench. It does not design the wiring for you, and it does not know your vehicle, your cable or your installation.\n\nChecks and hints are guidance only\nThe design checks, the fuse and load figures, the wire size guide figures, the live test and every other hint in LoomWright are guidance only. They are rules of thumb based on the numbers you type in, and they can be wrong or miss things.\nThey do not show that a design or an installation meets BS 7671, IEC 60204, the Building Regulations or any other standard, regulation or code.\nCheck every wire size, fuse rating and part number against the cable, fuse and component makers' data before you build.\n\nNot a replacement for an electrician or engineer\nLoomWright does not replace a qualified electrician or engineer. If you are not sure that a circuit is safe, ask someone who is qualified to check it.\n\nMains electricity and compressed air\nWork on mains electricity, and work on compressed air systems, must be designed, installed, inspected and tested by a competent person. Do not rely on LoomWright for it.\n\nNo warranty\nLoomWright is free software, released under the GNU General Public License, version 3 or later. It comes with no warranty, as far as the law allows. You use it at your own risk.\nThe authors and contributors accept no responsibility for any loss, damage, injury or death arising from the use of LoomWright or of anything drawn with it.\nThe full terms are in the licence, the LICENSE file in the LoomWright source (LICENSE.txt in the portable Windows copy).","sections":[{"anchor":"","title":"Disclaimer","level":1,"start":0},{"anchor":"a-planning-and-drawing-tool","title":"A planning and drawing tool","level":2,"start":92},{"anchor":"checks-and-hints-are-guidance-only","title":"Checks and hints are guidance only","level":2,"start":407},{"anchor":"not-a-replacement-for-an-electrician-or-engineer","title":"Not a replacement for an electrician or engineer","level":2,"start":946},{"anchor":"mains-electricity-and-compressed-air","title":"Mains electricity and compressed air","level":2,"start":1147},{"anchor":"no-warranty","title":"No warranty","level":2,"start":1355}]},
{"id":"compressed-air","file":"24-compressed-air.md","title":"Compressed air planner","html":"<h1 id=\"lm-compressed-air\">Compressed air planner</h1>\n<p>The compressed air planner helps you lay out a workshop or garage air system: the compressor, the receiver (air tank), air treatment, pipe runs, drops and the tools on them. From what you type in, it works out the air demand, the pressure at each tool, the air speed in each pipe, the receiver size, how hard the compressor works, the running cost, the cost of leaks and the water to drain. It then lists anything to look at.</p>\n<p>Open it with <strong>Tools › Compressed air planner…</strong>. The systems you plan are saved in the project file, and every change can be undone.</p>\n<blockquote class=\"lm-note\"><p>The planner is a basic tool. Its figures are planning estimates, made from published rules of thumb and the numbers you type in. They can be wrong. They do not show that a pressure system is safe, or that it meets any regulation, and no responsibility is accepted for their use. If the system is used at work, the Pressure Systems Safety Regulations 2000 apply: see <a href=\"#lm-compressed-air--pressure-systems-at-work\" data-chapter=\"compressed-air\" data-anchor=\"pressure-systems-at-work\">Pressure systems at work</a> and the <a href=\"#lm-disclaimer\" data-chapter=\"disclaimer\" data-anchor=\"\">Disclaimer</a>.</p></blockquote>\n<h2 id=\"lm-compressed-air--before-you-start\" data-anchor=\"before-you-start\">Before you start</h2>\n<p>The first time you open the planner, it shows a short disclaimer. Choose <strong>I understand</strong> to carry on. LoomWright remembers this on your computer, so it does not ask again. If you close the box without choosing <strong>I understand</strong>, the planner closes.</p>\n<p>A line along the top of the planner repeats the main points all the time. <strong>Read the disclaimer</strong> shows the full text again.</p>\n<h2 id=\"lm-compressed-air--the-planner-window\" data-anchor=\"the-planner-window\">The planner window</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>The header</strong> has the system picker, <strong>New system…</strong>, <strong>Rename</strong>, <strong>Delete</strong>, the exports, <strong>Undo</strong>, <strong>Redo</strong>, <strong>Help</strong> (this chapter) and <strong>Close</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>The left side</strong> is where you edit the system, in sections: <strong>System</strong>, <strong>Compressors</strong>, <strong>Receivers</strong>, <strong>Air treatment</strong>, <strong>Pipework</strong>, and <strong>Drops and tools</strong>. A card with a problem has a red edge; a card with a warning has an amber edge.</li>\n<li><strong>The schematic</strong> is drawn for you from the pipework. It is not to scale. Each pipe is labelled with its material, size and length, then its flow, air speed and pressure lost. Each drop shows its pressure, and each tool its air use and the pressure it gets. Red marks a problem and amber a warning. Click any item to edit it. Use <strong>−</strong>, <strong>Fit</strong> and <strong>+</strong>, or hold <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> and scroll, to zoom.</li>\n<li><strong>The results</strong> under the schematic have five tabs: <strong>Results</strong>, <strong>Checks</strong>, <strong>Pipes</strong>, <strong>Pressure at tools</strong>, and <strong>Leak test and calculators</strong>. Click a row or a check to go to the item it is about.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"lm-compressed-air--systems\" data-anchor=\"systems\">Systems</h2>\n<p>A project can hold several air systems, such as a garage and a separate paint booth. Pick one in the header.</p>\n<p><strong>New system…</strong> starts one of three ways:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Start a new system</strong>: one piston compressor, a 100 litre receiver, one main pipe and one drop, all with typical figures you can change.</li>\n<li><strong>Home garage example</strong>: a piston compressor, a 100 litre tank, an aluminium main and two drops with a sander and an impact wrench.</li>\n<li><strong>Workshop example</strong>: a screw compressor, a 500 litre receiver, filters and a refrigerant dryer, a ring main and two branches with four drops.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Rename</strong> and <strong>Delete</strong> act on the system shown. Deleting can be undone.</p>\n<h2 id=\"lm-compressed-air--laying-out-a-system\" data-anchor=\"laying-out-a-system\">Laying out a system</h2>\n<p>Every field starts with a sensible typical figure. Replace them with the figures from your own equipment&#39;s data plates and manuals where you have them.</p>\n<h3 id=\"lm-compressed-air--system\" data-anchor=\"system\">System</h3>\n<div class=\"lm-table\"><table><thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Where it is used</td><td><strong>At work</strong> or <strong>At home (hobby)</strong>. This changes the pressure systems notes.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Air quality needed</td><td><strong>General</strong> (no dryer), <strong>Dry</strong> (dew point +3 °C, a refrigerant dryer) or <strong>Very dry</strong> (−40 °C, a desiccant dryer).</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Electricity £/kWh</td><td>The price you pay, for the running costs.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Hours a year</td><td>How long the system is switched on in a year.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Room °C, Humidity %</td><td>The air the compressor draws in. They set how much water the system makes.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Altitude m</td><td>Height above sea level. Air is thinner higher up.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Leaks %</td><td>An allowance for leaks, as a share of the tools&#39; air use. 10 % is typical of a well-run system.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Growth %</td><td>An allowance for tools you add later.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Tools at once %</td><td>How much of the tools&#39; average use happens at the same time. For one person using one tool at a time, lower it.</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"lm-compressed-air--compressors\" data-anchor=\"compressors\">Compressors</h3>\n<p>Choose the <strong>Type</strong>: <strong>Piston</strong>, <strong>Screw, fixed speed (load/unload)</strong> or <strong>Screw, variable speed (VSD)</strong>. Then give its <strong>Air l/min</strong>, and say whether <strong>That figure is</strong> free air delivered (FAD) or displacement.</p>\n<p>Small piston compressors are often sold by displacement, the volume the piston sweeps. The air they actually deliver is usually only 60 to 70 % of that. When you choose displacement, the planner uses 65 % of your figure (an estimate). A piston compressor rated at more than about 140 l/min per kW probably shows displacement, and the planner says so.</p>\n<p><strong>Motor kW</strong> is used for the running costs. Leave it empty and a typical figure for the type is assumed. <strong>Cut-in bar</strong> and <strong>Cut-out bar</strong> are the pressures where the compressor starts and stops (or loads and unloads). The planner works from the lowest cut-in, the pressure you can always count on.</p>\n<p>With more than one compressor, the planner adds their output. For running costs it assumes the first one takes the load and the next ones only help when needed.</p>\n<h3 id=\"lm-compressed-air--receivers\" data-anchor=\"receivers\">Receivers</h3>\n<p>Give each receiver its <strong>Volume litres</strong> and its <strong>Rated PS bar</strong>, the maximum allowable pressure on its data plate. Tick <strong>Safety valve</strong> and <strong>Condensate drain</strong> if it has them. Every receiver needs both. The card shows PS × V in bar·litres, which matters at work: see <a href=\"#lm-compressed-air--pressure-systems-at-work\" data-chapter=\"compressed-air\" data-anchor=\"pressure-systems-at-work\">Pressure systems at work</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"lm-compressed-air--air-treatment\" data-anchor=\"air-treatment\">Air treatment</h3>\n<p>Add aftercoolers, water separators, filters and dryers with <strong>Add air treatment…</strong>, and put them in the order the air passes through them with the arrows. Give each its <strong>Rated l/min</strong> and its <strong>Loss at rating bar</strong> from the maker&#39;s data. The usual order is: compressor, aftercooler and separator, receiver, pre-filter, dryer, then the finer filters. Tick <strong>Before the receiver</strong> for parts that sit between the compressor and the receiver.</p>\n<p>A desiccant dryer uses some of the dried air to regenerate itself. Its <strong>Purge %</strong> (typically 15 %) is added to the demand.</p>\n<h3 id=\"lm-compressed-air--pipework\" data-anchor=\"pipework\">Pipework</h3>\n<p>Pipes form a tree. The first pipe starts at the receiver (after the air treatment). Each card&#39;s <strong>Branch</strong> button adds a pipe that starts at the end of that pipe, and <strong>Drop</strong> adds a drop there. You can also change where a pipe starts with <strong>Starts from</strong>.</p>\n<p>For each pipe, set:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Use</strong>: main, branch or drop. It sets the air speed limit.</li>\n<li><strong>Ring main</strong>: for a main that loops back to where it started, so air reaches each point two ways.</li>\n<li><strong>Material</strong> and <strong>Size</strong>: aluminium push-fit, steel, copper, air hose, or PVC water pipe. The planner uses each size&#39;s internal diameter. Never use PVC water pipe for compressed air: it is only listed so the planner can warn about it.</li>\n<li><strong>Length m</strong>, and under <strong>Fittings</strong> the elbows, bends, tees, valves and couplings. Each fitting adds an equivalent length of straight pipe.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"lm-compressed-air--drops-and-tools\" data-anchor=\"drops-and-tools\">Drops and tools</h3>\n<p>A drop is a take-off from a pipe to where you plug in. For each drop, set:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>On pipe</strong> and <strong>Taken off the</strong> top, side or underside of the pipe. Take drops from the top, so water stays in the main. Tick <strong>Drain leg</strong> if the pipe carries on down past the take-off to a drain.</li>\n<li><strong>Outlets</strong>: the number of couplings on the drop. Each outlet runs one tool at a time.</li>\n<li><strong>Filter-regulator (FRL)</strong>, with its rated flow, its loss at that flow, the <strong>Regulator bar</strong> it is set to (leave it empty for wide open) and whether it has a <strong>Lubricator</strong>.</li>\n<li>The <strong>Hose</strong> size and length, the <strong>Coupling</strong> size, how many couplings are in the line, and <strong>Safety coupling</strong> if one is used.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Add tools with <strong>Add a tool…</strong>. The list groups common tools with typical air use. After you add one, you can change its <strong>l/min running</strong> (the air it uses while running), its <strong>Use %</strong> (the share of the time it runs), the pressure it <strong>Needs bar</strong>, and whether it <strong>Runs for minutes</strong> (sanders, grinders, blasting, spray guns) or is a <strong>Spray gun</strong>. For a <strong>Pneumatic cylinder</strong>, give its bore, rod, stroke, cycles a minute and pressure, and the planner works out its air use.</p>\n<h2 id=\"lm-compressed-air--how-the-figures-are-worked-out\" data-anchor=\"how-the-figures-are-worked-out\">How the figures are worked out</h2>\n<p>All flows are free air, in litres a minute (l/min), as compressor makers quote them. Pressures are in bar gauge.</p>\n<h3 id=\"lm-compressed-air--air-demand\" data-anchor=\"air-demand\">Air demand</h3>\n<p>Each tool&#39;s average use is its running air use × its <strong>Use %</strong>. The planner adds these up for all tools and multiplies by <strong>Tools at once %</strong>. Then it adds the leak and growth allowances, and any dryer purge. This is the <strong>design demand</strong>, which is compared with the compressor output.</p>\n<p>The <strong>Compressor running</strong> figure is the average demand, with leaks but without growth, as a share of the compressor output. Piston compressors are usually built for 50 to 60 % running time. Above 60 % the planner warns you.</p>\n<h3 id=\"lm-compressed-air--flow-in-each-pipe\" data-anchor=\"flow-in-each-pipe\">Flow in each pipe</h3>\n<p>A drop&#39;s peak flow is its hungriest tools running flat out, one on each outlet. The flow in a pipe is the larger of:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>the design demand of every drop beyond it, and</li>\n<li>the biggest single drop beyond it running flat out.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>This sizes pipes for the worst moment at one drop, not for every tool at once.</p>\n<h3 id=\"lm-compressed-air--pressure-drop-and-air-speed\" data-anchor=\"pressure-drop-and-air-speed\">Pressure drop and air speed</h3>\n<p>The pressure lost in a pipe uses the Atlas Copco formula for steel pipe:</p>\n<p><code>Δp = 450 × q^1.85 × l / (d^5 × p)</code></p>\n<p>where <code>Δp</code> is the loss in bar, <code>q</code> the flow in litres a second, <code>l</code> the length plus fittings in metres, <code>d</code> the internal diameter in mm, and <code>p</code> the absolute pressure at the start of the pipe in bar. The figures 450 and 1.85 are typical; smooth aluminium pipe will lose a little less. The formula is not meant for losses above about 1 bar. A ring main is worked out as two halves, each carrying half the flow over half the length.</p>\n<p>Air speed is the flow, squeezed by the pressure, divided by the pipe&#39;s area. The guide figures used are:</p>\n<div class=\"lm-table\"><table><thead><tr><th>Pipe</th><th>Air speed</th><th>How firm</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Main</td><td>Below 9 m/s; 6 m/s is better, so water and dirt stay in the drain legs.</td><td>Published guidance (BCAS)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Branch</td><td>Below about 15 m/s</td><td>Typical</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Drop</td><td>Below about 15 m/s</td><td>Typical</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Aim to lose no more than 0.1 bar from the receiver to the furthest drop (Atlas Copco), and no more than 10 % of the compressor pressure before the air reaches a tool.</p>\n<h3 id=\"lm-compressed-air--pressure-at-each-tool\" data-anchor=\"pressure-at-each-tool\">Pressure at each tool</h3>\n<p>From the pressure at the drop, the planner takes off:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>The filter-regulator.</strong> Its loss at its rated flow, scaled with the square of the actual flow. Then, if you set the regulator, no more than that setting.</li>\n<li><strong>The quick couplings.</strong> A 1/4&quot; coupling is taken to lose about 0.25 bar at 600 l/min, an estimate. A 3/8&quot; coupling loses about one-sixth as much. A 1/2&quot; coupling is scaled from the 3/8&quot; one (an estimate). The loss goes with the square of the flow, and a safety coupling passes about 20 % less air.</li>\n<li><strong>The hose</strong>, with the same formula as the pipes and a typical bore for each hose size.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The filter-regulator, couplings and hose together should lose no more than about 0.6 bar. Most air tools are rated at 6.3 bar at the tool. Each bar short costs about 25 to 30 % of a tool&#39;s output.</p>\n<p>When a tool uses more air than the compressor delivers, the <strong>Pressure at tools</strong> tab shows how many seconds the receiver and pipework alone can keep it going.</p>\n<h3 id=\"lm-compressed-air--receiver-size\" data-anchor=\"receiver-size\">Receiver size</h3>\n<p>For a fixed speed or piston compressor, the planner works out the receiver needed so the compressor loads no more than once every 30 seconds (Atlas Copco):</p>\n<p><code>V = 0.25 × q × p1 × T0 / (fmax × Δp × T1)</code></p>\n<p>where <code>q</code> is the compressor output in litres a second, <code>p1</code> the air pressure at the intake, <code>T1</code> the intake temperature, <code>T0</code> the receiver temperature (taken as 20 °C warmer), <code>fmax</code> one cycle in 30 seconds and <code>Δp</code> the gap between cut-in and cut-out. The air in the pipework counts towards the volume you have. A variable speed compressor needs much less; use its maker&#39;s figure.</p>\n<h3 id=\"lm-compressed-air--running-cost-and-pressure-setting\" data-anchor=\"running-cost-and-pressure-setting\">Running cost and pressure setting</h3>\n<p>Each compressor&#39;s yearly electricity is its motor power × how loaded it is × <strong>Hours a year</strong>. A fixed speed screw compressor running unloaded still draws about a quarter of its full power (the published range is 15 to 30 %).</p>\n<p>Every 1 bar of extra pressure costs about 7 % more energy (BCAS). The planner works out the highest pressure a tool needs at the compressor, adds 0.5 bar, and shows any setting above that and its cost. Lower the pressure in small steps, and check your tools still work well.</p>\n<h3 id=\"lm-compressed-air--leaks\" data-anchor=\"leaks\">Leaks</h3>\n<p>Leak cost is the leak allowance × the compressor&#39;s power per litre a second of air × the hours × your electricity price. Well-run systems lose under 10 % of their air to leaks. Poorly kept systems often lose 20 to 30 %.</p>\n<h3 id=\"lm-compressed-air--water\" data-anchor=\"water\">Water</h3>\n<p>The planner works out the water the incoming air holds at the room temperature and humidity, and how much of it stays in the air once it is compressed and cooled. Without a dryer, the air is taken to cool back to room temperature. With a dryer, it is cooled to the dryer&#39;s dew point. The difference ends up as liquid water, shown per 8-hour day. Fit drains wherever it collects.</p>\n<h2 id=\"lm-compressed-air--tool-figures\" data-anchor=\"tool-figures\">Tool figures</h2>\n<p>The tool list gives typical air use while each tool runs. They come from published tables and makers&#39; data, and every entry says how firm it is:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>typical</strong>: a usual figure from published tables;</li>\n<li><strong>estimate</strong>: where the sources only give an average at 25 % use, the running figure is four times that average;</li>\n<li><strong>maker&#39;s figure</strong>: a figure for one named product.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Sanders vary widely between sources and makers. For anything that matters, use the figure from your own tool&#39;s manual. Machines, blast cabinets and other tools have no figure until you enter one.</p>\n<h2 id=\"lm-compressed-air--checks\" data-anchor=\"checks\">Checks</h2>\n<p>The <strong>Checks</strong> tab lists problems first, then warnings, then notes. Each one names the item and its rule. Click it to go to the item. A short list does not mean the system is safe.</p>\n<div class=\"lm-table\"><table><thead><tr><th>Rule</th><th>What it looks for</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>D1-PVC</td><td>PVC water pipe used for compressed air (a problem).</td></tr>\n<tr><td>D1-RATING</td><td>Aluminium push-fit pipe on a compressor that cuts out above its typical 12.5 bar rating.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>D1-VEL</td><td>Air speed above the limit for the pipe&#39;s use.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>D1-DP-NETWORK</td><td>More than 0.1 bar lost from the receiver to a drop, or a pipe far too small for its flow.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>D1-TAKEOFF</td><td>A drop taken off the underside of a pipe with no drain leg.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>D1-TOOL-P</td><td>A tool getting less pressure than it needs.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>D1-DP-TOTAL</td><td>More than 10 % of the compressor pressure lost before a tool.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>D1-DROP</td><td>More than 0.6 bar lost in a drop&#39;s filter-regulator, couplings and hose.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>D1-HOSE</td><td>A long or narrow hose, or 1/4&quot; couplings on a tool using over about 400 l/min.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>D2-UNDERSIZE</td><td>No compressor yet.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>D2-BAND</td><td>A cut-out pressure that is not above the cut-in pressure.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>D2-FAD</td><td>A displacement figure, or a figure that looks like one.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>D2-POWER</td><td>No motor power given, so a typical figure is assumed.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>D2-DUTY</td><td>A piston compressor running more than 60 % of the time.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>D2-PRESSURE</td><td>A pressure setting more than 0.25 bar above what the tools need.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>D2-INLET</td><td>A warm room. Every 5 °C cooler intake air saves about 1 % of the energy.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>D3-RECEIVER</td><td>A receiver too small for the compressor, or no receiver.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>D3-SAFETY</td><td>A receiver without a safety valve or condensate drain (a problem).</td></tr>\n<tr><td>D3-RATING</td><td>A compressor that cuts out above a receiver&#39;s rated pressure (a problem), or no rated pressure entered.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>D3-PSSR</td><td>Notes on the Pressure Systems Safety Regulations 2000, or on home use.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>D4-DRYER</td><td>Dry air needed, for example for spray painting, without a suitable dryer.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>D4-DRYER-SIZE</td><td>A dryer that cannot handle the compressor output. A refrigerant dryer&#39;s rating is corrected for your pressure and temperatures.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>D4-SIZE</td><td>A filter or dryer with more flow through it than its rating.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>D4-ORDER</td><td>Air treatment out of the usual order.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>D4-DRAINS</td><td>How much water the system makes, and where to drain it.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>D4-PAINT-LUBE</td><td>A lubricator on a drop with a spray gun (a problem).</td></tr>\n<tr><td>D5-LEAK-ALLOW</td><td>A leak allowance above 10 %, with its yearly cost.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>D6-DEMAND</td><td>Design demand above the compressor output (a problem).</td></tr>\n<tr><td>D6-CONTINUOUS-TOOL</td><td>A tool that runs for minutes and uses more than the compressor delivers.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>D6-FRL-CAPACITY</td><td>A filter-regulator rated below the drop&#39;s peak flow.</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"lm-compressed-air--leak-test-and-calculators\" data-anchor=\"leak-test-and-calculators\">Leak test and calculators</h2>\n<p>The <strong>Leak test and calculators</strong> tab has four small tools. They use your system&#39;s figures and settings.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Load/unload test.</strong> With every tool off, time how long the compressor runs on load and off load over a few cycles. Leakage is the time on load as a share of the whole cycle. <strong>Use this figure</strong> puts it into <strong>Leaks %</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Pressure decay test.</strong> With every tool off, pump the system up, switch the compressor off, and time the fall in pressure. The planner works out the leak flow from the volume of the receivers and pipework.</li>\n<li><strong>One leak.</strong> What a round hole of a given size leaks at a given pressure, and what it costs a year. A sharp-edged hole leaks about 40 % less.</li>\n<li><strong>Receiver for a short peak.</strong> The local receiver that would cover a short burst of air on its own, such as a blow-off or a machine cycle. It ignores what the compressor adds during the burst, so it errs on the large side.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"lm-compressed-air--exporting\" data-anchor=\"exporting\">Exporting</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Export CSV</strong> saves the summary, the pipework, the pressure at each tool and the checks, for a spreadsheet.</li>\n<li><strong>Export SVG</strong> and <strong>Export PDF</strong> save the schematic with the results, the checks and the disclaimer under it.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Every export carries the disclaimer.</p>\n<h2 id=\"lm-compressed-air--keys\" data-anchor=\"keys\">Keys</h2>\n<div class=\"lm-table\"><table><thead><tr><th>Key</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td><kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Z</kbd></td><td>Undo.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Y</kbd> or <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>Z</kbd></td><td>Redo.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>S</kbd></td><td>Save the project.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><kbd>0</kbd></td><td>Fit the schematic to the window.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+scroll</td><td>Zoom the schematic.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><kbd>F1</kbd></td><td>Open this chapter.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><kbd>Esc</kbd></td><td>Leave a field, or close the planner.</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>On a Mac, use <kbd>Cmd</kbd> in place of <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"lm-compressed-air--pressure-systems-at-work\" data-anchor=\"pressure-systems-at-work\">Pressure systems at work</h2>\n<p>If the system is used at work, the Pressure Systems Safety Regulations 2000 apply to it. In short:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The user or owner must set safe operating limits and fit protective devices, such as safety valves, set correctly and kept working.</li>\n<li>For most receivers, a competent person must draw up a written scheme of examination before the system is first used, and the system must then be examined to it.</li>\n<li>The scheme and examinations are not needed while every vessel in the system is under 250 bar·litres (its rated pressure in bar × its volume in litres). A 25 litre receiver rated at 10 bar is already 250 bar·litres.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The planner works out PS × V for each receiver. When <strong>Where it is used</strong> is <strong>At work</strong>, it notes any receiver at or above 250 bar·litres. This is a note only. Whether the regulations apply, and what they need, is for you and a competent person to decide.</p>\n<p>At home, follow the maker&#39;s instructions, keep the safety valve working, drain the receiver, and never go over its rated pressure.</p>\n<p>Whatever the setting, use only pipe, fittings, hoses and vessels rated for the pressure and temperature, and never PVC water pipe. The HSE leaflet INDG261, <em>Pressure systems: a brief guide to safety</em>, is a free and short introduction. Have a competent person design, install and examine the system.</p>\n<h2 id=\"lm-compressed-air--limits-of-the-planner\" data-anchor=\"limits-of-the-planner\">Limits of the planner</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>The formulas are rules of thumb for steady flow. They do not model surges, pressure waves, or a compressor catching up after a burst.</li>\n<li>Fittings, couplings, filters and dryers use typical or estimated losses. Real parts differ, and filters lose more as they clog.</li>\n<li>Tool air use varies a lot between makers and with wear. The figures in the list are typical, not your tool&#39;s.</li>\n<li>Running costs assume steady use over the year at the hours you give.</li>\n<li>The planner does not check wall thicknesses, supports, expansion, temperature ratings or the strength of any part.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>See also the <a href=\"#lm-disclaimer\" data-chapter=\"disclaimer\" data-anchor=\"\">Disclaimer</a>.</p>","text":"Compressed air planner\nThe compressed air planner helps you lay out a workshop or garage air system: the compressor, the receiver (air tank), air treatment, pipe runs, drops and the tools on them. From what you type in, it works out the air demand, the pressure at each tool, the air speed in each pipe, the receiver size, how hard the compressor works, the running cost, the cost of leaks and the water to drain. It then lists anything to look at.\nOpen it with Tools › Compressed air planner…. The systems you plan are saved in the project file, and every change can be undone.\nThe planner is a basic tool. Its figures are planning estimates, made from published rules of thumb and the numbers you type in. They can be wrong. They do not show that a pressure system is safe, or that it meets any regulation, and no responsibility is accepted for their use. If the system is used at work, the Pressure Systems Safety Regulations 2000 apply: see Pressure systems at work and the Disclaimer.\n\nBefore you start\nThe first time you open the planner, it shows a short disclaimer. Choose I understand to carry on. LoomWright remembers this on your computer, so it does not ask again. If you close the box without choosing I understand, the planner closes.\nA line along the top of the planner repeats the main points all the time. Read the disclaimer shows the full text again.\n\nThe planner window\nThe header has the system picker, New system…, Rename, Delete, the exports, Undo, Redo, Help (this chapter) and Close.\nThe left side is where you edit the system, in sections: System, Compressors, Receivers, Air treatment, Pipework, and Drops and tools. A card with a problem has a red edge; a card with a warning has an amber edge.\nThe schematic is drawn for you from the pipework. It is not to scale. Each pipe is labelled with its material, size and length, then its flow, air speed and pressure lost. Each drop shows its pressure, and each tool its air use and the pressure it gets. Red marks a problem and amber a warning. Click any item to edit it. Use −, Fit and +, or hold Ctrl and scroll, to zoom.\nThe results under the schematic have five tabs: Results, Checks, Pipes, Pressure at tools, and Leak test and calculators. Click a row or a check to go to the item it is about.\n\nSystems\nA project can hold several air systems, such as a garage and a separate paint booth. Pick one in the header.\nNew system… starts one of three ways:\nStart a new system: one piston compressor, a 100 litre receiver, one main pipe and one drop, all with typical figures you can change.\nHome garage example: a piston compressor, a 100 litre tank, an aluminium main and two drops with a sander and an impact wrench.\nWorkshop example: a screw compressor, a 500 litre receiver, filters and a refrigerant dryer, a ring main and two branches with four drops.\nRename and Delete act on the system shown. Deleting can be undone.\n\nLaying out a system\nEvery field starts with a sensible typical figure. Replace them with the figures from your own equipment's data plates and manuals where you have them.\n\nSystem\nField What it does\nWhere it is used At work or At home (hobby). This changes the pressure systems notes.\nAir quality needed General (no dryer), Dry (dew point +3 °C, a refrigerant dryer) or Very dry (−40 °C, a desiccant dryer).\nElectricity £/kWh The price you pay, for the running costs.\nHours a year How long the system is switched on in a year.\nRoom °C, Humidity % The air the compressor draws in. They set how much water the system makes.\nAltitude m Height above sea level. Air is thinner higher up.\nLeaks % An allowance for leaks, as a share of the tools' air use. 10 % is typical of a well-run system.\nGrowth % An allowance for tools you add later.\nTools at once % How much of the tools' average use happens at the same time. For one person using one tool at a time, lower it.\n\nCompressors\nChoose the Type: Piston, Screw, fixed speed (load/unload) or Screw, variable speed (VSD). Then give its Air l/min, and say whether That figure is free air delivered (FAD) or displacement.\nSmall piston compressors are often sold by displacement, the volume the piston sweeps. The air they actually deliver is usually only 60 to 70 % of that. When you choose displacement, the planner uses 65 % of your figure (an estimate). A piston compressor rated at more than about 140 l/min per kW probably shows displacement, and the planner says so.\nMotor kW is used for the running costs. Leave it empty and a typical figure for the type is assumed. Cut-in bar and Cut-out bar are the pressures where the compressor starts and stops (or loads and unloads). The planner works from the lowest cut-in, the pressure you can always count on.\nWith more than one compressor, the planner adds their output. For running costs it assumes the first one takes the load and the next ones only help when needed.\n\nReceivers\nGive each receiver its Volume litres and its Rated PS bar, the maximum allowable pressure on its data plate. Tick Safety valve and Condensate drain if it has them. Every receiver needs both. The card shows PS × V in bar·litres, which matters at work: see Pressure systems at work.\n\nAir treatment\nAdd aftercoolers, water separators, filters and dryers with Add air treatment…, and put them in the order the air passes through them with the arrows. Give each its Rated l/min and its Loss at rating bar from the maker's data. The usual order is: compressor, aftercooler and separator, receiver, pre-filter, dryer, then the finer filters. Tick Before the receiver for parts that sit between the compressor and the receiver.\nA desiccant dryer uses some of the dried air to regenerate itself. Its Purge % (typically 15 %) is added to the demand.\n\nPipework\nPipes form a tree. The first pipe starts at the receiver (after the air treatment). Each card's Branch button adds a pipe that starts at the end of that pipe, and Drop adds a drop there. You can also change where a pipe starts with Starts from.\nFor each pipe, set:\nUse: main, branch or drop. It sets the air speed limit.\nRing main: for a main that loops back to where it started, so air reaches each point two ways.\nMaterial and Size: aluminium push-fit, steel, copper, air hose, or PVC water pipe. The planner uses each size's internal diameter. Never use PVC water pipe for compressed air: it is only listed so the planner can warn about it.\nLength m, and under Fittings the elbows, bends, tees, valves and couplings. Each fitting adds an equivalent length of straight pipe.\n\nDrops and tools\nA drop is a take-off from a pipe to where you plug in. For each drop, set:\nOn pipe and Taken off the top, side or underside of the pipe. Take drops from the top, so water stays in the main. Tick Drain leg if the pipe carries on down past the take-off to a drain.\nOutlets: the number of couplings on the drop. Each outlet runs one tool at a time.\nFilter-regulator (FRL), with its rated flow, its loss at that flow, the Regulator bar it is set to (leave it empty for wide open) and whether it has a Lubricator.\nThe Hose size and length, the Coupling size, how many couplings are in the line, and Safety coupling if one is used.\nAdd tools with Add a tool…. The list groups common tools with typical air use. After you add one, you can change its l/min running (the air it uses while running), its Use % (the share of the time it runs), the pressure it Needs bar, and whether it Runs for minutes (sanders, grinders, blasting, spray guns) or is a Spray gun. For a Pneumatic cylinder, give its bore, rod, stroke, cycles a minute and pressure, and the planner works out its air use.\n\nHow the figures are worked out\nAll flows are free air, in litres a minute (l/min), as compressor makers quote them. Pressures are in bar gauge.\n\nAir demand\nEach tool's average use is its running air use × its Use %. The planner adds these up for all tools and multiplies by Tools at once %. Then it adds the leak and growth allowances, and any dryer purge. This is the design demand, which is compared with the compressor output.\nThe Compressor running figure is the average demand, with leaks but without growth, as a share of the compressor output. Piston compressors are usually built for 50 to 60 % running time. Above 60 % the planner warns you.\n\nFlow in each pipe\nA drop's peak flow is its hungriest tools running flat out, one on each outlet. The flow in a pipe is the larger of:\nthe design demand of every drop beyond it, and\nthe biggest single drop beyond it running flat out.\nThis sizes pipes for the worst moment at one drop, not for every tool at once.\n\nPressure drop and air speed\nThe pressure lost in a pipe uses the Atlas Copco formula for steel pipe:\nΔp = 450 × q^1.85 × l / (d^5 × p)\nwhere Δp is the loss in bar, q the flow in litres a second, l the length plus fittings in metres, d the internal diameter in mm, and p the absolute pressure at the start of the pipe in bar. The figures 450 and 1.85 are typical; smooth aluminium pipe will lose a little less. The formula is not meant for losses above about 1 bar. A ring main is worked out as two halves, each carrying half the flow over half the length.\nAir speed is the flow, squeezed by the pressure, divided by the pipe's area. The guide figures used are:\nPipe Air speed How firm\nMain Below 9 m/s; 6 m/s is better, so water and dirt stay in the drain legs. Published guidance (BCAS)\nBranch Below about 15 m/s Typical\nDrop Below about 15 m/s Typical\nAim to lose no more than 0.1 bar from the receiver to the furthest drop (Atlas Copco), and no more than 10 % of the compressor pressure before the air reaches a tool.\n\nPressure at each tool\nFrom the pressure at the drop, the planner takes off:\nThe filter-regulator. Its loss at its rated flow, scaled with the square of the actual flow. Then, if you set the regulator, no more than that setting.\nThe quick couplings. A 1/4\" coupling is taken to lose about 0.25 bar at 600 l/min, an estimate. A 3/8\" coupling loses about one-sixth as much. A 1/2\" coupling is scaled from the 3/8\" one (an estimate). The loss goes with the square of the flow, and a safety coupling passes about 20 % less air.\nThe hose, with the same formula as the pipes and a typical bore for each hose size.\nThe filter-regulator, couplings and hose together should lose no more than about 0.6 bar. Most air tools are rated at 6.3 bar at the tool. Each bar short costs about 25 to 30 % of a tool's output.\nWhen a tool uses more air than the compressor delivers, the Pressure at tools tab shows how many seconds the receiver and pipework alone can keep it going.\n\nReceiver size\nFor a fixed speed or piston compressor, the planner works out the receiver needed so the compressor loads no more than once every 30 seconds (Atlas Copco):\nV = 0.25 × q × p1 × T0 / (fmax × Δp × T1)\nwhere q is the compressor output in litres a second, p1 the air pressure at the intake, T1 the intake temperature, T0 the receiver temperature (taken as 20 °C warmer), fmax one cycle in 30 seconds and Δp the gap between cut-in and cut-out. The air in the pipework counts towards the volume you have. A variable speed compressor needs much less; use its maker's figure.\n\nRunning cost and pressure setting\nEach compressor's yearly electricity is its motor power × how loaded it is × Hours a year. A fixed speed screw compressor running unloaded still draws about a quarter of its full power (the published range is 15 to 30 %).\nEvery 1 bar of extra pressure costs about 7 % more energy (BCAS). The planner works out the highest pressure a tool needs at the compressor, adds 0.5 bar, and shows any setting above that and its cost. Lower the pressure in small steps, and check your tools still work well.\n\nLeaks\nLeak cost is the leak allowance × the compressor's power per litre a second of air × the hours × your electricity price. Well-run systems lose under 10 % of their air to leaks. Poorly kept systems often lose 20 to 30 %.\n\nWater\nThe planner works out the water the incoming air holds at the room temperature and humidity, and how much of it stays in the air once it is compressed and cooled. Without a dryer, the air is taken to cool back to room temperature. With a dryer, it is cooled to the dryer's dew point. The difference ends up as liquid water, shown per 8-hour day. Fit drains wherever it collects.\n\nTool figures\nThe tool list gives typical air use while each tool runs. They come from published tables and makers' data, and every entry says how firm it is:\ntypical: a usual figure from published tables;\nestimate: where the sources only give an average at 25 % use, the running figure is four times that average;\nmaker's figure: a figure for one named product.\nSanders vary widely between sources and makers. For anything that matters, use the figure from your own tool's manual. Machines, blast cabinets and other tools have no figure until you enter one.\n\nChecks\nThe Checks tab lists problems first, then warnings, then notes. Each one names the item and its rule. Click it to go to the item. A short list does not mean the system is safe.\nRule What it looks for\nD1-PVC PVC water pipe used for compressed air (a problem).\nD1-RATING Aluminium push-fit pipe on a compressor that cuts out above its typical 12.5 bar rating.\nD1-VEL Air speed above the limit for the pipe's use.\nD1-DP-NETWORK More than 0.1 bar lost from the receiver to a drop, or a pipe far too small for its flow.\nD1-TAKEOFF A drop taken off the underside of a pipe with no drain leg.\nD1-TOOL-P A tool getting less pressure than it needs.\nD1-DP-TOTAL More than 10 % of the compressor pressure lost before a tool.\nD1-DROP More than 0.6 bar lost in a drop's filter-regulator, couplings and hose.\nD1-HOSE A long or narrow hose, or 1/4\" couplings on a tool using over about 400 l/min.\nD2-UNDERSIZE No compressor yet.\nD2-BAND A cut-out pressure that is not above the cut-in pressure.\nD2-FAD A displacement figure, or a figure that looks like one.\nD2-POWER No motor power given, so a typical figure is assumed.\nD2-DUTY A piston compressor running more than 60 % of the time.\nD2-PRESSURE A pressure setting more than 0.25 bar above what the tools need.\nD2-INLET A warm room. Every 5 °C cooler intake air saves about 1 % of the energy.\nD3-RECEIVER A receiver too small for the compressor, or no receiver.\nD3-SAFETY A receiver without a safety valve or condensate drain (a problem).\nD3-RATING A compressor that cuts out above a receiver's rated pressure (a problem), or no rated pressure entered.\nD3-PSSR Notes on the Pressure Systems Safety Regulations 2000, or on home use.\nD4-DRYER Dry air needed, for example for spray painting, without a suitable dryer.\nD4-DRYER-SIZE A dryer that cannot handle the compressor output. A refrigerant dryer's rating is corrected for your pressure and temperatures.\nD4-SIZE A filter or dryer with more flow through it than its rating.\nD4-ORDER Air treatment out of the usual order.\nD4-DRAINS How much water the system makes, and where to drain it.\nD4-PAINT-LUBE A lubricator on a drop with a spray gun (a problem).\nD5-LEAK-ALLOW A leak allowance above 10 %, with its yearly cost.\nD6-DEMAND Design demand above the compressor output (a problem).\nD6-CONTINUOUS-TOOL A tool that runs for minutes and uses more than the compressor delivers.\nD6-FRL-CAPACITY A filter-regulator rated below the drop's peak flow.\n\nLeak test and calculators\nThe Leak test and calculators tab has four small tools. They use your system's figures and settings.\nLoad/unload test. With every tool off, time how long the compressor runs on load and off load over a few cycles. Leakage is the time on load as a share of the whole cycle. Use this figure puts it into Leaks %.\nPressure decay test. With every tool off, pump the system up, switch the compressor off, and time the fall in pressure. The planner works out the leak flow from the volume of the receivers and pipework.\nOne leak. What a round hole of a given size leaks at a given pressure, and what it costs a year. A sharp-edged hole leaks about 40 % less.\nReceiver for a short peak. The local receiver that would cover a short burst of air on its own, such as a blow-off or a machine cycle. It ignores what the compressor adds during the burst, so it errs on the large side.\n\nExporting\nExport CSV saves the summary, the pipework, the pressure at each tool and the checks, for a spreadsheet.\nExport SVG and Export PDF save the schematic with the results, the checks and the disclaimer under it.\nEvery export carries the disclaimer.\n\nKeys\nKey What it does\nCtrl+Z Undo.\nCtrl+Y or Ctrl+Shift+Z Redo.\nCtrl+S Save the project.\n0 Fit the schematic to the window.\nCtrl+scroll Zoom the schematic.\nF1 Open this chapter.\nEsc Leave a field, or close the planner.\nOn a Mac, use Cmd in place of Ctrl.\n\nPressure systems at work\nIf the system is used at work, the Pressure Systems Safety Regulations 2000 apply to it. In short:\nThe user or owner must set safe operating limits and fit protective devices, such as safety valves, set correctly and kept working.\nFor most receivers, a competent person must draw up a written scheme of examination before the system is first used, and the system must then be examined to it.\nThe scheme and examinations are not needed while every vessel in the system is under 250 bar·litres (its rated pressure in bar × its volume in litres). A 25 litre receiver rated at 10 bar is already 250 bar·litres.\nThe planner works out PS × V for each receiver. When Where it is used is At work, it notes any receiver at or above 250 bar·litres. This is a note only. Whether the regulations apply, and what they need, is for you and a competent person to decide.\nAt home, follow the maker's instructions, keep the safety valve working, drain the receiver, and never go over its rated pressure.\nWhatever the setting, use only pipe, fittings, hoses and vessels rated for the pressure and temperature, and never PVC water pipe. The HSE leaflet INDG261, Pressure systems: a brief guide to safety, is a free and short introduction. Have a competent person design, install and examine the system.\n\nLimits of the planner\nThe formulas are rules of thumb for steady flow. They do not model surges, pressure waves, or a compressor catching up after a burst.\nFittings, couplings, filters and dryers use typical or estimated losses. Real parts differ, and filters lose more as they clog.\nTool air use varies a lot between makers and with wear. The figures in the list are typical, not your tool's.\nRunning costs assume steady use over the year at the hours you give.\nThe planner does not check wall thicknesses, supports, expansion, temperature ratings or the strength of any part.\nSee also the Disclaimer.","sections":[{"anchor":"","title":"Compressed air planner","level":1,"start":0},{"anchor":"before-you-start","title":"Before you start","level":2,"start":991},{"anchor":"the-planner-window","title":"The planner window","level":2,"start":1371},{"anchor":"systems","title":"Systems","level":2,"start":2274},{"anchor":"laying-out-a-system","title":"Laying out a system","level":2,"start":2898},{"anchor":"system","title":"System","level":3,"start":3071},{"anchor":"compressors","title":"Compressors","level":3,"start":3861},{"anchor":"receivers","title":"Receivers","level":3,"start":4862},{"anchor":"air-treatment","title":"Air treatment","level":3,"start":5154},{"anchor":"pipework","title":"Pipework","level":3,"start":5713},{"anchor":"drops-and-tools","title":"Drops and tools","level":3,"start":6500},{"anchor":"how-the-figures-are-worked-out","title":"How the figures are worked out","level":2,"start":7593},{"anchor":"air-demand","title":"Air demand","level":3,"start":7738},{"anchor":"flow-in-each-pipe","title":"Flow in each pipe","level":3,"start":8245},{"anchor":"pressure-drop-and-air-speed","title":"Pressure drop and air speed","level":3,"start":8559},{"anchor":"pressure-at-each-tool","title":"Pressure at each tool","level":3,"start":9581},{"anchor":"receiver-size","title":"Receiver size","level":3,"start":10542},{"anchor":"running-cost-and-pressure-setting","title":"Running cost and pressure setting","level":3,"start":11124},{"anchor":"leaks","title":"Leaks","level":3,"start":11656},{"anchor":"water","title":"Water","level":3,"start":11883},{"anchor":"tool-figures","title":"Tool figures","level":2,"start":12269},{"anchor":"checks","title":"Checks","level":2,"start":12828},{"anchor":"leak-test-and-calculators","title":"Leak test and calculators","level":2,"start":15269},{"anchor":"exporting","title":"Exporting","level":2,"start":16168},{"anchor":"keys","title":"Keys","level":2,"start":16424},{"anchor":"pressure-systems-at-work","title":"Pressure systems at work","level":2,"start":16680},{"anchor":"limits-of-the-planner","title":"Limits of the planner","level":2,"start":17990}]}
] };
